        <p>houseltoli.i, are made</p>
        <p>piquant and bisty the addition of</p>
        <p>ALVirAYS N C1)0 U GALL'S PATENT SELF-RAISING FLOUR 1T 47111 tiBST USB</p>
        <p>A WAR OF THE HOUSEHOLD.</p>
        <p>By the Author of " The Double Life," "The Bailiff's Scheme," &amp;c., &amp;c.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XIII.</p>
        <p>AN INOPrOILTUNE Ar.RIVAL.</p>
        <p>The young squire comprehending, in his single sweeping glance around the little lodging-house parlour, the painful pc‘sition and horrible desola- tion of poor Dora, received her, as she sprang toward him, with outstretched hands and a face full of yearning tenderness, mingled with the gravest concern, "My poor little Dora 1" he exclaimed, pressing her bands warmly. "How you tremble!" Oh, the unutterable delight which that firm, re- assuring clasp (rave to the frightened girl ! Oh, the relief and gladness thot surged over her heart as she looked up into his grave, honest and tender face!</p>
        <p>"It is not blightedrhe answered. "My love, like the waters of the Nile, which, overflowing, make the neighbouring lauds fertile, will make my whole life richer and more fruitful. But I do not wish to speak of myself, Dora, except in connection with your need of me. Since you believe Mr. Warner will be here this week, I think you had better not endeavour to obtain any pupils. Mr. Warner will probably wish to be married at once, and in that case you will be obliged to break up any engagements on the very eve of makiNt them."</p>
        <p>"You are right, Noel. I will not see about ob- taining pupils until I have heard from him." "And until he comes," continued our hero, "1 shall constitute myself, in some measure, your guardian. I shall take lodgings at some hotel in thisneighbout hood, and be on hand should Mr. Narr arrive before Mr. Warner. If these persons are your parents, they ate not suitable guardians and companions for you. You will give me leave to stay, Dora ?" "Oh, yes, Noel. You have been my friend since my earliest remembrance. I look upon you as a dear brother, and love you as such.' "Then let me be your brother, Dora. I will watch over you as if you were my young sister, until another comes to claim you." They continued to converse, Mrs. Narr's slumbers deepening. Noel hid his own griefs deeply within his breast, and showed himself noble, tender and unselfish. Dora, putting her own sorrows into the background, strove to cheer and console him, and .the hour's interview did more to knit their souls together than previous years of social gayety had done.</p>
        <p>"Oh, Noel, Noel !" she cried, clinglng to him. " I 'm so glad you 'ye come !" "Didn't you know I would come, Dora ?" asked the young squire, in tender reproach. "Did you think I would not be here when you needed me?"</p>
        <p>Dora did not answer, but still clung to him, trembling. Mrs. Narr, who had been regarding the scene stupidly, now aroused herself, saying, with drunken solemnity: " Wh' are you, anyhow? What want here! You mustn't come here in'fering 'tween me and my clatter. G'way !" She flourished the black bottle, to give emphasis to her command. The young squire did not permit his disgust of the intoxicated creature to become visible, either in looks or words.</p>
        <p>At last as the 'evening fell, and the landlady brought up the lighted candles, the young squire took his leave, promising to call again in the morning. Mrs. Narr did not arouse herself, and Dora drank her tea alone, and at last retired to her bed, leaving the intoxicated womati still sleeping in her chair. In the morning, however, when the young girl came out into the sitting-room fully dressed, Mrs. arr was not there.</p>
        <p>"I am Miss Dora's friend," he said, "and have called to see her—"</p>
        <p>"She don't want see nobody ! " interposed Mrs. Narr, with ludicrous attempt at dignity. "She's perf'ly satisfied, and so 'm I !" Mrs. Narr put the black bottle to her lips again, swallowing a portion of its contents noisily and with evident gusto. The young squire released Dora's hands, and gently put her from him. He then went up to the poor creature, who was rocking her body to and fro in her chair, and beuting her head, in its big bent bonnet, against the wall. "You have had enough drink for to-day," he said, in a tone of quiet command. "Give me the bottle, Mrs. Narr. The woman, under the influence of the liquor to which she had of late been unaccustomed, was in a quarrelsome mood, and she flamed up now, ready and eager to battle for what she conceived to be her rights. But her haggard eyes fell before the steady, commanding gaze of the young squire. His blue eyes clear and keen, had a look of power in them which it did not occur to her that she could suc- cessfully resist. "Give it to me," he repeated gently, not re- moving his gaze from her, and holding out his hand.</p>
        <p>"She has awakened and gone to bed," thought Dora. "What a mother tate has given me! Oh, Heaven! can she be my mother ?" She went to the window, and, leaning against the sash, looked out upon the dreary little quad- rangle of grass called "the park," and upon the straight row of dingy, tall brick houses hey4:nd. -</p>
        <p>The hour was yet early, and the novel sights peculiar to a town morning abounded. Milkmen, grocers' wagons and hucksters' donkey-carts were well represented, with various other industries, and Dora was absorbed in watching them, when the door of the sitting-room opened, and Mrs. Narr entered, bearing a tray. It was plain, at the first glance that the woman was quite sober, and fully ashamt:d of herself. She set down the tray upon a chair, spread a clean white cloth on the table, and proceeded to lay the breakfast, sending frequent deprecating glances at the young girl. "Good morning, Dora," she said at last, coming nearer to the window. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Good morning, Mrs. Istsrr," rejoined Dora coldly, turning around. "I suppose I didn't act very well last night, Dora," said the woman, deprecatingly. "The truth is, I aint used to liquor, and I took a drop list night and it sent me wild. I don't see how I could have been so foolish. Was that gentleman who was here Mr. Warner?"</p>
        <p>The woman began to snivel and protest, but there was something in• the young squire's air of gentle authority which compelled her submission. She handed him the filsk. He put it in his pocket quietly, and went back to the young girl. "How pale you are, Dora I" he said, in a tender compassion. "You have had a hard trial. Come and sit down by me, while you tell me all about it."</p>
        <p>"No. He was Squire Weir, who lives near the Grange. I have known him from my childhood." " Ah, I thought he was a lord at the very least ! What a stern way he had. I—l didn't say any. ; did I, Dora ?" "1 scarcely comprehend you," responded Dora. "If you mean to ask if you told anything you wished to keep secret, I presume you did. You said your husband was alive in Cheshire, and would be here this week." The woman coloured a little, looke'l embarrassed, and then exclaimed, defiantly: "Well, you must have known it, sooner or later. Jack is alive, and will probably be here to-morrow. I've been out already this morning, and have telegraphed to him. Of course you won't betray him, she added, anxiously, "being hea your own father."</p>
        <p>He led her to the sofa and held her cold hand in a warm and gentle clasp. Dora shuddered, and the hot, quick tears sprang to her eyes. "Oh, Noel !" she exclaimed, looking up at phim piteously, "it has been a hard trial—almost more than I can bear 1 But tell me, first, how you came to be here so opportunely 1" "Oh, that's simple enough," replied the young squire, cheerfully. "I had not been to the Grange since the morning when I overtook you as you were going home from the Hare and Hounds Inn. At first it seemed to me that I =lst leave home for a while, and I was actually preparing to leave on an extended tour, when I heard, the next morning, that the good old squire was dead. Then I knew that my duty lay nearer home, and I resolved to stay. I could not intrude upon you in your first grief, Dora, and, although I inquired after you daily, it was not until to-day that I ven- tured to call upon you in person. I atrived at the Grange an hour after you had left it." "Were you greatly surprised to find that I was gone ?" " Surprised ? I was amazed! Edmund Ches- som met me at the gate and told me—what I had for some time known—that you were not his sister. He said, too, that your own mother had been lingering in the neighbourhood for some days, that she had the best claim upon you, and that he had sent you away in her charge. He told me that you had gone to London, and gave me your address. How could he have let you go? How could he have so coldly sent out from the shelter of his roof, with such a guardian, the child his father idolised? I could not have believed him so base. I hurried to Horsham, and came on to London by the next train. I think you needed me, Dora ?" " Yes ! yes !" said the young girl trembling. She looked up at him in a shy pleading, and for the first time saw how he had changed since they had parted a week before, at the gate of Chessom Grange. He looked years older. The gay, bright debonair look that had sat so well on his hand- some face hail given place to a grave expression, which thrilled Dora half with awe, half with tenderness.</p>
        <p>"I shall not betray him." The woman breathed more freely. "He'd ought to have changed his name before coming back to England," she said. "But, after all, it wouldn't have done any good. He'd have let out his: true name at the first ale-house he came to. But come to breakfast, Dora." Dora obeyed the summons, taking her seat at the table. Mrs. Narr sat down opposite to her, and proceeded to wait upon her. It was noticeable that the woman's manner was more respectful than it bad been. Dora's coldness and slight hauteur seemed to have had their due effect upon her. She watched her constantly and closely, as if seeking the cause of Dora's altered manner.</p>
        <p>It was as if she feared she had given utterance to some vital secret daring her Intoxication on the previous night. After breakfast, Mrs. Narr took the tray down to the kitchen, and Dora busied herself with some sewing. The day wore on. In the afternoon Squire Weir came again, remaining until evening. This day was a sample of the two following it. Jack Narr did not arrive as expected, and no letter had yet arrived from Felix Warner. On the fourth day, however, the peculiar knock of the London postman was heard on the door, and a letter was presently brought up to Dora. She recognised the handwriting as that of War- ner, and, disregarding Mrs. Narr's questions, hastily withdrew into her own room, locked the door, and gave herself up to its perusal. It was an ardent letter, full of protestations of love and assurance that Dora's changed circum- stances had not affected his passion for her. To the contrary, he declared that her desolation touched his very soul, and that he longed to fly to her, that he might comfort and protect her. He said that he was compelled to remain at Ss.ltair Manor with his cousin for some weeks yet, but that he would make her a flying visit on Tuesday "And to-day is ,uesday," thought Dora, with a thrill of pleased anticipation. "The letter was written on Saturday. The servant must have delayed to post it. Ho is on his way now. He does not tell me at what hour he may arrive. He may be here at any moment." She put the letter in her bosom and hastened to make a few alterations in her toilet, which might make her more pleasing in the eyes of her aristo- cratic lover. _</p>
        <p>Be had cast aside his boyhood wholly, and was a man, after a grand type—a noble, grave, chival- rous man, in whom was united the strength of a lion with the gentleness of a woman. "How you have changed, Noel !" the girl ex- claimed, involuntarily. "Have I? I have grown older, if you mean that. I have kept until very lately, much of my old boyish spirit, but it was time I looked at life squarely, and with a man's eyes. But now tell me of yourself, Dora." " There is but little to tell, Noel," said Dora, sighing. "The morning you overtook me on the road from the Hare and Hounds to the Grange, I had been to the inn to see her," and she inclined her head toward Mrs. Narr who had sunk into a drunken slumber. "Until that morning I had never &amp;earned that I was not the squire's daughter. "My poor Dora l" "She told me the truth. And that night, when I asked papa, he told me that I was only his adopted child. The shock of that discovery was second only to the awful shock that came a few hours later." "Poor little Dora ?" Encouraged by his genuine sympathy, Dora told the young squire all her story. When she had finished, he asked:</p>
        <p>Then she went out to the sitting-room. " Well, what's your news ?" demanded Mrs. Narr. "Mr. Warner will be here to-day !" "He will !" and the woman's eyes brightened. "That is good. You can marry him, if you want to, Dora. Only, whoever does marry you will have to pension off Jack and me, if he don't want us to settle in his neighbourhood !" Dora had not thought that her marriage was to afford the Narrs an opportunity to obtain black- mail, and while she was considering the subject, in a bitterness of spirit, a heavy step was heard on the stairs, and a thundering knock sounded on the door. "Conic in !" called out Mrs. Narr. The door opened, and a man strode into the room.</p>
        <p>"Have you written to—to Mr. Warner?' "Yes, I wrote to-day, before leaving the Grange. Tom posted the letter at Horsham, as we went to the station. Fe—Mr. Warner will get the letter to-morrow at the latest." "And he will be here on the day after," said Squire Weir, with a paling face. "He will fly to you, of course, Dora. "Yes, he will come at once," said Dora, blush- ing. "He—he told me that he loved me, Noel, and I expect to marry him, although I wrote to him yesterday, and offered to rele7tse him. I told him all about my birth and parentage, and sent him my intended London address. He comes of a proud family, Noel. Do—do you think that it will make any difference tc him that I am poor and her daughter ?" "He will but love you the more, Dora," Noel forced himself to say, in a choking voice. "Do you think so ?" said Dora, with something of her old brightness. "Oh, Noel ! Forgive me ?' She had seen the spasm of pain whitening his lips and convulsing his face, and remembered that the happiness she son vht would be builded on the young squire's misery. " Hush, Dora! Don't be troubled on my ac- count," Noel answered, with a quavering smile. "It is something to love as I love you, even if that love must be in vain. This love will deepen and widen my whole life, and I shall be the better for it. lam one of those who, loving once never cease to love; but that fact need not give you pain. I would rather see you happy with another than unhappy with me ; for my love is not selfish, Dora. I prefer with all my soul your happiness to my own." His noble self-Jmegation, his generosity and for- getfulness of self touched Dora as nothing could have done. " Noel, you must not live single for my sake," she whispered. "Do not let me blight your life." He smiled sorrowfully.</p>
        <p>With a great cry of welcome Mrs. Narr 4ew to- ward him, drew him further into the room, and embraced him. "It's Jack, sure enough 1" she ejaculated. "Jack at last." Dora looked at the new corner curiously. He was a stout man, of medium height, yet, by reason of his breadth, seeming shorter than he was. He had a red, fuli-moon face, framed in red and bristling hair, cropped clo,e to his round head. A red ftinge of whiskcrs adorned his ft cc. He looked the incarnation of vulgarity, was coarse and bluf and ignorant: fond of drink and the vices that go hand in hand with drunkenness, and had, as he had had all his life, a positive genius for getting his living at the expense of some other person. He was gaudily di'essed in plaided trousers, a red velvet waistcoat adorned with brass buttons, a blue satin scarf, in which was thrust a pin dis- playing an immense paste diamond, a velveteen coat, and, fin illy, an old dented hat set jauntily on the back of his head..</p>
        <p>•••••• r.s.a tav,..•••. It was evident that he had propped up his courage, or his strength, by a use of his favourite beverage just before art iving at Lowater Crescent. He looked, in short, like a retired and well-to-do prize-fighter. Such was J:ek Narr—the man who claimed to be the father of the pure, dainty and blossom-like Dora. "Yes, it's jack !" cried the man, with a hoarse laugh, looking around him. " Very nice quartrs, old girl ! Siting up for a nob, aint you? Beg !yarding, Miss," he added, his gaz:2 setting on Dora, and he made a sweeping bow to her. "Imeant no 'fence."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Narr pullea his sleeve. " Don't you un4lerz4tatll ?" she exclaimed. "That Is Dora—our girl, you know !" " All, yes. Happy to see you, Miss Dora."</p>
        <p>_ "Not Iltiss Dor;., -Jack, She's our dear girl that Squire Chesswii adopted, and left without a far- thing, you know. The Squire's dead, and his son has set Dora adrift to look out for herself. She's going to teach and suil..nt you and me, or else—"</p>
        <p>"is she ?" asked Jack Nurr, brightening at the thought of being suppoi ted by somebody. "It's very good of her, I must say. Shall be delighted, Miss Dora—"</p>
        <p>"Miss Dora again !" cried Mrs. Narr, impatient- ly. "Can't an idea be got into your head, Jack, short of vaccinating it in? The girl's ours, and you must call her Dora, or Dolly, just as you choose. But don't go to talking as if she was our superior." Jack Na.rr looked at the pale, high-bred girl, who was as unlike him and his wife as a stately lily or cultured rose is unlike a field bean, and his round face grew puzzled. "1 know it's all right," he muttered. "But dashed if I can quite get at it." " You 've been drinking Jack. That's why you 're so dreadful thicli -headed."</p>
        <p>"1 'vu be n drinking only enough to feel meller," said Mr. Narr, putting his thumbs into the arm- holes of his waistcoat and balancing himself jauntily and alternately on his beds and toes. " Sure there's no mistake now, old woman ? That's the girl ?" " Yes. How often must I tell you ? She is going to teach, us I said, to support us ; or, what's more likely, she's going to marry a rich fellow, an heir to a title, and we can live on Lim. He'll pay us any sent to keep out of the way and save his pride. Alt, Jack, there are easy times ahead of us."</p>
        <p>" Ah!" said a deep and significant voice at the open door. Dora turned a sti aka look in that direction.</p>
        <p>There, on the threshold, ‘vhere he had been standing unseen for several minutes, with a trauge and inscrutable expression on his counten- ance and a strange gleam in his eyes, stood Felix Warner!</p>
        <p>CHAYTER XIV. COLONEL EFFINGHAM.</p>
        <p>The boat-house at Saltair Al,Lnor, to which repeated allusion has been made, demands at this juncture a fuller description. It lay at the end of the little stone pier, which stretched from the foot of the stairway iu the rocks out into the waters of the little bay. It was built in two stories, the lower one being of stone, and some twenty feet in height. The upper storey was only about twelve feet high, and As side walls were almost entirely formed of movable shutters, capable of being opened or closed at pleasure, after the fasnion of many West Indian houses. These shutters were painted a deep, cool green, and in winter were protected by additional blinds. The roof projected slightly, and was painted in gay stripes, looking like a Venetian awning. This cool upper chamber in the boat-house, high up over the water, and swept through its shutters by the coolest sea-breezes even in the sultriest day in summer, was one Of the favourite resorts of the Lady Barbara Champney, who loved to lie npon its divan and listen to the musical swashing of the waves against the stone walls below, or against tho cliff's forming the sea-wall of the uninor.</p>
        <p>It had become an especially favourite retreat since Lord Champney's return to Saltair, and since he had developed a taste for boating. Left unfurnished during the winters the boat- house parlour, as it might well have been called, was now, in June, in its full summer attire.</p>
        <p>The floor was covered with a pale India matting. A few pictures, tiny scraps of bright scenes, were hung on the narrow panels between the jalousie shutters. A divan was range 4 against the wall, and a couple of arm-chairs of cane-work stood in the centre of the apartment, near a large round table. The remainder of the furniture consisted only of a large supply of Moorish cushions some of them heaped upon the divan, and others scattered in luxurious heaps about the floor. Upon the afternoon of the day on which Felix Warner had chosen to go to town for his flying visit to Dora, Cee Lady Barbara was half-reclining upon the cane-work divan, looking out upon the sea through partially opening shutters. Since the day on which he had made his memor- able visit to her sitting-room, bringing with him the mischief-making bouquet, the Lady Barbara had not seen Lord chanipney. Insrlted, tortured, and aggrieved, she had kept her chamber, not going forth even to her meals. Tiring of her self-imprisonment at last, h‘wever, she had come out this afternoon for a stroll in the garden and a lounge in the boat-house, and intended to make her appearance at the dinner- table. The afternoon was bright and pleasant, with a glorious flood of sunshine on the glittering waters, with their dancing foam-crests, and with a strong, sweet breeze that was inexpressibly delightful after the sultriness of the mGrning. The sea was dotted with the bright sails of fisher craft and larger vessels, and the Lady Barbara was idly watching them, and wondering which among them all was Lord Champney's sail, for he had gone out an hour before in the little yacht.</p>
        <p>"He may have gone over to Cromer," she thought, toying with the slats of the shutters. "He must miss Felix Warner, who started for town this morning. And that teminds me. Wil- lard Ames and I were strangely unjust to poor Felix, in thinking him guilty of a eonspiracy against me. But that is the way we misjudge people," she added, sighing. "We throw away a sincere friendsi ip for a seeming one—the real dia- mond for the paste 1 Sidney knew his cousin beat, after a 11.,, Watching yet a little longer, the Lady Barbara distinguished one sail which was making direct before the wind to Saltair. A further scrutiny con- vinced her that this was the graceful little yacht, homeward-bound. "I don't wish Sidney to find me here," she said to herself. "He will think I have been watching for him. I will go up on the lawn 1" She regarded the approaching boat through her spy-glass, and then, convinced of its identity, arose and shook down the long folds of her dress, pre- paring to retrace her way to the land. She presented a charming picture as she stood there, taking a last peep at the swift-sailing yacht through the interstices of the shutters, with her pale gold hair coiled high at the back of her head, one long curl straying over her shoulder, gleaming like sunshine, and with the folds of her sea-green silk floating about her, giving her the look of an Undine. And so thought—it was apparent—a man who had come up the flight of stone stairs silently, from the boat-house below. He was standing on the topmost stair, anl re- garding the Lady Barbara with a glance of the most intense admiration, taking in with his keen gaze every feature of her dazz!ing loveliness, from the perfect face to the gleaming curl, and the white arm which, bare to the elbow, the loose drapery falling away from it, looked as if sculptured from the whitest marble. This man, who was of middle age, was strikingly handsome, after a bold and wicked fashion, cud had a military air that was infinitely becoming to him.</p>
        <p>He was Col. Effingham, an officer in the army, a man of the worst type ; yet, because of his hand- some face and fortune, his polished manners, and air of fashion, he was courted and admired by half his fashionable world. _</p>
        <p>The look of admiration deepened in his bold black eyes as the Lady Barbara's attitude changed to one of unconscious grace. • "Beautiful !" he exclaimed, aloud, as if in a rapture. "Superb !" The Lady Barbara started as if stung, and turned hastily, facing him. "Colonel Effingham 1" she ejaculated, in amaze- ment. The colonel, holding his hat in his hand, bowed deeply. "It is I, Lady Barbara," he said, humbly. "Pardon my intrusion, but the seivant sent me here in quest of you." "Indeed 1" cried the Lady Barbara, in a haughty scorn. "I receive no one here but my friends. Be kind enough to relieve me of your preseucc at ouce."</p>
        <p>ipe colonel flashed under her cutting tone, trelik ..e. up the stair, approaching her. _</p>
        <p>" Some one must have maligned me to you, Lady Barbara," he said, deprecatingly. "Am I not your friend? Certainly, if a kindly feeling toward you could constitute me such, I am one." "I have no wish to hold a conference with you." said the Lady Barbara, pointing to the staircase. " Go !"</p>
        <p>"Surely you will not condemn me unheard?" Zerf4iBted the colonel.</p>
        <p>The Lady Barbara's blue eyes flashed like drawn steel.</p>
        <p>"You dare urge your defence," she cried, "when you have grossly insulted me beyond all forgivaness by the bouquet and the note inclosed in it which you sent me the other day? You have not been condemned unheard, Colonel Effingham. My hus- band would have punished you on sight—" "You showed him the note, then ?" interrupted the colonel, his countenance falling. " No ; but be saw the bouquet, and knew that it inclosed a note. To save my name from scandal, I screened you from Lord Champney's just wrath." The colonel smiled again. "But beware of so insulting me the second time," said the baroness, haughtily. "I know Lord Champney's unfortunate pecu- liarity," observed Col. Effingham, coolly. "To speak against any man's attentions to him would be like pouring spirits on a fire. He has a theory that any woman who respects herself will never be ins.ulted. Consequently, when you tell him you have been insulted, what will he say to you ?" "I shall not appeal to him. I shall avenge my own wrongs." The colonel arched his brows. "But how ?" he asked. "If you venture on my premises again, I will have you horsewhipped; that is how! Since you are insensible to appeals made to your honour, let 113 see whether you will be sensible to an infamous punishment at the hands of a menial," The colonel glared at the baroness. "This to me ?" he ejaculated. "Be careful, Barbara Champney. I never forgave an insult in my life. I could not forgive one even from you, it I did not love you so." "Love me, a married woman l" "Yes. You are married, it is true; but such marriage as yours is a marriage but in name. love you; and, loving you I forgive you. Hei me, Barbara. I have loved you always._ 1 knot .1,11 u U 'y iiiyour mat Ld life—that your husband is not worthy of you." " It is false I He is worthy "lie is jealous and exacting to the highest de- gree. He has no faith in you, and dares not let you look upon another man, lest you prefer that other. This is no life for you, Barbara, beautiful B irbara I You were made to live in an eternal suNshine. This cruel jealousy on the part of your husband is killing you. If you would but listen to me, I make your life a very dream of bright- nes. I love you, Barbara! I love you !" He told the truth. The Lady Barbara's scorn of him, her repellant coldness, had caused his first fancy for her to deepen into a wild, mad passion. It was his nature to long to possess that which was, or seemed, beyond his reach. Besides, Effingham was the dupe and instru- ment of Felix \Varner, who had persuaded him that lie had but to sue earnestly to possess. War- ner's great scheme was to separate Lord Champ- 'ley and his wife, by persuading the former, whose weakness he well knew, of the infidelity of the latter. In the event of the baron's dying childle,s. Warner would reap his great success by inheriting both title and estates. And to make the Liuiy Rarbara appear faithless in the eyes of her husband, Warner had impressed upon the flood of Etli.ioiant that the Lady Barbara secretly loved him, bat that her pride was stronger than her lovr,</p>
        <p>Effingham's vanity made him believe this hor- rible falhehood even now, in the face of her lady- scorn and contempt. " 1 love you, Barbara 1" he repeated. "I love</p>
        <p>you !" - The 14,.“1y Barbara grew white to the very lips. t; lancing out of the (Teo shutters, she saw the littlo yacht under a spread of sail, driving straight fo, the bout-house, and so near that she could dis- tinguish Lord Chtbmpuey's features as he sat in the b-,6t aboie.</p>
        <p>A feeling of desperation came over her. " You will not relieve me of your hatefa presence ?" she demanded. " Not till you say you love me." The baroness plated through the shutters. " Du you see that boat coining?" she asked. calmly. "It contains my. husband. Will you go flow?' " I must repeat what I said. I will not go till you say you love me. Let your husband come. He shall find me on my knees at your feet. Then, Barbara, you %ill have to take refuge in my arms from the storm of his hatred and jealousy." " Monster ! " saai Le im,roikess, her eyes gleam- ing like meteors, " Coward ! And you call your- self a gentleman I s there ever known a crea- ture base under that name before?'</p>
        <p>" Queenly Liarbara—" "Silence ! Do not dare to speak my name I You dare talk of love to ,ne ?" " They should have named you Medea, Barbara." If a look could have slain nim Effingham would have dead at her feet.</p>
        <p>LA l• • V4.‘.• "vs But he, believing in his ultimate victory, only laughed at her awful anger and scorn. " There is no use in putting on all these airs to me, Barbara," he exclaimed. "I have sworn to win you, and I shall. Your husband does not deserve you—" "Hush Dare not to take his honoured name on your vile lips." "You have greater faith in him than he has in you," sneered Effingham. The Lady B,Li bara again looked from the window. How very near the boat was coming She could see the sombre look on Lord Champ- ney's face now, as he sat on the boat cushions, moody and troubled, and darkly anxious. ‘Vhat if he should come and tied this man there!</p>
        <p>" How near he is 1" said Effingham, coolly, his glance following hers. " lie will come into the boat-house ; he will hear voices up here, and come up ; he will find us—and then ! What will he say, Ilirbara., to beo me at your feet? Ah ! you tremble ! You are afraid of him. Tell me that you will try to love me ; encourage me the least bit ; say I may came ngsio, and I wili avert from you the coming terror. ,•liay something, Barbara, and I will go." He waited breatldely for her reply. "Coward ! miscreant 1" was her indignant response ; "I would not spe..li the word you want were it to save my hie. I would sooner die than give you the smaliest word of ,encouragement." The boat was corning swiftly nearer. Col. Effingham drew closer to her, a smile of triumph deeptnim: on_his visage.</p>
