        <head>r PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>r PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>THE old hen flew from her nest and cackled loud d long. BROWN : "So your wife has gone over to the °silent majority'?" J4nies : Well, they called it that before she died." Kirrii: "harry won't take no for an answer." trrilbena : "how do you know ?" Kittie "Be- cause I sha'n't give it to him." "WELL, I'll have to bid you good-bye." "Oh ! ot now, George. Mother says we musn't make any noise until father gets to ICep." A YOUNG lady who has given much thought to the subject says that a kiss is the mouth-peace of love.</p>
        <p>"MAMMA, why has the month of February twenty-nine days every fourth year?" "What a foolish question ! So that people born on February 29 can have a birthday once in a while."</p>
        <p>" Wfizs eggs are sixpence a dozen," said the old rooster, eyeing the performance with languid disapproval, "it is a ridiculous exhibition of vanity to make all that fuss over one egg." "JUST one kiss ! " he pleaded, with outstretched arms. She burst into tears. "Oh ! you stingy thing !" she wailed. '</p>
        <p>MOTIII•M : "Oh ! you naughty boy. You've been to dada's desk and upset all his papers. What will he say when he comes home?" Son (hopeful) :"I know what he'll say, hut you'd spank me if I told you." EDITH : " Which are the most prone to marriage, the tall or the short men, Proh,o-; " professor " Well, from the number o I,,,decunee I see hang- ing round pretty heiresses, I should say, decidedly it is the men who are short."</p>
        <p>"I WANT to marry your dughter," said the young bicycle scorcher. "C-n't you give us a start in life ?" "Certainly," replied the old man, opening the front door; "I beli.tve you've tried the flying start before."</p>
        <p>A CEP.TAIN missionary to the heathen made a good many converts, to whom he was exceedingly liberi!; but growin..; in time weary of this method of Christianising„ he one day told a party of chiefs that they would get no more gifts. "No more blankets, no more 'lncca, no more rum?" said the chief. " Well, then, no more hallelujahs." YOUNG Mr. Fitts came in from his daily toil, and passed his wife with a friendly nod "I think you forgot something." she said with a pout. "I believe I did," said he, and tlien kissed her. "Really," she said, "I wasn't thinking of that at all. I was thinking of the baking powder." She was even with him.</p>
        <p>"WHERE is your money?" demanded the masked man. The miser was nearly paralysed with fear. " Un—und—under the carpet," he faltered. The pistol fell from the nerveless hand of the burglar, and he turned feebly towards the door. It was taking up carpets that had driven him to drink and crime.</p>
        <p>" HERE'S a box addressed to you," said the wife of a prominent man. "I don't hink you'd better open it, though." "What shall we do with it?" "I'll tell you what—we'll take it out and get the servant to open it. She has lit the fire with paraf- fin three times this week, and I don't think that dynamite will hurt her." ALCMS US (meeting his old professor, after greet- ing): " I'm glad to hear, Pr,,fessor, that you are going to lecture in our town on your favourite old- tini topic." Professor : "Yes, yes. But I have di vested it of technicalities—in fact, recast it, so that it will reach all persons of very limited ac- quirements. Hope to see you there.'</p>
        <p>s THE SEASON'S DEPRESSION.—" The weather is very trying for everybody," says the: physician. Yep," replied Mr. Meekton. "1 don't see how my wife is going to bear up under it. When the sun .loesn't shine it gives her the blues, and when it does she says it's fading the carpet." " Georgie, dear ! " She looked anxiously at the Zock, which pointed to 12.30. " What is it, love ?" "I don't wish to hurry you away, George, tett von know that, papa is not over fond of you, and .he is an early riser." At this hint George commenced to get ready to take his departure. ADVICE OF AN EXPERT.--HOja:4 "I don't know -what to do with that dog. I've tried a dozen times to give him away, but no one will have him." Tomdik : Tried to give him away, did you? That'e no way to get rid of a dog. Ask forty-five or fifty ,dollars fir him." A CLEVER HOAX.—One of the best of the numer- ous class of sham Latin inscriptions was one which appetred in a Dublin paper some years ago. It was in antique" Latin," as follows: "I, Sabilli- lunges' ago, ' fortibus' es in aro. Nosces mare threbe ' trirc, votis innem . . . pea' an , dux,'" and purported to have been found near the site of a church dedicated to "the saint known to the old chroniclers as Uricatus Ambulans." The "Latin " inscription was in reality an absurd rhyme—" I say, Biliy, here's ago !—forty 'buses in a row." No,' says Mary, they be trucks.' What is in ' ern ?' 'Peas an' ducks.'"</p>
        <p>A PARTY of friends were sitting at a tavern, full of tun and mischief, when all at once an ac- quaintance of theirs, named Sampson, appeared on the scene. " !" exclaimed one of them, "now Ue can dare the police and the bum-bailiffs to do their worst; with Sampson to help us we have nothing to fear from such Philistines." "Quite so," Sampson replied, "if only one of you woe' I lend me his jawbone." A RARA Avis.—A fisherman of Exmouth lately caught a gull, and flattening out its beak and .stuffing it full of sand made it resemble a villa duck, which he sold to a gentleman for such. On the bird being prepared for table, the trick was discovered. Meeting the 'fisherman some days after, the gentleman asked him how it happened that the bird was full of sand. " Lor' sir," was CIL: answer, "them sort o' birds always takes in iiand for ballast just before rough weather." .IZAINNY AND HIS KNIFE.—A well known bishop via.. teaching a class of street waifs, and one ragged 11 1,!() boy was so much impressed with Iti. exhoria- ti.es that he took a knife out of his pocket, saying -1,0 the bishop: " You cam keel) it, so's 1 won't cut the benches." A little while ait.erwa.rds there was A coni!notion in the class, and the little iiokeinpt tho knife-owner came up to the bishop, and, vtilting with excitement, said: "Mr. bishop ! johliny )rati'A his knife back again. • He says he's -•••, o.'t? to i;WOP it for a P</p>
        <p>zls e.</p>
