      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>The result of the Emperor William's Transvaal telegram is still with us. At the time of the out- cry German travellers were handicapped terribly in the race for business. One English firm which left off buying German goods on account of the tele- gram still sticks to the goods of its own country, though it is out of pocket by it. A prominent member of a firm of English agents was telling me in the City on Monday that their orders for Eng- lish steel from one Lincolnshire house alone had increased threefold since the Emperor William penned his telegram. The buyers, of course; have to pay a bigger price. This instance of commercial patriotism is by no means unique.</p>
        <p>The Czar, I am told, has left the impre.ssica on some of those who have seen him during this of not being at all reserved or reticent by nature, but of being timid through inexperience and silent through fear that he may make a mistake. The air of melancholy which is characteristic of the fiomanc,ffs is very marked in him at times, and he is said to suffer from fits of f'x! reme depression. His position seems to weigh him down as it did his father, and of course he has not yet the mental and physical robustness or the experience of the latter. His mind is said to be one which would be deeply impressed in such conditions as those in which he has spent the last fortnigb; but then the impression might be dimished or effaced by his responsible advisers. Not much initiative, still less a change in a settled policy, is anticipated from the Czar himself.</p>
        <p>Then it i 3 mentioned to me as a somewhat un- fortunate coincidence that the Minister in attend- ance on him should be the famous head of the Asiatic Department of the Russian Foreign Office. M. Shislikin has had more to do with this country than any other Russian offijai, save one or two of the members of the diplomatic service, and his business with us has usually been of a contentious kind—Afghan fron- tier disputes and troublesome incidents, the Pamirs, Cores, Persian intrigues, and the I,lce,—so that the worst side of Great Britain, or at least the most unpleasant side from a Russian point of view, has always been exposed to M. Shislikin's contem- plation. It is said, however, to be probable that the Czar will rely on M.de Staal rather than any one else at present in regard to Great Britain, and it is not doubted that the Balmoral visit will be benefi- cial to some extent to the Armenian cause, but to what extent or in what particular way few can at present tell. There are probably not more than half- a-dozen persons in the British islands who know what was said to or by the Czar about the Armen- ian question during the past fortnight, and none of these are likely to speak about it. Hence those whose hearts are sick for the sufferings of the Ar - meniana must look for the beneficial results of this visit and of their own energetic agitation to Con- stinople and to the events which will follow there.</p>
        <p>In the death of Mr. Byron Reed the Tory organi- sers lose one of their best workers and most effec- tive speakers; while the Church Defence Union is deprived of a zealous partisan and, perhaps. its ablest lecturer in the anti-disestablisbment crusade The capture of the entire representation of Brad- ford by the Tories was one of the heart-breaking results of the Liberal defeat in the summer of last year, and the Liberal chief organisers are deter- mined that advantage shall be taken of the vacancy to make a strenuous endeavour to regain the seat. Local Veto and a considerable Labour defection were put down by the party managers as the main CRUSE'S which contributed to Mr. Caine's repulse. With those obstacles practically out of the way,the Liberals at headquarters are very hopeful of win- ning at the by-election.</p>
        <p>The Agricultural Rating Act is not proving such a boon and blessing to farmers as Mr. Henry Chap- lin anticipated. I was in Norfolk the other day, where, as everywhere else,the farmers have suffered sf,verely from the bad weather during harvest. I asked one farmer if he was satisfied with the Act. His reply was eloquent. He silently drew from his pocket a notice informing him that his housesvzhich t,nd previously been assessed at £22 106., would as a result of the Act be assessel in future at £37 10s.</p>
        <p>An earnest of the determination of the Irisb Unionist Alliance to give no more electoral a-sist- .nce to the Government for the present is afforded by the stoppage of " Notes from Ireland." The current issue of this periodical, which was etarted in 1886 ostensibly as an impartial record "of the sayings and doings of the Home Rule parties," but really to provide material far Unionist speakers and writers, contains an announcement that " after tit- publication of the present number the issue of 'Notes from Ireland ' will be suspended;" and al- Though the readers of the sheet are informed that "a record of events such as has hitherto been pub- tiahed in "Notes from Ireland' will continue te b. made and tabulated in the nffices.of the Alliance," the fact that the London office is to be closed at the end of the year deprives the saving clause of its pretended value. To put the matter in a nutshell, the Irish landlords are incensed at their treement by Mr. Gerald Balfour in connection with the Land Bill; and, believing that Home Rule nas been -*eyed off for some years,they are bent on reprisals of this kind.</p>
        <p>When the Parisians make au their mind to enjoy a brief season of madness they do the thinv thoroughly. Never, apparently, was Paris so lull nor so frenzied nor so well dressed as on Tuesday. The telegraph service betwcen Cherbourg and Pari. seems to have broken down at the moment when the Czar was expected to land in France, and con- sequently some very remarkable descriptions of the event appeared in the Paris newspapers. Th.- " Patrie " authoritatively announced the arrival at two o'clock in the afternoon, which was about an hour before the Imperial yacht was sighted. !Lieber announced that on the stroke of the hour the Czar stepped upon Cherbourg quay, and, falling upon the neck of M. Faure, kissed him, amid the frantic plaudits of the bystanders. I understood that several 14'.nglish visitors who left for Paris on Sunday were put to extraordinary straits for lodg- ings, and finally had to take up their quarters in a distant suburb.</p>
        <p>The Colonial Office adv,ertisement inviting apoli- , cations for one hundred vacancies in the Imperial Police Force for British South Africa has drawn forth shoals of letters and telegrams. More that, ,llffielOht applications were received on Tuesday. , and large numbers were delivered on Wednes,!a, , [Horning. It is considered likely that the number of appointments will be increased.</p>
        <p>The rutnoured betrothal of the daughter of the Prince of Wales to Prince Joseph of Battenberg is not likely to be true. It would not be a populat step. At this time it would be tactless also, and that is a fault never committed by the 13,,ya1 Family. Prince Joseph is the younger brother of the late Prince Henry, and he is an estimable fel- low personally. But, all that apart, another Ger- man alliance would be vastly disagreeable at a time when tLe Emperor is acting on the continent as our bitter foe.</p>
        <p>No invitations were sent out for the funeral of William Morris, but, nevertheless, a large number of friends and fellowworkers gathered at lielmscott House on Tuesday !morning in order to accompany the body to Paddington. Station. Morris would have greatly valued this spontaneou• mark of affection, the more especially because the attend- ance was largely made up of working men of the district. An ordinary hearse was used at this end, hut at Lechlade a farmer's waggon littered with moss bore the coffin, which was finally lowered to the grave by the farmer's men. The coffin was straight in shape, following the custom of that medifeval period which the poet loved so much, and was of plain unpolished oak, with wrought-iron handles and an inscription Cut into the lid. Two beautiful mats lay in the coffin, forcibly recalling the accounts of that other well-lo ved poet and man in far-off Samoa.</p>
