      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <p>LADIES" LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DR NELLIL;</p>
        <p>October is here, atmosphere. with the f us that it is time to pr to replen is: ments. Th</p>
        <p>—d a fresh crispness in the -ailing of the leaves, warns for cooler weathei. and s with seasonable gar- be a pleasurable task,</p>
        <p>h our warth:</p>
        <p>Imno</p>
        <p>for, as usual at tlw Commene,:inent of a new season, all shops are full et attractive novelties, suitable for every oeeasi,n; and very 'beautiful model, Fire almaily Lobe ~e,tri in ir,;ol. les, costumes, and nil:hoer,. T;.e :levy f;re,.s mato:la.,: are par- ticularly handsome and original in design, and a very distinctniange ill be seen in the fashion of the winter mrottles. The millinery s quiet and elegant in i.yle, with tottelies of bright colour, chiefly in velvet, to relieve it. from sombreness. Some great no'velties have been brought out in furs, notably electric sealskM, Which will be welcomed hy thoSe of modest means who have hitherto sighed in vain for a seal,kin</p>
        <p>A very ele!zant hat is in cherry-coloured bass straw, trimnied with black lisse quilling, and black ostrich tips. In the front is a bow of black velvet ribbon.</p>
        <p>Some smart tailor costumes are 'being built for the present season. They are sometimes composed of tweed, cloth, or again of. thick serges and alpacas. The newest models are made with plain skirts and tight-fitting bodices. For example, a neat costume 9f prune cloth has the usual plain skirt, the bodice is cut in a double-broasted shape with a short pointed basque, the upper part is cut well off the shoulders, and crosses beneath a fitting vest of velvet, with large revers. The sleeves are in an ample coat shape. and the -front of the bodice fastens on the left side with three large buttons.</p>
        <p>Coat and skirt costumes still continue to be worn, although they are not so popular as they were at one time. A becoming coat and skirt is of coarsest t:cru toned sacking with cuffs aud revers of black glitcó ilk, a vest of white muslin and car- ickmacros3 lace. A hat to match is of rough black straw, deeted with black. A new coat is comple- ted with a square sailor collar at the back, sur- mounted by another in a medici shape, which sets up high at the back of the neck. Another novel coat has a turned down collar, cut in one with square revers, which end at the bust line, this is faced with velvet. The coat fronts are arranged to fasten hick on either side beneath the revers with large bone buttons. Cape mantles will be much worn this season as well as jackets. Those who intend only to adopt the latter may have their dress bodioes fitted with movable trimmings, which may be removed when the jacket is worn. Dainty collars, capes, and &amp;hos of white lace are always charming at any time of year; they are worn extensively this sea- son, and are frequently arranged so that they can I* taken off when desired.</p>
        <p>There is very little to call for remark in the Change of fashion of dressing the hair at present, .but some ingenious novelties have hen devised to facilitate the arranging of the hair in the present %ode, and the most important of these is a new Pompadour frame for the back of the head only, Which, When covered with naturally wavy hair, an be worn tv anyone even with short hair, and gives all the appearanee of a stylish puff under the toil at the hack. This is quite a new idea, and being very light and transparent, it keeps the head Cool and comfortable. Another new Pompadour frame goes all round the head, and holds out the pair in puffs in a most approved fashion. A good idea is a new fringe, parted in the middle, with Witvy hair at the sides and very light curls on the forehead. These useful Pompadour frames can also be had for the front and sides only, extending from tar to ear, and holding the waved hair well up at the top and sides oely, but it is more fashionable tO have the hair pdnd all round. Your loving JENNIE.</p>
