      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>It is'curious ant no one has drawn a favourable omen fromthe fact that the Emperor of all the Russsfas is con- tent to trust his little dauAter to the care of an English nurse. The little Grand Duchess is everything to the Czar and Czlrina, and yet no consideration of the " traditional" hatred of Russia and England has been allowed to come in here.</p>
        <p>Saturday last was the h'lthday of Princess Henry of Battenberg's youngest child, little Prince MaJrice, who was five years old. Be was born at Balmoral and upon that happy October day a magnificent benfire was kindled on Craig Gowan and all the eillies,cottagers and keepers on the Queen's estate mar hed to the Castle to the sound of the bagpipes and danced and made merry after the Highland fash'on. Princess Henry's children are all exceptionally pretty. but Princess Ens has that almost wistful expression of sadeess characteristic of the late Princess Alice and of her daughter the present Empress of Russia. Apropos of the little Prince's birthday, grown people I th ok de not always appreciate what an event a birth- day is in a child's life. Seven is so very much older than six, and in some homes the perfectly na' ural delight is increased by the hearty mu Dal hy of the parents, but there are other households in which the all important day passes unnetieed, save by a carelvss reminder to the child that he has begun another yeer. Only last week my heart was moved to pity by talking with a bright little fellow who told MP he was seven years old on that day. I Raked him to .how me his presents, but he looked surprised,and told me that they didn't keep birth- days at all, as his mother thought it foolish. We cannot help feeling grieved for such unfortunate little ones. Childhood is so brief and the time when each recurring birthday is not a jet- comes so soon, that it is a thousand pities not to make the anniversaries joyous while it is psesible to do so. During the latter end of next month the Prince and Princess of Wales, with Princess Victoria and prcbably Prince and Princess Charles of Denmark, purpose paving a visit to the Duke and Duchess of Marlborough at Blen- heim. Among the wonderful things to be seen there is the painted ceiling by Sir Joshua Reynolds which is val- ued at £40,000. This beautiful work of art has a curious history. After its completion, Sir Joshua desiring to have a smaller drawing made of it, entrusted the work to one of his pupils to whose studio it was taken. It was then seized for debt, and the man who took it de- cided to cut out all the head. and make different pictures of them. The pupil however confessed the state ot affairs to his master, who was, fortunately, just in time to rescue his work from the hands of the creditor.</p>
        <p>At the present time Mr. Sims Reeves, accompanied by his young wife and three months old beby,is fulfilling a successful tour in S eith Africa. It appears his future plans include a tour in either Awtralia or America. Mr. Reeves has not sung in America before, thniieh twice he accepted offers ansl signed contracts with that end in view. In the first instance he was offered by Stephen Friske £30,000 to sing at a hundred concerts in the States,but Frisks was shot before the aff air was com- pleted. The second arrangement was with Flartog but she died suddenly in Charlestown. Tw )or three great ladies of the London world, who for some years past had assunsed the onerous task of leading the fashions in dress, came recently to hopeless financial grief,and have perforce resigned the role. It is a high price to pay for such setorial eminence to be for years in debt an difficulty and not know where to turn when one is in need of a few gowns, and such ominous words as " the messenger has been instructed not to leave the dress until the account is settled " are heard with a sinking of the heart. in many a palatial London house where money and money's worth appears as plen- tiful as dust. Royal Princesses, as we all know, never adopt a new fashion until it is at least twelve months oldiand never relinquish it until a year or so after the date that other people do so. This is as it should be. Our Princesses have all too high a standard of life to permit themselves so frivolous and at the same time BO costly an occupation as leading the fashion in dress. Linen caars and cuffs are in considerable favour,and there is no denying the fact that they are a great iin- provenaent to our morning eowes. Adorned with a turned down collar,turned back ciiffs,and a pretty apron a middle aged frock, provided it pe,eesses no furbelows of any sort or deseripPion, can he made to do duty for the morning, until it reaches advaric, d old age, and yet look dainty. Pretty trained enwris f3r 'semen who pos- sess no household duties are of course delightful, but for the busy housekeeper skirts destined to he worn during the early hours of the dey should well clear the around, as it is possible to move about much more swiftly if the clinging trailing skirts are dispensed with. Princess dresses will be mu •h worn this winter. The skit' •t in one with the bodice are of the new form, which eave superseled the hell cut and all the fussy ful- ness at the foot. New they are shaped sufficiently in fronteteit the rest of the folds are relegated to the back. Tinsel is being lavishly used, epaulettes, collarettes, bre- tellee, are all outlined with ties. I paseementerie,or with narrow gold and silver hrail. Jot also i in greater re- quest than ever, an item of news we cannot he too grateful for, as it is a trimming which can be liberally used without looking everdone,and it is handsome on all colours.</p>
        <p>Seams are asfmrting themselvos. TAlor-made gowns are frt quentlr strappnd in a sit nrnamental fashion, which for the benefit of thn nninitiated means that a strap of the material is stitched over the join at either edge,the width being mo,,tly half an inch or loss. 1 have also espied seam q marke3 hy entnured velvets, and the strapping formed info diatrrind and other dosigns,intro- &amp;iced on the shouhler tlade g at the back, and at the top of the dart seams in front.</p>
        <p>The growth of the Amvriean post office business has been amazing of late years. At rh. el-';e of the revolu- tionary war there were on- 75 n.-)st 1%•-efi in the States. It the close of the war in 1812 tho-e were 8 f)oo.while at the beginning of the civil war the.. were 28.588 There are now 70.380 pest offices in the United Seates and the number is constantly being, inereAsed. Speaking of the post office, I am reminded of me of the oddest requests ever recorded. An Indian gentleman. residing in Lan- don, asked to be allowed to send home the cremated ashes of his brother for burial in the Ganges. He was informed that if he could limit- the weight of his brother to eleven pounds the post offiee would undertake the deity! The very latest craze amoog• op.to-dste women, is to have their initial or crest on the ftnit. that is sent to table, and it is rosnaged in tbig wise. A few days before the fruit ripens, the rioairzn which it isintended it should show. is cut nut nn pip. and nvited on it. The fruit is allowed to remain for a few days in this condi- tion and then the paper is remove i in the process of washing. It is then found that all. that portion which has been covered has bec,tne whiPP Ind the design shows to a greater or less extent according toithe action of the revs of the sun.</p>
        <p>-Hero le an excellent recipe far making! coffee. The water ehould he boiled in a iiane,ovari kept for coffee making only. When the water hail% the coffee, mixed with a very small quantitr of el-icory (the proportion being half an ounce to a nannd of coffee) And the quan- tity' of coffee waid a dessertspoonful to each breakfaat cup required. ehould he rut into thp aaccopan of boiling water, removed from the fir. ani loft to stand three minutes, after which a quartcr of a re,enpful of cold water should he poured i'lto it quickir In a few moments the crounde all apttle. The coffee strong and cleat should then be floured thr-ueh a. flannel bag into a saucepan of hot milk, the m;lk and coffee together bronvht to the boil, and the result will he. the best cup of coffee in the world.</p>
