        <p>N. A</p>
        <p>sATiTui) ya OCTOBER 10 1896.</p>
        <p>dition of the channel would be restored to its proper con- dition iu accord ince with the terms of the agreement. The Town Clerk had handed to him two letters from Mr. Ilunter, the engineer to the Ship Canal Company, in which the writer referred to the plans in regard to the prnposed penstock and the lay. by. The engineer stated that the company was now prepared to construct both these important works without further delay. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor HEATON seconded the confirmation of the minutes.</p>
        <p>Councillor BENNETT believed the sewage scheme of the Manchester Corporation was one of the most important schemes which had been before the public for many years and was one that was calculated to do more in- jury to Warrington than any scheme in their m-tnory. It was intol,rable that Manchester having been tined f polluting the upper reaches of the river should propose to take their sewage to the lower reaches, alid inflict an intolerable nuisance there. The Council were bound to oppose the scheme resolutely. It had teen algued that the sewage would be treated but if so what was the necessity of building a culvert from Manchester to Handles Creek? He personally objected most strongly to it. He protested against any further pollution of the river. It was at a particularly bad time that Manches- ter should have the audacity to thrust its sewage on them.</p>
        <p>I Councillor limits asked the Chairman of the Rivers Committee to very carefully consider this important matter. With all due respect to the Chairman and corn- mittee,Warrington had in a measure been left out in the cold with respect to the Manchester Ship Canal in many matters. He considered that their own sewage might be disposed of by the same pipe used by the Manchester Corporation, and he thought if the matter was laid before them in the shape of an agreement it might be easy to settle the question. Why not dispose of the sewage of Warrington by the same means that the sew- age of Manchester was carried to the lower reaches of the river? He was sure that every member of the Coun- cil would agree with him that if it could be done it would be a good thing for this town. Councillor LONOTON said they all recognizr:d the gravity and importance of this matter. There wa.s,how- ever, one important point that had not been referred to, viz: the abstraction from the river of a large coentity of water, which would result from the scheme. That would be a serious matter for those who used the river for shippine purposes. Alderman Surrox thought it bad been an unfortunate thing that they ha i spent such a large sum of money in obtaining what they had got from the Snip Canal Co. He theught if they had only had the experience of the past at the time the q lesion came before the Rivers Committee, they might have seen their way to have saved a large sum and to have obtained a good deal more than they had. Be hoped that with the experience they now had they would not have a recurrence of the large expenditure in connection with the Ship Cnal In the proposed sewage disposal scheme of the Manchester Corporation. He thought it behoved every member of the Rivers Committee to zo int ) the scheme thoroughly, and consider very carefully any step they might have to take regarding what they thought was necessary to be done in referenee to the scheme. He thought they could not afford to go on spending money in the fashion they had done. from a legal point of view.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS said he did not understand what it was Alderman Sutton referred to. If that gentleman had stated—as he might have done—some specific ques- tions with regard to which there had been xcessive ex- penditure it would have given him an opportunity of ,tating the facts in regard to that expenditure. It was difficult to reply to general statements. All he could say was that they had avoided litigation with the Canal Company,which would have cost the town ten or twenty times the amount which had been expended on the negotiations. He could say without fear of being chal- lenged that they had secured by negotiation benefits for Warrington that no amount of mon:‘y in litig tion would have secured for them. Fortunately they hid had a certain amoent of control, or Warrington would havc: been left high and dry to take its chances and do the best it could. There are been no litigation since he had been chairman of the committee; on the contrary every- thing had been done by friendly negotiation. Alderman Surrow : There has been a lot of unneces- sary litigation. Councillor Rosman said that was a matter of opinion. If there had been. Alderman Sutton was a member of the committee which sanctioned the expenditure, and he ought to have prevented it. It was not fair to the com- mittee and to him as chairman to bring up that charge that night, when it was utterly and absolutely un- founded. In all these negotiations the committee had cansidered not merely the interests of the Corporation as a Corporation, but the interests of the traders and the riparian owners, and that had been done in joint con- ference. The terms came to had received the support of these bodies all along the line. The whole of the terms of the agreement with the company were that day pre- juliced by the present proposal of that company. Were the Corperatien of Warrington prepared to sit dawn to the poisibility of an entir revarsal of tha terms of the agreement, and to give away now and for ever what- ever value the river might have as a navizable stream? No! They would be false to the interests of Warring- ton, and degrading the presperty and further extension of Warrington, if they were not determined at any cost to protect that which belonged to them and which no one elie had a right to. One of the questions involved was that by the scheme they would be robbed of part of the flow which at present Came down the river with the sewage. Ev.7ry- one knew perfectly well that when the canal was con- structed the natural flow of water was interfered with ; in other words, where they had previously got the whole of the water from the Irwell and the Mersey they only got that portion which did not pass down the Ship Canal. The effect of that had been to diminish the deptb of the chanetel, and the reason of their agreement with the comnany was to get them to make up by artificial means what they had previously had by natural means. The proposal of Manchester was in effect to further rerluee the flow of water at Howley by from 4,000,000 to 7,000.000 gallons per day. The effect would be this— that the Ship Canal Company would find it absolutely Impossible to carryout the terms of their agreetnent with the Warrington Corporation, and they would he the first parties to turn round to the Corporation and say "When we entered into an agreement• with you the eonditions were not as they are to-day. You have allowed those conditions to be altered and we repudiate our responsi- bility." (Hear. hear.) If the Council wanted someone else to take the chairmanship of the Rivers' Com- mittee--</p>
        <p>Aldsrman Surrow : I think you are going a great den 1 toq f r.</p>
        <p>Councillor PIRRPOINT: "No, no." Alderman Surrott : I am appealing to the Mayor and not to Feu. Mr. Pierpoint. I observed that ev.ryone of us would have to be careful in dealing with this ques- tion, and that we should not spend a larger sum of money than is absolutely necessary. There is not the slightest peobability of the scheme as propounded-- Councillor PIERPOINT : This is too bad altogether. I protest Th. MAYOR: You cannot make a speech Alderman Steton. (Cries of "Chair, chair.") Cminrillnr PiesPoiNT : I protest. The MAYOR: I think this discussion is very unwise. Councillor PIRRPOINT : Most unwise. TWe MAYOR: You are only showing your hand to the Manchester people.</p>
        <p>Councillor Nguema (to Alderman Sutton) : Sit down.</p>
        <p>Alderman Sutton said he would obey the Mayor. He then took his seit,Coonedlor Pierpoint again telling htm to do so.</p>
        <p>CouncillorßommT4 (nntinning) said if the Council was prepared to sacrifice what the Chairman of the commit- tee and the committee knew to be of vital importance to Warrington for the sake of a fey hundreds or a few thousand pouads,he should be no party to such conduct. He believed even now that it was possible to come Co terms with Manchester that would be satisfactory to them without litigation, and that would be their first attempts but failing to reach the issue in a friendly way they were prepared to fight. (Rear, hear.)</p>
        <p>The minutie were then eerei.d to.- ELEC CMG AN ALDERM N.—A CLOSE CONTEST.—</p>
        <p>_ LIVELY PROCEEDING. The MAYOR said the next business before the Council was the election of an alderman in place of Alderman Holmes to continue in office until the 9th day of Novem- ber. 1899. He added that he would not allow any dis- cussion on that subject at all. They had simply got to fill their voting papers and hand them up. Councillor HaPIIIIRD moved and Councillor CANNELL seconded that Alderman Francomb take the chair. Ciuncillor DAVIRS rose to speak beton' the 114yor had left the chair, and the latter told him that he was out of order. Councillor Devhcs: But you are not in the chair. The MAYOR : Yes. I am. Councillor DAVIRS asked to sp,ak as a matter of cour- tesy as it would probably be the last time his voice would be beard _in that Chamber.</p>
        <p>rhP MAYOR: You are out of order. If I allow you to sleak I ‘hall haw to allow others to speak. I shall not allow you to sneak.</p>
        <p>Councillor DAVIRS still remained standing and again attempting to speak the Mayor repeated that he was out of order and told him to sit down. Alderman ElAnarscri : Suppose I feel it necessary to move that this question be adjourned natil the next Council meeting, will that he out of order The MAYOR: That is a question for t e Town Clerk. Alderman HARRISON : I shall then require to be heard.</p>
        <p>Councillor DAVIES : I won't make a speech; lam siawlv going to ask a question. Surely a councillor ought to be able to ask a question.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: You are out of order. Counci•lor Clams Not to aqk a queqtion, The MAYOR : I have app,ded to the Foam Clerk. CunciUor Dawns : It that is the rulin!r of the Town Clerk lam sorry. - - -</p>
        <p>The Tows Culair : I have ruted that no discussion can take place on the election of an alderman. I cannot say whether your question iq out ot order because I have not heard it.</p>
        <p>Coun. DAVITS said his question was whrther they had not a right to say individua.iv why they gave their vote. The MAYOR: Celt:4llß, fro-. Councillor DAVIZS Tht nit is very unfair.</p>
        <p>. I . —= —= =7+ - ffi 111 I"I 11 11 11111 11 1 111111 rs</p>
