      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>WARRINGTON TJWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A LIVELY MEETING.-ELECTION OF AN ALDERMAN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evvning the monthly meeting of this Council was held in the Council Chambor at the TIMA Hall. The Mayor (Councillor Fairclough) presided, and there were also present—Aldermen Harri! on, Wriuht, Sutton, Francomb,Hutchinson ; Councillors Hy.Rob.rts, Sbavr-Green, Plumpton, Cannel], Smethurst, Bennett, Evans. Davies, Pierpoint, Ellis, Duncan, Furness, Tin- nion, Rogers,Woods, Monks, Baird, Shillcock.Wiikinson, Longton, Hepherd, Heaton. Shaw, with Messrs. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), S. Wilson (denuts-town clerk), J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer). Dr. Gorn.ll (medical officer), J. Deas (sanitary engineer), Sutcliffe (deputy-borough surveyor). There was a large attend- ance of the general public. THE RESIGNATION OF ALDERMAN HOLMES.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said he had received a communication from Alderman Holmes. It was as follows :—" 98, Bewsey read, Warrington, 30th September, 1896. J. Lyon Whittle, Esq., town clerk of Warrington. Dear Sir,—For some time past I had been in hopes of being able to resume my place in the Warrington Town Coun- cil, but as that now seems impossible the only course I can adopt is to resign iv seat in the Council. I enclose one shilling with that object. In thus separating my- self from the members of the Council, with whom I have always had most friendly relations, I desire to thank them for the many acts of kindness I have received at their hands.—Yours faithfully, Cgas. J. Horztes." The Mayor added that they were all sorry to lose Alderman Holmes who had served the Council a good many years. He moved "That this Council receives with regret the resignation of Charles John Holmes, Esq., hen. freeman of the borough, of the office of alderman, and desire to place on record their warm appreciation of the eminent and valuable public services rendered by him during his connection with the Town Council for upwards of 40 years." Alderman HARRISON seconded the motion. Alderman Surrots supported the motion And said they all regretted the circumstances which compe led Alder- man Isolmes to retire after a long service of something like 40 years. At the same time he might say that that was not the first occasion on which they had had the: resignation placed before them, and he thought that if it had not been planed before them previously they would not be in the difficult position he felt they were in that night. Councillor Ronnars also had great pleasure in sup- porting that motion as oee who had known Mr. Halmes ever since boyhood. It could never be anything hut a matter of regret when a man had worn himself nut by years of service and was compelled to give up office on that account. He did not see the dilemma in which that Council was placed. He did not wish to anticipate what might he done later on,but it seemed to him that that Council had distinctly on those occaeione settled for itself its order of nroeseitere,and any departure from that order of procedure would be s;mply giving the lie to what had been done OR se many previous occasions. (Hear, hear.) The motion wee then nut and carried, and the Mayon declared the office held by Alderman Holmes to be vacant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>TEN WIDENTNO OF BRIDOE-STRIIET,</head>
        <p>TEN WIDENTNO OF BRIDOE-STRIIET,</p>
        <p>Councillor SHAW GREEN, in moving the adoption of the minutes of the Finance and Estate Committee, said that the application for borrowing p,wers which tney intended to make to the Local Grivernment Board was in order to buy the property between Friars' Gata and Itylands-street for the widening of Brilge-atreet. They were acquiring that property as rapidly as circumstance would allow.</p>
        <p>Councillor L')NGTON seconded, and the minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>STREET IMPROVEMENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON and Alderman WRIGHT seconded the confirmati-n of these minutes.</p>
        <p>Councillor Berm Err complained that the Counsil had not ot the elan showing the line of frontage on the north-east side of St. James-street and Knutsford-road, which the committee proposed should be "signed and deposited." Councillor SMETHURST asked if it was not possible for the committee to take down a portion of the building in Horsemarket-street. He had seen twelve conveyances in that part of the street and two carriages were locked together. _ _</p>
        <p>- _ Councillor Prxxrrow noticed that plans had been passed for a movable room in the drill-ahed yard, and he wished to know whether it was intended for a com- mittee roma in connection with the election in Town Hall ward. (Rear, hear and laughter.) Alderman Surrotr, in reply, said he did not know any- thing about the moveable room or whether refreshments would be provided thera en the occasion referred to. (Laughter.) The minutea were confirmed. IRON VERSUS WOODEN PAILS.</p>
        <p>Councillor MONKS, in moving the confirmation of the minutes of the Sanitary Works Committee, referred to the tender of Messrs. Parkidsou and Swasner, to supply 100 galvanized steel pails at 5.. each and 64 galvanized covers at Os. 9d. each, and said the pails would be much cheaper and easier cleaned than the wooden ones. Councillor Snot asked how it was that the cost of these Pails had rigAn to lls. 91. when Councillor Monks said on the 25rb Mtrch that they would be 98. 6d. Alderman HARRISON asked whether the iron pails would be as dnrable as those made of wood. Councillor MONKS, in replr,said be had no recollection of ever saving that the cost of these pails would be 93. 61. As to the durability of the pails, the clmmittee had had no experience themselves, but they knew that many other towns had discarded the wooden ones in favour of those made of galvanised iron. Besides.they only proposed to buy 100 nails as an experiment. The minutes of the committee were then adopted. NIGLICTED STREETI.</p>
        <p>Alderman Watear moved the confirmation of the minu'es of the Paving and Sewerage Committee. Connelllor RonnaTs called attention to the deplorable conditinn of Mill-lane and some other streets in White- cress ward. The state of these streets had been brought under hi' notice in consequence of his inverviewing the constituents of that ward, and he was only sorry that his attention had not be called to the matter earlier. The MAYOR thotlght it would be better if a complaint honk were provided so that the chairman of the Com- mittees might amazer these queNtioes in the Council. It would save the salting of a lot of questions. Alderman WRIORT, in reply, said he quite endorsed what Cnimeillor Roberta had said, but the Committee kad always acted on the principle that when a street wax built upon both sides they would attend to it.</p>
        <p>The minutes werr then adopted. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE. In movinv the adoption of the minutes of this com- mittee. Councillor SznAw Gnxxx mentioned that on the Thursday previ Nis 210 boy!' and girls sat in an examina- tion for lshour certificates. There would be another exarninaion towirde the end of November, and this year thern wont 1 h. at least 500 scholars examined in- stead of about 300 as in the past.</p>
        <p>TRH TRCHNICAL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBRRTS, on moving the minutes of thia Museum and Teehnical Instruction Committee, referred to the free a4m;aginn to the School of Art offered to all plipita of elementary schools who obtained cards of merit, and said the results of the experiment in that direction w.rp cr proirely satisfactory that he thought the entinsil wmld folly endorse the policy of the CORI.' mittee in earrvi+off it qtiti further. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>ttlon nnnfirrned. - THE ti,TwR ATIONS AT THE CEMETERY.</p>
        <p>AirmnlAßßlSWifl meving the confirmation of the Cemetery r, Imm;t'e 's minutes referred to the criticisms passeri ',erne t;me aye en the additional fractures erected et the Cometr.r. FP maid he believed that the new, erections and the improvements made would meet with nubile sympathy an ! r-snect. end he hoped they would have no more idlinz faults found with the additions made. Conn. EVANS seconded.</p>
