      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>Coun. BENsirrr said that unless some considerable al- terations had been made since he last saw ths new erec- tion at the Cemetery he must adhere to his opinions ex- pressed at the previous meeting. At one end of the lodize they had put imitation stone and with the red bricks they had placed velliw bricks. (Laughter.) They had therefore three colours. The MAYOR : You are out of order, and you know it as well as I dn. Councillor BENNETT : With all respect to the chair. Alderman Harrison has introduced the subject. The MAYOR: You ought to bring it up in a proper shape and form. Councillor BENNETT said he would like to point out that instead nf making the Cemetery lodge better, tney had made it worse by the alteration. If the committee wanted to make a good job of the work they should have made the section in unison with the one interfered with, and some approach to architectural taste. Alderman Scrrron thought it was a great mistake,after a building was completed, to make complaints. They should have been made when the building was in course of erection. Councillor BENNErr : That is exactly what I did. Alderman SUTTON continuing his remarks said mem. bers of the Council seemed to leave everything to the monthly meeting. o,lllleillni ROBERTS (interrupting) said he would like t 3 ask whether Alderman Sutton was in order? The Mayon said he was informed that the accounts of the Cemetery Cominittc,e included those for the new erection. and the speakers were therefqre in order. The minutes were passed wihout further discussion, -</p>
        <p>THE COST OF ALTERING BANIc; HOUSE. Councillor HEATON proplied the confirmation of the Town Hall, Park and R iths Cemmittees' mimuttss which recommended that the tender of Mr. R W. Collin to erect a new entrance doorway at Bank House and repair the Enntits at £29 ho accepted ; also that two rooms in Rank Howse be fitted up for the use of the Medical Offiner of Health. Councillor ROBERTS wished to ask the deputy-chairman of the committee if he would give the Council a detailed statement of the expenditure incurred \on Bank Home. Instead of spending the money on an enlargement of the Town Hall, which would have been s permanent struc- ture and worth looking upon for all time, they were frittering their money away on a structure which in a few years would h- inadequate for the purposes intended, in order to gratify the whims of certain persons who did not know what business was. Councillor WOODS said he objected very strongly to the £5OO grant to the " rid-raddting " of that old house. The committee had undorminied the house, spent all their :rionev. and now wknted money for a new front door. He thought it was :in abominable waste of money. The nroperty was bought for one phrpose and that was for street imnrovement. nameiv,the widening of Sankey- street to the lite, of frontage of the Town Hall gates. He pretested aeaiest this Riffling away of money on altera- tions of Bank Rouse. He thought it was time a stop was put to it. He moved that the minutes of the com- mittee he passed with the exception of those relating to Rank Rouse. Councillor BENNETT seconded. CoUnCilhT LONGTON thought many of them were anxious to know hew much had been spent in the direction referred te,but the matter was a simple one. The MAYOR said the speaker was only wasting time be referring to the matter again. Councillor LONGTON said the &amp;lent of the amendment was transnarent enough, and was to get at the expense of the alterations. Re rose for the purpose of sugetest- ing to the Chairman that he should at ogee as a business man and one fully aware of what members wet%) entitled to, give an aaeuranee that the return mentioned should be furnished. The Council had no intention of prevent- ing the Medical Offer of Health having his rooms. They wanted to know the expenditure en the house, and that only. •</p>
        <p>" The MAYOR : The R,ynuoh Treasursr can give you that. He save that £7OO has been expended. Councillor WOOM : Oily £530 was asked for. That is very good. Councilbr Suaw•GnEvaa wishNi to know why the ernnmittee were nroviding two special rooma tor the Medical Officer. Ho (Council! 1r Shaw-Green) thought the Medical Offieer WAR admirahlv suited in the present He did nnt see the necessity of spending any mnre money for other rooms in annth,r house. Conneillor Mum.: : The Medical Officer is not suited. Councillor DAVIES said that everyone who had seen the Medical OFfieer of Health in his prea,ant offices must have felt sorry for him. (Laughter.) His 3es were situated in a damn cellar a. the 133ttom of that old house, and everyone who went to see him either on busi- ness or pleasure must have felt sorry for him. The place was not fit for any gentleman to have as an office Red he was surprised at the remark of C3uncillor Shaw Green. Councillor SHAW GUMS said he had never heard in that Council Chamber or (Alta it that any objection was raised by the MedicalVficer to his present office or from anyone on his hnhalf. Elm was surnrisall to hear that they ware not suitable, but he was prepared to believe what Councillor Davies said—(Coun. Davies: "Thank you "—laughter). Councillor PLITIIPTON said heWnuld like to ask why the receipts were only half what they used to be at the Baths? _ _ _</p>
        <p>- --- - The Mayor: Last rear we had exceptionally fine weether ; this rasa% it is exceptionally wet. Councillor HEATON said the reason for the fall in the receipts at the Baths was that they had not got as many ha,therm. With re.. ard to the expenditure on Bank Meuse, he could only say that the estimates had been before the Council and nagged without a word of coin- plaint. He did not see why the members now com- plained. Councillor ROBRRTS WO are not complaining; we are asking for information. Cetincillor ARATON (continning) said the Council asked the committee to make the alterationa.end he contended that they had done the hest they could. The committee had Provided offi-ea that would he suitable for the next 20 or 25 year...and that would be quite as coon as the Council would he prepared to extend the Town Hall. With respect to tha door which they intended to replace in Bank Rouse, it was completely rotten. Councillor Noma r With a knocker on. (Laughter.) Councillor HnoTox : I can tell when a piece of timber is rotten as wall as the man that soya it isn't. (Loud laughter.) Whoever said that a rood jlb had not been made of the offices, knew nothing whatever about it. (Laughter.) The minutes were then confirmed. MISCELLANEOUS.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the followine Committaes were adopted without dieenecion :—Wateh Committee, Health Com- mittee, Market' rommittee, Gas Committee, Fire Engine Committee, Water Comm;ttee, and General Pur- poses Committee.</p>
        <p>THE MANCIUSTER SEWAGE SCHEME.—THE DUTY OF THE CORPOR kTION WITI RAGARD TO IT. Councillor ROBERTS moved the confirmation of the minutes of the Rivers Committen erhinh recommended that the Town Clerk and Borough Surveyor prepare a re- port on the Manchester S.:wage Disnoql S:,heme. "That the Manchester Shin Canal Company be urged to pro- ceed with the dredging of the river and the construction of the proposed nenstock,under the agreements with the Corporation." In referring to the Mancheater sewage disposal scheme he said the committee hal instructed the Town Clerk Riad Rorrugh Surveyor to prepare a re- port, and they would then come to the Council with some suggestions in regard to it. He was unable to anticipate those suggestions hut he could only sayithat the scheme was pregnant with the most tremendous issues as affecting Warrington. He could not prophesy what the result would be, hut he could say this, from a superficial acquaintance with the proposals of the Man- cheater Corporation, that it would take all the combined wisdom of that. Council and all the profes4lonal aid it could invoke of the highest and most qualifisd character to enable the Corporation to steer clear of the difficulty which they might he involved in if they did not take the right course in regard to those proposals. With re- gard to the second resolution on the minutes, he might gar they had urged the Ship Canal Company to proceed with the dredging of the river and the construction of the nenstock.—The company had since commenced the dredging, and they hoped that in a short time the con-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>COLEMAN'S WINvIRNIS. COLEMAN'S WINCARNIS. 4000 MEDICAL MEN hay. sent Tes'irnoniale in its favour. Sold in Bottles 2s. 9d. and 3s. 6d. everywhere.</p>
