      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>The MAYOR: If any man has promised Councillor Roberts to vote for him let him vote for him—he will not I offend me.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS said he hoped that,whatever might be the resuit of that vote, it would not make any differ- ence in the personal friendship which had existed between him and the Mayor. Ile MAYOR: Not at all. I may say that I have not been interviewed by anyboly ; it is only that I object to the way the thing has been done. Councillor PIRRPOINT hoped that Alderman liarrison would not move the adjournment of the matter. (Hear, heir.) Councillor WOODS: Let Alderman Francomb take the chair.</p>
        <p>Councillor PIERPOINT : But ha is the casting-vote alderman. (Llughter.) _</p>
        <p>Alderman FRANCOMB : Yes, and he will give it if he gers the chance. (Laughter.) The MAYOR (leaving the chair) : I only wish to ask Councill lr Roberts it he is gong to vote for himself P Councillor ROBERTS: I am, Mr. Mayor. The MAYOR: Very well; I only wish to know for my own guidance. Alderman Faaarcosts then teok the chair and asked the numbers of the Council to hand up their voting papers. The papers were rapidly delivered, and after they had been all received the Chairman declared the voting to be as follows :</p>
        <p>For the Mayor :—Aldermen Sutton, Wright, Hutchin- son, and Francomb ; Councillors Lse, Evans, Shillcock, Shaw, Cannel!, Bair!, HAoherd, Longton, Heaton,Smet- burst, and Fairclough-15.</p>
        <p>Fur Coun(;ilior Roberts :—Alderman Harrison ; Coun- cillors Monks, Wls,Pierp Hat, Davies, Furness,Ellison, Shaw-Greenjinnion, Plumpton, Duncan, Wilkinsen,and Roberts-13.</p>
        <p>The CKAIRMAN said that as thirteen had voted for Councillor Roberts and fifteen for Mr. Fairclough. he declared Councillor James Fairelough an alderman ot the borough. (Applause.) He added that one unsigned voting paper had been banded in. The two members of the Council present who did not vote were Councillors Bennett and Rogers. The Mayor then again took the chair. _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS said : Mr. Mayor, I thiak that, perhaps, under the circumstances, I may say a few words,and I premise the Council that they shall be very brief. some time ago—a few years ago new—it was decided by the dominant party in this Corporation that the principle of seniority should from that time apply to the election of an alderman. (Hear, hear.) That de- cision was confirmed en a second and on a third occa- sion. Balieving that it was the intention of the domin- ant party to carry out honourably and to the letter the precedent which they had formed, the other members of the Corporation behoving to the party to which I have the honour to belong felt in with that pro.:edent, and supported it loyally. On the three "CCISIOLIB on which that precedent was adopted it happeued as an accident that the gontlemee elected all belonge.l to the parts' which proposed the precedent. This is the first occasion a Conservative majority has had al opportunity of show- ing how far it intended to be loyal to the precedent which it had itself adopted. I am sorry that it has not been found convenient and in accordance with the exi- gencies of the Conservative party to carry out the pre- cedent. but I am hound to take this opportu- nity of publiely aclrnowledging my indebtedness to Aldermal Harrison and Councillors Davies and Woods for not having allowed themselves to be dragged into an unfair and dishonourable proceed- ing—(applause)—that they have shown that it was possible, for gentlemen wbo ar,.. as opposed to me on grounds which do not come into anything referring to the management of municipal business as strongly as one man can be opposed to another, and they thus recognize the fact that the precedent to which I have alluded was adopted They have been loyal to their opinion, and hava loyally carried it out. I have only this to say, Mr. Mayor, th it I have been loyal to you as the mayor of this borough—as lcyal as any man couli be. I have supported you on every possible occasion,and I have always considered that you were—and I hope yet to consider you—a man of the highest honour and in- tegrity. My only 'regret is that rota have been the in- strument of inflicting upon vac the gross injustice which has been inflicted upon me. (flear,hear, and applause; and cries of " Ni, _no," and " Order, order.") The ?Mount : Order, plea.% Alderman WRIOXT : I won't sit and listen to that sort of nonsenee.</p>
        <p>The+ Mane: Let him have his say; it won't du me any harm. . _ _</p>
        <p>Alderman WRIGET persisted, however, in protesting against what Councillor Roberts was saying, and he and two or three others left the Council Chamber amidst some lerisive laughter and cries of" sit still." Councillor ammeters: I can quite understand that what I have said is not parlcularly comforting to the gentlemen who have gone cot of the room ; I did not intend it should be. but I am here to-night simply to say this—that I belting to a race that for centures, both in regard to religion and politics and nationality,has been made to suff,r, and I should be false to the traditions of the people I am proud to call myself one of if I did not feel that to some extent I have also to pay the price for the opinions I hold. (Lou I applause amongst tbe public at the end of the 1.N:0n.) But Ido fail to see what on earth questions of imperial policy have to do with municipal government. (Rear, bear.) I have tried my level beat anti more especially during the last three years to give all the p mer I have and what little ability lam endowed with to work for the benefit of Warrington as a whole irrespective of party and per- sonal interest. lam prepared to continue that work. Whether it shall ever be within the province of the Cor- poration to give me a seat which is considered more honourable than the one I now hold I care very little. A man who works solely for the sake of honours of that kind is not the man to talc. up a position in public life. (Applause.) And what I have tried to do up to new has met with the confidence and support of men in this community who allow themselves to see things in the light in which they exist, and de not allow themselves to be blinded by mere party prejudices. (Applause, more especially from the spectators at the hack of the room).</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON then roar to speak saying that he could ant in justice to himself and the Mayor.-- The MAYOR: You are nut of order. I will say what I have to say for myself. Alderman Strirrox still persisted in attempting to opeak,but there were loud =ie. of " Order, order "and Chair,chair " and a gener4l hubbub. Alderman Strrrox,on obtaining a chance to speak,eai he wished to Rive an explanatiln. The MAYOR: If I allow you I must allow othc.ra. Councillor PIERPODIT : I claim an opportunity to speak if Alderman Ston sneaks. The MAYOR lam tot !Ding' to allow that.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON Atilt persisted, but was warmly told by the Mayor to " sit down," and there were more cries of" sit down" and " Chair,_chair."_</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON: This-is insulting. The MAYOR then said : I thank you for the honour yea have done me in making me an alderman of the borough. I should not have dreamt of being an alder- man if it had not been for the way in which things have gone on No one round the table had a notice sent to him that the aldermanic seat was vacant,and a hole and corner meeting WAR held v:ithout me knowing anything about it. Erpe since Warrington was incorporated all past Msyors have had th.. aldertninship conferred upon them when there was a vscancv,and the only time when that was not done was when my brether was Mayor two years, and then they pitchforrad Alderman Davies. and ne:resigned his Neat on tht: Council. Some farther attempts to speak were made but the Mayor ruled that he could allow no one else to speak. Councillor Davin : That is most unfair Mr. Mayor. Yon make a speech and refuse to allow us to reply. The meeting which had during the election of an alderman been very excited then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>RAILWAY COMPANIES AND THE</head>
        <p>THE RAILWAY COMPANIES AND THE</p>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>THE THREATENED COMPS TITION OF MOTORS. Respecting the competing interests of the Manchester ship Canal and those of the leading railway companies taking goods traffie to Liverpol, a correspondent of a Manchester contemporary who has interviewed the chief officials of one of the comp inies concerned says that a. new element has come into play—viz the electric mo- tor vehicles whme use will he auth rised neat month. The advent of this means of traction for the carriage of goods is seriously engaging the attention of the railway managers. Apart from the tihip Canal conveniences and advaotage,, the eatablishment of plateways for ve- hicles propelled by electric power is now regarded by the railway experts as a probable development. No con- ference of railway managers has so far been arranged, but correspondence is gning on which will doubtless lead to a review of the whole position as aff-cting the canal authorities, the railways, and the projectors of new schemes to benefit the disturbed trade of Liverpool. Unti; the effent nf the new aystem of traction has been fully tested the railway companies are not likely, au 8 the correspondent, to do anything in the way of altering their rates.</p>
      </div>
