        <p>_ those Continental - Pen to conviction tb:i d i 3 Rushed forwar</p>
        <p>purely humanitarian sentiments of the people of this country and not by any sel- fish diplomatic reasons. That is in itself a tremendous gain, for so long as it was believed that England was acting upon selfish motives there was a danger that the slightest move on our part might bring about those European complications of which Lord ROSEBERY, has expressed so much fear. That the agitation has accomplished some useful results is seen by the changed tone of the foreign, and especially the Russian, press in its comments upon the Eastern question. The change has, indeed, been so marked, that there seems now to be a probabil- ity of joint action at Constantinople in applying the necessary coercion or threat of coercion upon the Sultan. The agitation in which Lord ROSE- BURY has not taken why part and has apparently discountenanced, has, in a word, so far distinctly justified itself. As to Lord ROSEBERY' s resigna- tion of the leadership of the Liberal Party there will undoubtedly be some differences of opinion. Many people will no doubt think that the want of harmony between leader and followers had not reached a point which called for this resignation. Every one, however, will agree that there is nothing to find fault with in either the tone or phrasing of Lord ROSEBERY'S letter. It is a plainly-worded and straightforward document. Lord ROSEBERY feels that he is not in harmony upon one important subject with a large mass of the Liberal Party and he wishes to be in a posi- tion of perfect freedom to express his opini ons. Ele obtains this freedom by means of his resigna- tion, and the country will be prepared to give the fullest consideratior. to the opinions that he sets forth in his speech at Edinburgh.</p>
        <p>THE Czar has now exchanged the The Cz9.r in quietness of Balmoral for the Paris. noisy festivities of Paris. The "stern and simple" republicans of France appear to have almost lost their heads in welcoming the most autocratic monarch in Europe. The enthu- slam of the French for their imperial visitor is something that will not readily be forgotten by those who have seen it. They have flocked into Paris in such numbers that it has been found almost impossible to provide accommodation for all the visitors. The hotels are, it is said, packed from their " sky parlours" to their dining room tables, and every unlet hall has been turned into a dormitory. Hundreds, if not thousands, of the more hardy and less wealthy provincials have spent a night or two in the open air, and in order to provide for their wants special licenses were granted to the cafes and restaurants to keep their doors open all night. There has, too, actually been a danger of a partial famine. The million or ix{ of extra people who have been suddenly added to the normal population of the gay capital have taxed to the utmost the resources of what may be described as the victualling department of the city. Some of the more necessary articles of food have run up to extraordinary prices and retail dealers have reaped a fine harvest. The owners of .win- dews and other points of vantage on the main routes have been asking high prices from those who wished to have a good view of the high function- aries, and one window it is said was let at the rate 'of £l2O a day. All the accounts in fact go to prove that the French are celebrating the CZAR'S visit with something like a frenzy of enthusiasm. But after all there is some method in this mad- ness, and it would be a mistake to put all the ex- citement down to a spirit of mere flunkeyism. It is in some respects an expression of gratitude, especially if we accept the cynical definition that gratitude is" a lively sense of favours to come." The French are not without hopes that the CZAR nk ay Yet help them to recover the two provinces Which were taken from them by the Germans in 1870. And in any case, an alliance with Russia vastly strengthens the bands of France in her dealings with foreign countries and makes her feel more secure from attacks by any of her neigh- bours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>LOCAL FRIENDLY SOCEETIES.</head>
        <p>LOCAL FRIENDLY SOCEETIES.</p>
        <p>BENEVOLENT STAR LODGE.</p>
        <p>At the fortnightly meeting of tn 3 Benevolent Star Lodge No. 449 held at the White Bull, Eforsemarket-st., on Saturday a prosentation of an emblem of the Order to Bro. Green a past officer was made. Brother Richardson made the prasentation and in a short speech expressed the hope that Bro. Green would long be spared to help the cause of Oddfellowship. Bro. Green in accepting the presentation remarked that the work he had done in the past he would always be willing to again perform and he would do all in his power to help the lodge and the order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>LOYAL ORDER OF ANCIENT SHEPHERDS.</head>
        <p>LOYAL ORDER OF ANCIENT SHEPHERDS.</p>
        <p>- • t The quarterly meeting of the Warrington dis ric was held on Saturday at the Sons of the Mersey Lodge (Little Horse Shoe Inn, Horsemarket-street), under the presid- ency of the district chief, Bro. William Eckersley. He was supported by the deputy chief, Bro. Henry Warbur- ton, Bros. James Parr (treasurer), Peter Edgar (secre- tary), Bennion (trustee), and the delegates from the several lodges connected with such district. The Chair- man opened the meeting with a few appropriate words of welcome, and after the "Roll call " had been made,he called upon the District Secretary to read the quarterly accounts. The statement showed that the past three months had been, from a financial point of view, a most 'successful period ; again the funeral claims had been extremely light. After paying all claims and other expenses a substantial sum had been placed to the funeral fund, raising that fund to over £4OO. Messrs. Eirkham and Massey gave the result of their audit, 'which was of a flattering nature, and on the proposition of the senior auditor the same were passed. After other business had been gene through Bro. Gleave was called upon by the chairman to present Bro. T. C, Duckworth with a handsome timepiece. Bro. Gleave said he did so with great pleasure. Bro. Duckworth, he stated, had gone thrmigh the office with credit both to himself and to the district, and be had performed the duties of the office in an admirable manner. He had al- ways acted straightforwardly, and his conduct had been most impartial while presiding over their deliberations. He trusted that he. his wife and family might be long spared to enjoy the pleasure of looking upon the time- piece, and that it would remind him of the marry friends and the pleasant hours he had spent amongst them. Bro. James Parr remarked that he had known Bro. Duckworth for many years and could vouch that he de served all that had been said of him. Bros. Haley.Kirk- ham, Bennion, Miller, F. Taylor. and Wilkinson, also spoke bearing their testimony to the work of Bro. Duck- worth. Bro. Duckworth,in reply, said their knd words and expressions had completely nonplussei him. From the bottom of his heart he heartily thanked them for their handsome present. He thanked Bro. Gleave and the others who had spoken for their good wishes, and the delegates for the enthusiastic manner in which those remarks had been received. The present he valued very 'Much, and assured them that it would occupy a very Prominent position in his house. He had tried to do his 'duty. The meeting shortly afterwards terminated.</p>
        <p>Fever Hospital, Aikin-st.,</p>
        <p>. . Warrington. Report for the month ending 30th September, 1896. In hospital on the Ist of September Admitted during the month— Cases of scarlet fever (7 Warrington 22 Newton) 29 Cases of enteric fever 2</p>
        <p>_ Total 4B</p>
        <p>Discharried cured Dean " In hospital on the Ist of October (9 Warrington 28 Newton) 37</p>
        <p>_ Total</p>
        <p>JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Superintendent.</p>
        <p>e"ntiny's Cocos is both a stimulating and refresh- th and s nutritious food, probably more nourishing " ttrtv other beverage. It is nbrolutel3 pure, containing -retZn admixture.-4dt4</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>THE LIBRARY LECTURE.</p>
        <p>We would remind our readers that the Free Library Committee lectures commence on Monday, when a lec- ture will be given on " Thiee famous books." Further particulars will be seen in our advertising columns. "VISIT TO BELGIUM AND HOLLAND."</p>
        <p>" A visit to Belgium and Holland" is th. 3 title of a lec- ture to be given by Mr. E. M. Sneyd Bynnersley, H.M. Inspector of Schools, on Friday evening next,in the Vic- toria-road Board schools. Mr. Kynnersley has travelled a great deal in and about Holland and his lecture will no doubt prove very interesting. The proceeds are to be utilised, in providing prizes for the students of the continuation schools. ACCIDENT AT ROPE WORKS.</p>
        <p>An accident befel John Sharkey,aged 15 years, Water- street, whilst following his employment on Wednesday at the Rope Works,Top Locks, by the" carriage " pass— ing over his thigh. Dr. Delaney was soon in attendance and the young man is progressing favourably. MR. JUDE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday —evening Mr. W. H. Jude of Liverpool, gave a musical evening,entitled "Musical reminiscences" in the Public Hall. Mr. Jude's performances on the piano and harmonium were excellent. A large audience was present,and the proceeds are to be handed over to St. John John's Pres!), terian Church fund. FOOT RACE.</p>
        <p>William Moorrs,and itWh-C-Ta-ye,both of Runcorn,took part in half a mile foot race. on the ground of the Run- corn Football Club, oh Wednesday night. Both men started from scratch,and Clays want off leading by about 20 yards. Moores kept some distance behind his oppon- ent until the last lap,whea he easily drew level, aid won by about 28 yards. The race was for a silver cup valued £2O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>RUNCORN FOOTBALL. CLUB</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FOOTBALL. CLUB</p>
        <p>-.- A telegram was received at the club's headquarters on Widnesday night that Houghton (Runcorn) and Barber (Widnes) had been suspended, the former a fortnight and the latter a month for fighting Gil the Widnes field during the match Runcorn v. Widnes, at Widnes, on Saturday. A NEW POOR RATE FOR RUNCORN. At the Runcorn Police Court on Wednesday, Mr. G. L. Davies and J. W. Lightburn, affixed their signatures to a new poor-rate for Runcorn, at is. 6d. in the £, estim- ated to realise £4,373 93. 3d. The new rate, which com- pares with the shilling rate for the past six months and is. 2d. for the corresponding period last year, is the heaviest which Runcorn has had during the past quar- ter of a century. TRAP ACCIDENT NEAR RUNCORN. . . . .</p>
        <p>A trap accident occurred on Saturday night between Moore and Runcorn. Mr. Edwin Tuckey, hay and corn merchant, and Mr. William Hankinson, mineral water manufacturer, both of Runcorn, had been visiting some friends at Preston Brook, and on their return when ap- proaching Norton their gig overturned at the edge of a deep ditch. Mr. Tuckey succeeded in getting free, but all his efforts to release Mr. Hankinson failed. At the expiration of an hour,however,the latter was released by the help of a man who was passing. A neighbouring farmer placed a shasdry at the parties' disposal, and Runcorn was reached after a most unpleasant adventure. Mr. Tuckey escaped scatheless, but his companion re- ceived several severe bruises. SEAMEN'S INSTITUTE. RUNCORN,</p>
        <p>The church was nicely decorated last evening (Thursday) the occasion being the annual harvest festival. Large quantities of flowers, fruit and veget- ables,to whic air. G. Percival, of Parkside Dairy Green- way-road, Runcorn,had so generously contributed, were seen adorning the walls. Several models of flats and a full rigged ship were also in prominent positions. The Rev. H. W. L. O'Rourke conducted the service and preached an appropriate sermon. The decorators were Mesdames Abel, Cooke, Shaw, Bowyer, Mack, North, Shaw, Coward, Price, Randle and Abel. RUNCORN AND THE ARMENIAN ATROCITIES.—SUGGESTED</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>TOWNS MNISTING.</head>
        <p>TOWNS MNISTING.</p>
        <p>A memorial signed by a number of the clergy and ratepayers of the town was sent to Mr. J. W. Light- burn, chairman of the Runsorn Urban District Council, asking him to call a town's meetiug, for the purpose of condemning the atrocities in irmenia. At the conclu- sion of the Health Committee on Wednesday. Mr. Light. burn consulted with some ef his colleagues upon the subject, and it was decided not to call a meeting. Mr. Lightburn has, however, sent a copy of a resolution to each clergyman, requesting them, if they so desired, to have it passed by their respective congregations en Sunday. ASSAULT WITH A POKER AT DINCORN.</p>
        <p>--- Mary Clark, Surrey-st., Runcorn. was charged at the Runcorn Petty Sessions, on Monday, with assaulting Mary Clark on the 29th September.—Mary Clark said on the date named she went to the defendant's house in reference to her daughter who had been using bid lan- guage. Mrs. Clark came out of her house and struck her with a poker and blackened her arm.—Mrs. Billing- ton, a neighbour, said she heard a noise, and saw the defendant strike:Abe cmplainant. A number of other witnesses were called in support of the complainant's case.—At this stage a summons was heard against Mary Ryan for assaulting Mary Ann Clark on the 29 ult. The evidence called showed that Mrs. Ryan had the com- plainant by the hair of the head on the floor and was thrashing her. A number of witnesses were called.—The magistrates bound Clark over in the sum of £5 to keep the peace for six months and to pay the costs, or in default seven days. The charge against Ryan was dismissed.</p>
        <p>THE REV. DR. GASTALDI AND THE SULTAN. -</p>
        <p>At St. Edward's Roman Catholic Chapel, on Sunday, the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi preaching en the festival of the rosary, alluded to the fact that, on that very Sunday, a great victory was won against the Turks, at Lepanto, in 1571, through the intercession of our Blessed Lady. Pope Pius the 6th instituted a feast in memory of this event, which was confirmed by Pope Gregory XIII,in 1573 un- der the title of our Lady of the Rosary. In like manner WAS the victory over the Forks won, by Prince Eugene of Savoy, at Belgrade, in 1716. At that time, the Turks with an army of two hundred thousand men, held the Christian Army, as it were besieged near Belgrade. The Isle of Corfu was also beleaguered by forty thousand Mahommedans. The victory of the Christians was fol- lowed by the taking of Belgrade, and the deliverance of Corfu, and also the preservation of all Germany and Italy, which were next threatened by the Turks. In those days the Turks wished to exterminate all the Christians, and Europe might in this very century be Mahommedan, had not the crusaders and the noble Templars, performed their duties, by checking the Turk- ish inv .mion. To-day they were face to face with an- other impudent attack by the " great assassin," against the Christian Armenians. It was time, that the Powers of Europe awoke, and brought pressure on the successor of Mahomet. and told him to stop bis evil deeds. RUNCORN CONGREGATIONALISTS AND THE ARMINIAN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>ATROCITIES.</head>
        <p>ATROCITIES.</p>
        <p>A large congregail;—;:ded at the Runcorn Bethesda Congregational Church, on Sunoay, to hear a discourse by the It.v. J. S. Simpson on "The Cry of Armenia : are we to listen to it." The rev, preacher in the course of his remarks, said that the question was more than a nolitical or a national one—it was one of humanity. It was the most fearful tale of wrong, the ghastly record of oppression, in the century now hastening to a close. Not only had villages and towns been devastated, but men, women, and children bad been put cruelly to death, and submitted to tortures from which the heart recoiled. After comdemning the powers, especially Great Britain, for not taking action. the rev, gentleman submitted the following resolution : —" That this congregation hereby expresses its pro- found detestation of the policy of massacre pursusd by the Sultan of Turkey, against his Armenian subjects. It views with shame and dismay the inactivity of the great powers of Europe, in view of a long continued cruelty, to which the Armenians have been exposed. It appeals to Her Majesty's Government to take such measures as may be necessary to prevent the repetition of such atrocities,and to deliver the Armenian people from cruel and abominable misrule." The congregation unanimously rose in assent to the proposition. A copy of the resolution has been forwarded to Lord Salisbury, Lord Rosebery, and to Sir J. T. Brunner, M.P.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>STORMY WEATHER AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THE STORMY WEATHER AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A BOAT CAPSIZED. NARROW ESCAPES.</p>
        <p>About six o'clock on Thursday morning,a boat named "Stag " registered number 98, and weight 20 tons, and belonging to Anderton and Co., Runcorn, capsized in the cut,cpposite the large warehouse. The boat with its occupants, Stephen and George Littlemore, arrived in Runcorn the previous night from the Potteries (Hanley) about six o'clock, loaded with 12 tons 17 cwt of crates for shipment. The boat wise fastened up, near to the warehouse, and the brothers shortly afterwards retired to rest. On awakening on Thursday morniog, about 5.45, they were sin prised to see the boat had drifted, and was gradually sinking. The brothers got themselves partly dressed,and then jumped ashore with the remainder of their clothing in their hands. No sooner had they left the boat than she sank, nothing be- ing saved only the clothes which the brothers Little. more were wearing. An alarm was raised, and a diver got,to work. A number of men and two steam derricks got the boat just above the surface of the water, at about five o'clock in the afternoon. The crates, which numbered 27, were got out, and a pumping machine put to work. The boat was emptied in about an hour. Had not the mishap occur ed, the boat would have left Runcorn on Thursday night for the Potteries with a load of flint.</p>
        <p>About 6.30 on Thursday morning one of the disused towero of the ammonia soda plant in the Runcorn Alkali Works now in course of demolition,yielded to the strong force of the wind, and fell in a confused mass. The tower which was composed of wool and iron was about 60 feet high and weighed about 50 tons. About 17 men were working close to at the time, but no ono was hurt.</p>
        <p>About noon the water in the river attained a height of 20 feet, whilst the height in the canal was .)ver 36 feet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUNCORN DISTRICT NURSE FUND.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN DISTRICT NURSE FUND.</p>
        <p>The seventh annual meeting of subscribers to this fund was held on Monday in the Council Chamber in the Town Hall, under the presidency of the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood. The committee reported that in the hands of Nurse Buckenham the work had progressed satiofactorily and mentioned that funds providei by Miss Pierpoint. and Mrs. Soulby had enabled a number of patients to be sent to country lodgings, and convales- cent homes. The number of visits paid by the nurse during the year as 2941, a decrease of 510 visits on the previous year. This was accounted for in a great measure, by the great decrease in typhoid cases as com- pared with 1895. There were 22 who bad received 531 visits, while in 1896 there had bean three cases which had received 101 visits. The population of the town had also decreased The balance sheet showed subscriptions £79 Bs. 6d., and the total income £l3O 7s. 2d. After paying all expenses, there was a balance in hand of £39 2s. The report was adopted, and the Chairman thanked the Runcorn Cycle Club for their efforts in trying to establish a cottage hospital. He (the Chairman) had witnessed a cycle parade in Florence,—the home of art—but it was noth- ing to be compared with the one that was held in Run- corn, neither by the number of machines,nor the beauty of their decoration. Mrs. Preston was re. elected on the committee and Mrs. W. B. Kay, and Mrs. Walter Tim- mins and Mrs. McLennan were elected on the committee in place of Mrs. Speakman, Mrs. Fisher, and Mrs. Light- burn (resigned).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>...._,....- RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>...._,....- RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above Board was held on Tuesday in the Board Room, Town Hall. Mr. J. Prit- chard presided ,and there were also present,the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Mr. F. J. Norman, J.P., Mr. E. Imison, C.C., Mr. C. Timmins, and Mr. G. Moss (clerk).</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE. I The minutes of meetings held on September Bth and 24th showed that 76 cases were summoned, and that 54 appeared,and it was decided that proceedings be taken against 13 persons for sending their children irregularly Ito school. It was also recommended that a girl named Mary Neish be sent to the Liverpool Blind School. The committee presented their annual report which stated that there had been 4,226 warning notices served,against 4.009 in the preceding year ; increase 217. There were 559 parents summoned before the committee,an increase of 7on the previous year. There had been 69 persons prosecuted by the magistrates, a decrease of 29 OD the preceding year. The average number of children en the rolls was 3,420, last year 3,440, decrease 20. The average attendance was 2,821, last year 2787, increase 34. The percentage of attend- ance was 82.5, last year 810; increase 1.5. The committee had done its best by moral suasion to deal with the chronic bad attendance, and had sent a less number of cases to the police court. The result was not discouraging as the perzentage of attendance 82 5 was better than in the previous year.and not much behind the highest recorded,B3 per cent. Still there waos considerable room for improvement. The Board had now no children attending industrial schools, but there was a boy and a girl at the school for the blind, Liver-' pool, and a bay at a school for the deaf in Yorkshire. Dr. Gastaldi proposed the adoption of the minutes.l With reference to the blind girl whom they recom- mended to be sent to a blind school, she was intelligent, and the parents were very poor. They were bound to Send her to a home. Mr.lmison seconded the proposition. He thought the I report a very fair one, and the committee had done their work in a very admirable way, particularly their worthy chairman, Dr. Gastaldi. He thought the Rev. doctor deserved their highest praise. The Chairman said they were greatly indebted to Dr. Gastaldi for his regular attendance on the committee. It was the most objectionable part of the work of the Board, but yet it was necessary. He had done good service. The report was a very favourable one, as there was an increase in the attendance, with fewer prosecutions before the magistrates. The minutes were adopted. SCHOOL MANAGEMENT COMMITTEE. The School Managemeta Committee recommended that Hilda Newell be appointed monitor at Granville girls' schools, at a salary of 2e. 6d. per week; that a cupboard for storing stationery, &amp;c., he obtained for the Victoria- road boys' school at a cost of about £6 10s. ; that a piano be purchased for Victoria boys' and girls' schools, and one for Granville-at. girls' school, the cost not to exceed £l9 each. It was further recommended that a gallery in the Granville-St. infant schoolroom be removed so as to provide room for desks. Mr. Norman in proposing the adoption of the minutes, said they were compelled to make the recommendations for the purchase of the pianos, because of the new regu lation code. Mr. Imison seconded. He said the pianos would be a very valuable aid to the teachers, and it was quite imperative that the Board shoald provide them. The pianos were necessary in order to secure a high grant. They might not entail any greater burden on the rates. The Chairman said the matter had been before the Committee time after time. They could now no longer postpone it in face of the code. The minutes were carried,and this concluded the busi- ness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>HEAVY SENTENCE ON FREQUENTERS</head>
        <p>HEAVY SENTENCE ON FREQUENTERS</p>
        <p>AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>DETECTED BY BLOOD STAINS.</p>
        <p>On Monday at the Runcorn Petty Sessions before Mr. T. Rigby (chairman) and Mr. J. W. Lightburn, two Liverpool youths named Thomas Bulger and Frederick Davies were charged with frequenting Waterloo-road with intent to commit a felony early on the 3rd of Octo- ber. Both pleade4 not guilty.—Mr. R. Unsworth, boot and shoe maker,Waterloo-road, said he retired to rest on the 2nd inst. about eleven o'clock. He left everything secured. About a quarter to one next morning he was aroused by two police officers. After what they said to him he examined the shop door and found a hole (about 1i inches in diameter) had been made in the window. There was a box of silk handkerchiefs near the hole. A quantity of blood was about, probably caused by a per- son putting his hand through the glass.—P.C. Moore said he noticed the prisoners near to Mr. Unsworth's shop about eleven o'clock. They walked away and wit- ness examined the shop door. He saw the window broken. He then gave information along with the description of the men to Sergeant Lee.—Ser- geant Lee deposed ;to {receiving information, about 12.45 a.m. from the last witness. He went to the shop and found the window-frame covered with blood. He met the prisoners about 1.30 a.m. in High-st. and ex- amined their hands. One of the prisoners had a rag covered with blood on his hand. Bulger ran off, but was soon caught. The last witness identified Ithem as the men he bad seen previously about Mr. Unsworth's shop.—ln reply to the charge, Bulger said, "Yes we did break the glass." Davies said, "We got inside, we intended to get somewhere and we wanted to get our fares to get across the bridge."—Superintendent O'kell produced the characters which he had received from the Liverpool police. Prisoners were committed to prison for three months each, with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>WESTON.</head>
        <p>WESTON.</p>
        <p>THE WESLEYAN BAZKAIL—The bazaar which was opened on Wednesday last week, and continued up to Friday, in aid of redecorating the Weston Wesleyan Chapel and schools, resulted in the taking of £142 4s. 81d. NEW Pooh RATE.—A new poor rate of is. 2d. in the £, estimated to realise £669 19s. 114 d. for Weston, has been sighed by Messrs. J. Brundrit, and F. J. Norman for the ensuing six months. This is the same rate as for the corresponding period last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>WESTON POINT.</head>
        <p>WESTON POINT.</p>
        <p>HARVEST THANKSGIVING:—The harvest thankcgiv.ing services were e-.mcluded on Sunday. The sacred edifice was most tastefully decorated by the following willing hauds:—Mrs. Ranks, Mrs. Mather, Mrs. J. Zeffs, Mrs. and the Misses, M'Lean (2) Misses Mills(2), Bates, Stott, Lunt, Grounds, Jenson, Dunbabin; Messrs. Rosenberg, J. Jeffs, W. Mather and Johnson. The sermons were preached by the vicar and the Rev. J.J. Bryant, vicar of Whitley, in the evening. The church, was crowded at both services. SHOCKING ACCIDENT.—About mid-night on Friday, a shocking accident befel Walter Stubbs, of 36, Shaw. st., Runcorn, at the Weston Chemical Works, of the United Alkali Co. He was assisting to discharge a cargo ot sulphur ore, when the crane bucket struck him a terrible blow on the forehead as it swung round. Dr. Delaney who was near at the time, attended to the injured man, who had sustained a lacerated wound on the forehead, measuring about 14 inches and reaching the bone. The injured man was subsequently conveyed home and on later inquiries being made we were informed that he was progressing favourably towards recovery.</p>
        <p>The Board of Trade returns issued on Wensesday show that the imports tor September smounted to £33,111,156, against £30,618,854, in the same month of 1895, being an increase of £2,492,302. Imports for the nine months ended September 30 were £316,368,471, against £303,935,714, for the corresponding period last year, showing an increase of £12,432,757. The exports for September were £19,797,080, compared with £:9.461,940 in the same month of 1895, the increase being £335,140. The exports for the nine months ended Senteiriber 30 were £180,436,153, compared with £166,620,437, in the corresponding period of last year, showing an increase of £13,815,716.</p>
        <p>CUTLERY and illectro-Plated GOODS.—GARLAND. Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. RANGES.—See the Herald Improved Lifting Fire Bangee.—Sole Agent, GAIs:.AND, Bridge-street.—Advt,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL MATCII.—A. match was played on Wednes- day between Frodsham and Aston hall on the ground of the former. The game ended in favour of the hoinesters by 3 goals to one. ASSAULT ON A LANDLORD.—At the Frodshain Police Court on Tuesday, before Mr. Thomas and Dr. Steele, William Campbell of Liverpool, was charged on a war- rant with assaulting Samuel Davies, landlord of the Railway Inn. Prisoner pleaded guilty.—Mr. Davies said the prisoner, along with a numWer of other men, arrived by the 11 o'clock train from Liverpool on Monday night. The men began to quarrel near his door, and he re quested them to go away. The prisoner at once struck witness and kicked him in the eye.—the magistrates imposed a fine of 103. and costa. A PEDLAR FlNED.—Elizabeth Gallaghan was charged before Mr. Thomas and Dr. Steele, at the Frodsham Police Court on Wednesday, with acting as a pedlar without a certificate at Overton.—P.C. Bevington proved the case, and she was fined 53. including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL AND nig HU? CANAL.</p>
        <p>An important discussion took place at the meeting of the Liverpool City Council on Wednesday concerning the relations between the Corporation and the Mersey Dock Board arid the increasing competition of the Man- chester Ship Canal. The Finance Committee submitted a recemmendation, in reply to the memorial of the Chamber of Commerce and other commercial associa- tions, that inasmuoh as the Dock Board were. not pre- pared to discuss with them such matters as were.e...sen- dal to enable them to form an opinion as to the necessity of the Corporation giving financial assistance to the Board in order to maintain the trade of the port, the Ciouncil would not be justified in promoting a bill to transfer to the Corporation the Board's liability for polic- ing the dock estate. Sir A. B. Forwood, M.P., moved an amendment indicating that the Corporation fully realised the importance of maintaining and extending the trade of the port, and were willing to co-operate to that end in every reasonable and practical manner with the Dock Board and other public bodies. In the course of the diccussim it was urged that the Do,;k Board and the Corporation should meet for discussion on a wider basis than that of policing the docks. Sir A. B. Forwood pointed out that the Board by a modification of its financial system could in course ! of time save something like £1.50,000 a year. There were, beside, sums of £30,000 for police charges and £60,000 for poor rates, and were the Board relieved of these obligations it would be in a position to successfully ! cope with any competition which Manchester might offer, even though the latter attempted to attract trade' to the canal at an absolute loss ; and realisation of that! fact would do much to lessen the headlong race and the I necesity for action on the Dcck Board's part. Sir Arthur i proceeded to deal at length with the methods adopted by Manchester for attracting' 'Ade to the canal, and ! urged that the public of Liverpool should not sit quietly down and allow emissaries to go to America or Bombay ! or elsewhere and circulate incorrect statements as to the comparative charges of Liverpool and Manchester. Sir W. B. Forwood advocated the construction of an inde- pendent railway line for goods between Manchester and Liverpool. This, he said, could be done for two mil- lions of money. The recommendation as amended was unanimously agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>GALE.</head>
        <p>THE GALE.</p>
        <p>GREAT DESTRUCTION AND LOSS OF LIFE.</p>
        <p>Considerable loss of life and immense damage to pro- perty have been caused by the heavy gale which blew tram the south-west during Wednesday night and Thurs- day nsorning. Ten lives were lost in the Humber by a collision during the gale between the Swedish steamer Alosandra and the Goole steamer Emden. The former sank almost iinmediately,and eight seamen and the two stewardesses wore drowned. At Holyhead an unknown schooner, which was being towed by a tug, snapped her hawser, and foundered with her five hands. The Daunts Rock lightship, stationed just outside Cork harbour, is reported missing, and it is feared that her crew of ten have been lost. A quantity of wreckage has been seen in the neighbourhood. The gale was felt severely in the Irish Sea, and the cross channel steamers were greatly delayed. Five men were imprisoned in the old wreck Palme, which lies embedded in the sands of Blackrock. The Kingstown harbour master ordere4 out one of the lifeboats. To those on shore it seemed, ac- cording to our Dublin correspondent's reportithat she did not go near enough to the wreck to ascertain whether anyone was on board. She returned and reported no one on the wreck, but in consequence of coastguard signals the harbour-master was not satisfied,aad ordered another trip. A retired captain of the merchant service took charge of the boat. The wreck was reached, and five men were found on her. They were rescued with much difficulty. Great indignation is stated to prevail in consequence of the action of the lifeboat crew on the first journey. Brave lifeboat rescues are reported frern Tenby and Ramsey, but losses of life among fishermen have to be recorded. Great havoc has been caused by the sea along the Welsh coast. At Towyn for nearly two miles the railway em- bankment running along the shore was washed away, and several bridges were also demolished. The gasworks were submerged, and on Thursday night the town was in darkness. In many o,trier parts of North Wales the Cambrian Railway has suffered much damage. Near Foryd a strong sea wall, in the course of construction, was completely demolished. Reports of heavy damage come from English ports. Tremendous seas were ex- perienced at Blackpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>TUB ARCHDRAXONRY OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>TUB ARCHDRAXONRY OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Rev. G. H. Spooner, rector of Woolton, at the request of a large number of the clergy of the Arch- deaconry of Warrington, has consented to accept nomination to the office of Proctor, rendered vacant by the resignation of Canon Diggle. The Rev. E, C. E. Carleton, of Warrington, has also been nomi- nated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>LOYAL LOCAL FARMIMS.</head>
        <p>LOYAL LOCAL FARMIMS.</p>
        <p>A few days ago a number of local farmers assem- bled at the Blue Bell Hotel, florsemarket-street, sent a telegram to the Queen congratulating her Majesty upon her record reign. Much gratification has been occasioned by the receipt of the following reply.—" Buckingham Palace, 2nd October, 1896. The private secretary is commanded to express the thanks of the Queen for the kind message of con- gratulation which you have forwarded to her Majesty. Mr Kershaw, Bell Hotel, Warrington." BA_TTIST CHAPEL, LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>_ On Sunday morning the Rev. Charles Andrew spoke on the Armenian massacres from the words " Execute judgment in the morning, and deliver him that is spoiled out of the band of the oppres- sor" Jer. 21-12. At the close of the sermon the following resolution was passed :—" That this con- gregation desires to express its deepest sympathy with the Armenians in the oppression and terrible sufferings to which they are subjected. Also its indignation with those who are directly respon- sible for the existence and continuance of such barbarities. At the same time assuring her Majesty's Government of its loyal support in any action they may take with a view to rescuing Armenia from the cruel grasp of the Sultan, and causing this gross tyranny to cease. With the prayer that the 'All-wise Ruler' may guide and :iirect in all the affairs of so important a crisis."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>WARRINGTON CRAMBER OF COMMERCE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CRAMBER OF COMMERCE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above chamber was held at Market Gate (2hambers, on Monday, under the presidency of Mr.lt-)bert Garnett J.?. Mr. Fred Monks J.P.,(who wit h Mr. John Fairclough J.P.,and Mr.Pohn Houghton attended the recent autumnal meeting of tbe Associated Chambers of Commerce at Southampton) reported that the business trans- acted was of a most interesting character, and had a most important bearing on the principal com- mercial questions before the country. The delegates were entertained with the usual hospitality, and the meeting was in every sense a great success.—A communication was read from the National Indivi- dualist Club reque•ting the co-operation of the chamber in the work of the club,which was defined as being to uphold individual freedom and private rights against State and municipal aggression. The Council decided that it was not desirable to take any action in the matter.—The Secretary (Council- lor Bennett) reported that he had received full particulars of the Brussls International Exhibi- tion, to be held next year, and that intending exhi- bitors might obtain all information on application to the chamber.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>FOOTBA LL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBA LL.</p>
        <p>WAERINCITON "A "V. RADCLIFFE FIRST.—On Satur- day the" A" team went to Raocliffe near Bury, to en- gage in a match with Radcliffe Ist team. There were about 2,000 spectators present on the ground. The War- rington team was composed of Boscow,full-back: Moores O'Brien, Isherwood, and Carey, threequarters, Wilcock and Fairhurst, half hacks; Savage, Freeth, Robev, An- drews, Meacoes, Jolley, Templeton, and A. N. Other, forwards. Robey kicked off for Warrington and a scrim- mage ensued from which Radcliffe got possession and some smart work came from their three-quarter line. r All through the first half Warrington were badly beaten more particularly at half-hack and the , score at the interval was—Radciffe 16 points to War- rington nil. Two of the goals scored were from tries by Goodman, the ex-Swinton three-quarter, and Moores got his side penalised near the posts and a goal was kicked. In the second half Warrington held their own and nothing further was scored. The forwards played a , strong game. The match ended with the score as at 1 half-time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>VISIT oF THE REV. JOHN McNEILL TO PARR HALL.</head>
        <p>VISIT oF THE REV. JOHN McNEILL TO PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>The long expected and much advertised mission of the Rev. John McNeill, the well-known evange- list, has been conducted in the Parr Hall. Warring- ton, this week, and has been crowned with success. Crowded meetings have been held, and it may safely be claimed that this united evangelist mission in Warrington has been the means ot bringing to- gether the largest gatherings of a religious kind which have yet been held in the town. ihe earnest and telling discourses of the evangelist have been I attentively foilowed, and it is hoped that much good will result. A large choir under the conduc- torship of Mr. Walter Brough, composed of mem- bers from the various choirs in the town, has been present each evening, and the sing- ing throughout has been especially good. Mr. Peter Harrison presided at the harmonium. The ener- getic secretaries (Rev. E, T. Reed and Mr. Edmund Osborne) have been ably backed up by a large body of stewards, and they are to be congratulated upon the excellent arrangements which have been made.</p>
        <p>The Rev. JOHN McNEILL received a hearty wel- come to Warrington at a well attended meeting of Christian workers held in Parr Hall on Saturday evening. Mr. E. J. Guest, J. P., presided, and he was supported on the platform by the Rev. John McNeill, and the Revs. John Yonge, (Wycliffe Con- gregational Church), William Potts (Wesleyan), J. Warnock (St. John's Presbyterian), J. S. Hughes (Baptist Chapel, Hill Cliff), C. Andrew (Baptist Chapel, Latchford), Charles Finch (Primi- tive Methodist, Legh-street). the Rev. E. T. Reed (Emmanuel Reformed Churchlßold-street), and Mr. Edmund Osborne (hon. secretaries), and Mr. John Fairclough, J.P. The CHAIRMAN said he was in sincere and hearty sym- pathy with the movement, and he was glad in the name of the meeting to give a cordial welcome to the Rev. John McNeill. (Applause.) He trusted the services would be cr)sned;with the Divine blessing,,and that during the short time Mr. McNeill was amongst them he would be physically strong and mentally vigorous,and, above all, that he would be filled with the Divine Spirit, and that the Spirit would speak through him to them.He regarded the present movement as a real re-union of the churches, and it was a blessed sign of the times. (Hear, hear.) Some people 'might think these meetings unnecessary. They certainly had their;church organisations,and it was asked why multiply meetings? For this reason, that they were liable to move slowly aed to get out of gear. It had been in the order of God's providence to ptepare men to d 3 special work for Him,:as in the case of Moses, who was fitted to lead the children of Israel, also in the case of Elijah, and in these latter times they had had Moody and Sankey, who had called the churches into a more lively and healthy condition. The churches of this country were better to-day by reason of the visit of Moody and Sankey. (Applause.) They had all heard of the Rev. John McNeill, and they were very glad that he had come to Warrington to strengthen ,help. and encourage th-m. (Applause.) The Rev. E. T. REED read the following letter from the Rev. J. Davies, vicar of St. Ann's, who, he said, would have been with them that night but for the acci- dent which befel him during the recent thunderstorm : —" 15. Folly-lane, Warrington, September 30th, 1896. near Mr. Reed,—l need scarcely say that I greatly re- gret not being able to be with you on Saturday next. It would have given me great pleasure, and I should have deemed it a special favour to have been able, in person, to have joined with you and others in extending the heartiest of welcomes to our brother in Christ, the Rev. John McNeill,on his coming among us to carry on the special evangelistic mission. I can desire nothing better for him, which I heartily and sincerely do, than that he may be greatly used of God,as heretofore, in the pulling down of Satan's strongholds, and in the strengthening and extending of the kingdom of our Lord Jesus Christ. May he be endued with the Spirit from on high ! May the mission be abundantly blessed ! May the Holy Spirit be manifestedly present in all the meetings, con- verting sinners.sanctifying saints,energising all workers! May Warrington and neighbourhood be richly blessed ! These are my earnest supplications, and with expectant hope that they may be heard at the Throne of Grace, I remain, faithfully yours, JAMES Davies." Mr. Reed said a letter had also been received from the Rector, who wrote " I do most earnestly desire that all blessing may attend your efforts." (Applause.) Since the meeting the following letter has also been re- ceived from Mr. J. C. Parr, J.P., D.L. :—•• Dear Sir,—l much regret that I am not able to be with you to-morrow to assist in giving a hearty welcome to the Rev. John McNeill on his first appearance in Warrington. I hope you will kindly explain to him that the change of date from November to October is accountable for my not being able to he present and to take advantage of the mission services as I should have liked to have done. I have to take the chair at a meeting hereon Tuesday,and am engaged in London on Thursday,and fear I shall not be able to return to Warrington till after the 16th. I sincerely hope his welcome may he a hearty as well as a , representative one, and that the mission he has came to conduct may be a source of lasting benefit to the town generally and individually. With my most sincere good wishes, believe me, yours faithfully. J. CHARLTON PARR. Mr. Osborne, general secretary, Y.M.C.A.. The Rev. Joule YONGE said it afforded him a great deal of pleasure to be there that night and to give his humble word of hearty welcome to Mr. McNeill. Pro- bably some of them had had the pleasure, as he (the speaker) had, of hearing him, and they all heartily re- joiced in the glorious evangelistic success which, by God's blessing, Mr. McNeill had achieved in that and other lands. He came to them with many trophies of spiritual victories, and they hoped he might win more even in Warrington. He was a man with special gif's for special work and he had their deep sympathy in the work he had come to attempt. Perhaps it would be well to be frank with him and say that those who had been engaged in hristian work in Warrington had been forced to the conviction that Warrington was not the easiest of soils in which to sow seed. It was not the most acceptable of all places in listening to the gospel. Not that he wished to urge one syllable of complaint. Personally he had reason for great thank- fulness for the kindness of the people of Warrington, during the past 20 years he had been a minister in the town. But still it was a fact, testified to by nearly all Christian ministers and by evangelists who had been here before,that Warrington was one of the most difficult places in which to arouse a sustained interest to reach the hearts and quicken the consciences of the people. Still they were hoping for great things. They bad to contend against indifferent,selfish absorption in business; the usual vices of life—drinking, gambling and impurity which were rank vices in Warrington. The churches needed quickening,and the town needed arousing by the glorious blessing of God. He trusted that the visit ot Mr. McNeill would accomplish those results. (Applause.) The Rev. W. Parrs said it would be a strange thing if the Wesleyans were not in sympathy with that move- ment. They were always supposed to be in the front in evangelistic and revival work; and in the hearts of all Methodist people there had been during the past two or three weeks a great cry going up to God that He we uld use his servant who had come amongst them. He telt that the special value of evangelistic work was that there were a great many who only needed a littie im- pulse and a little encouragement to declare themselves on the Lord's side ; and it was in those evangelistic services that a special appeal could be made to them. He trusted that as a result of the mission, many people would be brought to the Saviour. (Hear, hear.) The Rev. J. WARNOCK said on behalf of the church he represented it gave him pleasure to say a few words of I welcome. He was sure it was the earnest and fervent ' prayer of everyone of them that Mr. McNeill's brief so- journ amongst them might be fruitful in blessings that ' would spiritually enrich all their services. They were all delighted to see him. He might reckon him a fair specimen of what Presbyterianism under favourable con- ditions was able to produce. (Laughter). They were rather proud of him—(laughter)—and counted him one of themselves. He was not unmindful of the fact that that was a united mission in which many denominations joined, the basis being that of Christianity. (Applause.) It was a great adyan taee to bring so many Christian workers together. That was in itself a great gain. It enabled them to see that orit them and it w the soul of Christianity was greater than the boas'. (Applause.) They in Warrmeton, were exceedinsdy fortunateas for h that they a ley were greatly Mr. ' indehted to the energetic and persistent efforts of their tia n tthaving of good fortune N 1 cNei excellent secretaries, Rev. E. T. Reed and Mr. Osborne. (Applause.) The Rev. J. S. BIIOHES, speaking on behalf of the Baptist churches, said they hopeci that this mission was , the dawn of a brighter era in the history of the Chris- tian churches in that town. (Applause.) They had , tremendous forces of evil to contend a tainst in Warrin ton, and sometimes they seemed well-nigh overpowering and overwhehrine. There was the drink force, and also the gamlling force which burned like a fever in the hearts of young and old, doing dreadful havoc. There was the pleasure-loving force which made their people too exhausted to enter the house of God on a Sunday morning. (Hear, hear.) Then there was the force of indifference which killed all the efforts of the Christian churches and m icle them feel as though they were beat- ing the air or striking their fists against jagged rocks. These were some of the forces of evil with which they had to contend with in that town and locality, and they rejoiced in Mr. I,ilcNeill's help towards breaking the evil spell. (Applause.) Rev. E. T. REED said it was nine years ago since he heard Mr. McNeill preach at the conference of the Young Men's Christian Association. Ile desired to give him a most hearty welcome to Warrington as a humble mem- ber of the Young Men's Christian Association Commit- tee. (Applause.) During the last few years the great Christian Endeavour movement had come to the front, laying hold of the young men of the Christian churches and setting them to work in the service of the Lord. As president of the Warring.</p>
        <p>ton and District Young People's Societies of Christian Endeavour, consisting of about 700 or 800 memters, he could say that none had worked more energetically for that mission than the members of the Christian Endeavour Societies. (Applause.) Then there was no congregation in that town which was more hearty in its welcome to Mr. McNeill than that attending the Reformed Episcopal Church of which he (Mr Reed) had the pleasure and privilege to be the minister. There- fore, in the three capacities he had mentioned he gave a hearty welcome to Mr. McNeill with the earnest hope and prayer that his mission might be blessed of God. (Applause.) On rising to respond, the Rev. John McNeill met with a most hearty reception. At the outset he stated that it was a little trying to come amongst a people who did not know one, and whom one did not know, and also to hear half a dozen able and efficient speakers telling them with eloquence" what a fellow ye are"—(laughter)—and raising thereby ex- pectations in toe breasts of those who composed the first audience that he would defy even the Apostle Paul to satisfy. (Renewed laughter.) And yet he was glad to come amongst them. It was nice to be welcomed. He had been told how hard they were to move,but evidently this was a gathering of the best side of Warrington —(laughter)--for they did not appear to be so stiff aft, r all. He wished they were at their real work. One became a little impatient the nearer it came, but he was glad he had seen this meet- ing, because he felt that there was a power here, if they , were only equal to what was said about them, to shake that community,if it was even as dead as the proverbial door nail. He trusted that through God's grace and blessing there were bright and blessed things in store for them. He was glad it was an united mission, and he was glad to hear the letters read from the Vicar and the Rector. He hoped the singing would be hearty, and that there would be a chorus of praise and thanksgiving. It was also a pleasure to him to see such a large choir. There was a great deal of bene- fit in hearty simple singing although the hymns might be characterised by some as of the "jingling " order. (Laughter). Let them not be nervous, but remember that they were working for God, and God would get His own. "Be ye not doubtful men." They must trust God. He always gave hina what he asked for—far more,indeed than he deserved. There was no master like Him, and He was neither going to stint them nor stint Himself in a work like that in which they were then engaged. Perhaps God might not show them what He was doing with any great visability, because that might feed the tendency to walk by sight and not by faith. They must be themselves. The audience had been told that they must ot depend upon him. Let him also tell them the same thing. They would depend upon a broken reed if they depended upon him. Let their expectations be from Him, and then their labours in Warrington would be abundantly blessed. In conclusion he urged his hearers to work in the Mpster's cause and for the spread of His kingdom. Let them take hope from the fact that they were on the winning side. The rev, gentleman's remarks were frequently applauded, and he created a most favourable impression.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>SERVICES ON SUNDAY.</head>
        <p>SERVICES ON SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>The Rev. John McNeill gave the first of his services in the Parr Hall on Sunday afternoon when the building was crowded. On the platform was a choir composed of about 150 singers recruited from churches of every de- nomination In the town. Mr. McNeill himself was sup- norted by the Revs. E. T. Reed, J. Warnock, and W. Potts. The choir led the singing, and was c inducted by Mr. Walter Brou h. After devotional exercises the Rev. John McNeil ex- pressed his pleasure in being at Warrineton. He knew nothing abeut Warrington until Friday night, except that it was a stltion at which the London and Glasgow express trams did not stop. (Laughter.) He wanted to say one or two things about the mission. They most just dismiss their minds of the opinion that there was anything sensational in a bad sense about those ser- vices. It was a great pity that the word re- rival hPd got s bad name; there ought to be no word more popular. When they applied the word to health how sweet it was : a revival of health to someone who was beginning to waste and decay. In business how beautiful: it was : a revival in trade was music in their ears. Unhappily when they applied it to religion, a revival in religion, some people thought they referred to a kind of mad dog that had broken loose in the streets and might bite them. That was revival. (Laugh- ter.) IVltat they all longed to see in business, health, and the returning seasons of the year, they wanted to see in religion. (Hear, hear.) He referred to the pro- gramme ef the mission and stated that he would hold a meeting every night in the week except Saturday. "Six days shalt thou labour," applied to evangelists as well as other hard working people. (Laughter.) He exhorted them all to attend and implored shopkeepers, &amp;c., to let their assistants have the same opportunity. (Hear,hear). He was astonished sometimes to find Christian men who would not do anything to let their assistants come. He would not give a rap for Christianity like that. Let them do everything they could to set everybody "foot loose" to come as often as they would. One or two hymns were then heartily sung and a col- lection taken towards expenses. The preacher then announced his text as 119th Psalm, 59th and 60th verses "I thought on my ways and turned my feet unto Thy testimony; I made haste aid delayed not to keep Thy commandments." He said the Lord Jesus Christ had given great testimony to men that day. " Believe in Him and thou shalt be saved." There was the great testimony, the great statute, the great commandment. The man who uttered the words of the text said " I thought." Next he thought about himself; and third he was a man who was a practical thinker for he said " I thought on nay ways and turned my feet." Lastly they had a man who was prompt in action for he said " I made haste and delayed not." If he (the preacher)had come to Warrington that afterncoe to give an address on " Success in Life" he would have taken as his points those into which he had dividedhis text that afternoon. First of all they ttiould think for them- selves and not be led by every Tom,Dick and Harry who came with his cheap advice as to their business. Secondly, they should think about themselves; make it their business. Third they should be practical and not begin and end in merely thinking and talking but do Qom:thing. Lastly, they should be prompt and not simmer and wonder over things for ever. Mr. McNeill then preached earnestly and eloquently in exhorting his hearers to "think on their ways and turn their steps " in the right path at once. He was listened to with close attention and a most successful meeting closed with the singing of the Doxology.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>FORESTE RS' ANNIVERSARY AT EA RLESTO WN.</head>
        <p>FORESTE RS' ANNIVERSARY AT EA RLESTO WN.</p>
        <p>In connection with the anniversary of the Broad Oak Court No. 545 (Earlestown) of the Ancient Order of Foresters, a hot-pot dinner was held at the Wellington Icn, Earlestown. on Saturday evening. About 80 per- sons were present, and the hot-pot which was served up in an appetising form, was much enjoyed. After the cloth was removed the subsequent proceedings were pre- sidcd over by Bro. G. Jackson, who was supported by Bros. W. Davies,James Egan,and Dr. Paterson, the vice- chair being occupied by Bro. Gilbert. The usual loyal toasts were proposed and duly honoured. The toast of ' •‘ The town and trade of Earlestown and Newton," was given by Bro. W. Davies, who observed that at the pre- sent time trade was in a flourishing condition. He went on to refer to the Order whose anniversary they were met to celebrate,and alluded to Old Age Pensions, which he thought were a necessity in these times, cQnsidering that if well paid employes of the State could be granted pensions,surely the workers who build up the State had the first claim on their consideratiqn. He quoted a case which occurred at Lt.igh a short time back in which an old man of 63 years of age committed suicide .4wing to his having been discharged from his his work, and rather than be any trouble to his friends, he had put an and to his life. He thought that was a case in which tht old ago pensions would he applicable. Mr. Smith responded, and spoke of the dangers of min- ing, anel thought miners ought to be well paid. No matter how much money they got they would not be overpaid.—Mr. John Warburton proposed "The Ancient Order of Foresters."—Mr. J. Latham (secretary) res- ponded, and in the course of his remarks gave a report of his visit as delegate, to the High Court of Foresters held at Dundee on July 31st last.—The reading of the report was attentively listenen to and frequently ap- plauded. The Chairman ofterwards proposed the toast of "Court, Broad Oak 595," to which Bro. Egan res- ponded, mentioning that a few years ago the Court fin- ances were in a perilous condition. They, however, formulated a scheme which proved a very beneficial one, and now the Court was almost in a state of affluence, and had a prosperous career before it. He concluded by proposing the health of Dr. PattersoP, to which that gentleman responded. Several songs were given during the evening by members present, and an enjoyable meet- ing resulted.</p>
        <p>THAT BOY AGAIN:— An incident, sa.ys the Han Daily Mail, occurred at Goole the other day which is well worth recording. A boy, residing in -Percy Street, Old Goole, had been sent on an errand by his mother, and amongst other articles he had to purchase a box of Beecham's Pills. Instead of returning straight home the lad staved playing on a raft in the Barge Dock, and whilst rocking to and fro he acedentally fell overboard. There were other youths with him at the time, but they ran away. A labourer, named John Grassby, employed at the gasworks, apprehended that something was wrong, and ran to the side of the dock. All that he could see, however, was a box of 13eecham's Pills float- ing on the top of the water, hut directly afterwards the head of the lad appeared and the man succeeded in rescuing him. After this who shall ray that these Pils are not worth a guinea a box?</p>
        <p>A man and woman giving the name of Lapraik, and supposed to he of French extraction, have been lodging since Saturday last at a house in Littlehampton, on the Sussex coast. Early on Tuesday morning shots were heard in the house, and on the police obtaining at' en- trance the woman was found lying in a dying condition .on the staircase,wit la a bullet wound in her head. The man was dead when discovered, having shot hinaself with a revolver, which he still grasped in his hand.</p>
