      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>-= – – BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>-= - - BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>FBOCKING CHILD NEGLECT.</p>
        <p>At the Warringtonloroug.) Court on Friday before AldermanFlarrison and _other magistrates, Thomas Mit- chell, 2, Welch'.-yard. Latchford, was charged -with ney,lecting his five children in a manner likely to injure their health.—lnspector M'Ateer.N.S.P.o C., stated that oefendant was the father of five children between nine years of age and eight months. Fi consequence of a eomplaint made to him on Saturday night, the 19th of of last month, about eleven o'clock p.m., he went with P.C. Jones to defendant's house. The door was open, and all in darkness. They found defendant's wife helplessly drunk on the remains of an old sofa. On the centre of the floor lay J-imes,aged two years,partly dressed and fast asleep. They aroused the woman but she could not tell them where her husband was. He soon after arrived, also drunk. Upstairs they found one of the children qnite naked. They asked her for the other children and neither: of the parents could say where they were. They eventually found the children in the coal cellar of the next door neighbour's house in a filthy condition. De fendant was a drunken fellow who would not work. He had been cautioned by witness several times. He (the Inspector) would have prosecuted the wife but she was in gaol when he took proceedings.—P.C. Jones sup- ported the Inspector's statement.—Defendant, who bad been up for theft and other offences, was sent to gaol for one month's hard labour. A MOTHER SENT TO PRISON FOR NEGLECTING FIER CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>On Monday at the Warrington Borough Court, Cecilia Mitchell, wife of Thomas Mitchell, of Welch's-yard, Latchford, was charged with havine neglected her five children in a manner likely to cause injury to their health. She pleaded gnilty.—lnspector McAteer stated that in consequence of a report, he visited the defend-, ant's house in company with P.€l. Jones. Re found the door open and the house in darkness. The defendant was helplessly drunk lying on the remains of an old so- fa. The youngest child was lyiig asleep on the gigs partly dressed. They awakened the woman and she had a black eye. . Her husband came home drunk, and he could give them no satisfaction. They found some of ;he children in a neighbours house. The children were brought from the coal-hole in a most filthy condition. They went upstairs and on the remains of a filty bed one of the children lay naked in a shocking condition. All the defendants children had been neglected for the past twelve months. He (the inspector) had repeatedh cautioned the defendant, but she was a drunken, cal- lous woman. Any money she could get was spent in drink. If the children had been kent in the condition they were now presented in Court, no police action would have been taken. The clothing they were now wearing did not belong to them.—Alderman IlArrisen : They are dressed for the occasion, then.—lnspector M'Ateer : It is done .repeatedly in these cases. As soon as the summons is issued the parents go to the neighbours and collect clothing.—The magistrates committed the defendant to prison for one month, and instructed the Inspector to see to the removal of the children to the Workhouse. Tug N.S.P.C.C. OFFICER IN • NEW ROLE.</p>
        <p>At the-Warringto—n--B-oiliu-gh- Good on Mlnclay. a la- bourer named James Higham, of 7, Roger-street, was charged with stealing from the shop of the Winwick-at. Boot Company.two pairs of men's boots value 15s. Pri- soner pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable stated that a man named Parkes, manager for the Wincvick-street Boot Company,observed the prisoner hanging about the front of tiii premises in a rather suspicious manner. Later on he saw Inspector M'A.'-eer, N.S P.C.C.. in the neighbourhood, and mend med the mat- ter to him. The Inspector kept Lim under ob- servation and saw him come from the en- trance to a surgery next door to the boot shop,several times. At last the prisoner went to the doorway of the shop and picked up a couple of pairs of sisoes,and made off with them in the direction ot Town End. Inspector McAteer followed after him, collared him, and tried to take him back to the shop. A struggle ensued, but the Inspector prevailed. Be took the man b ick to the chop, and aft.rwards handed him over to the custody of P.C. Condliffa. Be (the Chief Constable) wished to trke that opportunity of thanking Inspector McAteer for the way he acted M the matter.—The prisoner said he was drunk at the time and did not know what he Was doing.—The Chief C mstahle said that was a stereotyped defence by prisoners in Warrington.—lnspector McAteer said the Prisoner was not drunk at the time. Witness watched him for ROMP considerable time and he acted in the man- ner of an adroit thief.—The magistrates committed the prisoner to gaol for one month's hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>FRlDLY.—Before Alderman Harrison (in the chair). Alderman Wright, Councillor Roberts. Dr. Goraall. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—ARdrew Duffy, last from Bur- tonwool, was fined ss. and costs for being drunk and dis- orderly in the Market Place at 7 15 on Thuraday night. CRUELTY TO AN Ass.—William Ward (15), 16 Portland- street, was charged with cruelly beating an ass on the 17th September.—De!endant pleaded guilty.—P.C. Corbett. said Li at on the date in question he was In Bewsev-road. and heard a "art coming down and the sound of blows He turned round and saw the defendant with two other youths in the cart. Defendant. bad a brush stale with which he struck the ass several blows. A piece of the stick flew off with the blows ' Defendant was not in oharge of the ass but having a ride with the others.—lnspector Bilson, R.S.P.O A. said he had exam- ined the animal and found it cose•ed with lumps and avoids. —Defendam. was ordered to be lockers up until Rix o'clock. A. infomEN's flow.—Bridget Costello. 19. Hill-greet, was charged with assaulting and beating Mary M'Donough, 9, Town Hill, on the 26th September.—There 'was a croay-sun, mons against M'Donough for assaulting Costello —Mr. F Roberts app-ared for complainant and stated that Costello dragged her 30 yards by the hair. Evidence *as called and defendant bound over to keep the peace for 6 months in two sureties of £5 each and pay costs. AN lx-TOWN CoUNCILLOR AND RIS D9G.—Alfred golmes. aged 14, was charged with cruelly beating a dog on the head with a stick.—Mr. Browne represented the defendant and pleaded not guilty.—Mr. J. Hill was proceeding to give evi- dence when Mr. Browne asked him if he saw the oecar- ,' rence. Mr. Hill admitted that he did not and had to et .nd down. In answer to Mr. Browne he said it was not a ;ferocious dog. Children were in the habit of '' cares- sing it "—A. daughter of Mr. Hill stet* ed that she was out with the dog and some boys called it. It. ran afte defend- ant, and he hit k on the head with a stick.—Bv Mr. Browne: She admitted that defendant shut the yard door on the dog and it tried to get over. It ran hays sometimes.—Mrs Hill described the nature of the do' s i„. juries.—Two sisters of defendant and his grandmothrr stated that he hit the dog to prevent it coming owl* the door.—Mr. Holmes g lve evidence as to Mr. Hill coming tw his hohae to see defe dant. He brought Mrs. Hill and her daughter and they all o 'me in. Mrs. Hill started ' ricking' and sh. could' rick " a bit. (Laughter.)—Mrs. Hill " Thank you."—Witness added that he ordered them out as he cold not talk to two women and a man.—Mr. Browne contended that deiendant was afraid of :he dog and was right in hitting it. Mr. Hill had offered to take 10s. to settle the case. The summons only cost him 3s. 6d. so that it was an attempt to make money out of them.—The magistrates dismissed the ease.</p>
        <p>To Alornints.—Mrs. Winslow's Soothing Syrup has been used u% er fifty years by millions of mothers fp theirehildrer while teething with perfect success It will relieve the po,e sufferer inunediately. It is pleasant to taste ; it producer natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child iron' pain, and th, little cherub awakes 'as bright as a button." Of all Chemists, Is. lick. Per bottle.—Advt.</p>
        <p>MOND/I-Y.—Before Mr. F. Vonks (chairman), Alderman Harrison. Alderman Franerimb. sir. T, Birchall, and Mr. S. Wright</p>
        <p>A BOY STRUCK BY MIITAIEVI. —Kate Ann Jones, Angel- yard was charged with beating a boy named Haley on the rth inst The boy apoeare tin c .urt with s lar.ee piece of plsster at the -eck of the head and stated that the wound wis caused by the priso ter striking him with a piece of wood from a barrel —The d-fendant admitted that she struck the by tut it was be mistake.—lt tra..spired that there ha bees a quarrel between lfirs. Haley, mom her of the boy, and the defendant, and the magistrates dismissed the cese Lieges:lra —Mr. Jenkins, sorcitor. applied on behalf of Mrs Sarah Crenshaw, for temporary authority to sell at the eld Crow beerhnuse, in place of her neeeased husband He stated that Messrs. Greenall. Whitley an I Company were agreeable to take her as t enant,end the Chief Oonstabie had no obje,tinn nrovidine she carried out certain conditions which he had named -The Chief Constable said it was rath ra slifli- Cult house to manage The apnlicant's late husband,far from being nay assistance was rattle in her way. She had managed it very creditably to harseir and he had no objection to the application if she engartmeri a potmas —Temporary aethority was granted. The following traus'ers were also granted : Off-license. 63. Priestley-street, to *lamb Hill. in lieu of her deceased husband. George Hill: Stamford Arms. R tonford- street to Ralph Barlow from James Lockett; Blacksmiths Arms, Crown-street to Thomas ledward Brumbell from his wife; Shakespeare lan. Dolman's-lane from Mrs. Green to William ; eft-license. Troyle-street, to John Landor from Mrs. Aline Gosling Beascrie or THE PEACV.—Jeleph Unison. labourer, Dial- street. and Michael McLair. labourer. it, Dial Curt, were charged with ommitting a breach of the peace, by fighting in Dial-str&lt;et —P C. Baxendale proved the case, and the de- fendants were bound over in their own recto inizances :3(25 to keep the peace. McLair was also ordered to pay the °oats. • WILY" Dils" VTIoN OF PROPERTY.—AIbert Jones (15), 88, Pierpoint-et., and J ha William Jones (16). 49, Lei Oester-et.. were charged with committing wilful damage to a hence No. 68 Plerpoint-st.. the property of Robert Isher- wood co the 26th ult. Both youths pleaded guilty to kick- ing the door of the hon-e. —P C. Corbett stated that from comp'aints received he had carefully watched unoccupied property in Pierpoint-et. He llv.d in the same street, aryl oa the date named he heard someone kicking a door. He went out and saw the two defendants kicking the door of an unwympled house. On seeing witness they ma away but he succeeded in catching the elder defendant. Witness asked him why he had broken the canal of the door la. and he replied that he had only put his foot against it. There had been great complaints of dam mge being done to unoccupied property IR the neighbour- hood. There were notices op Ist the houses warn- ing people 'against it. Mr. Isherwood said he es- timate' the damage done to his promsertv during the past six months would be £3OO The damage dome by the defend- ants was 93 6d.—The Chief Constab'e a mid the present was by no means an isolated case. Hem had received complaints from all parts of the town —Mr. Isherwood said fisore. cue- boards, bannisters, and even boilers had been removed from unoccupied houses—The defendants were tined 2s. 63. and ordered to pay the cost of the damage.</p>
        <p>TB F FXAMiNEK.,</p>
