        <p>following rersoas were dealt with for being dru-,k and dieo-derly in d fi!,!rent townships ;—Patrick Murnhv and 10s. for committing the offen^e in Zurton- wood ; John Jones, Sankey. finei 55.; Daniel Mane!). But- tonwood, fine-I 6s. inoltrilevr nosts. and John Haverty, Buttonwood fined 2s. 6d. end cast.; Paul nallwell, Ithaca, was finedss for being drunk there on September 19. A SLEEPY Diuvvn.—David Washington was eharged with being drunk in eharge of a horse and cart at Sankey on the lgth_sept amber -P.O. nizzdale ptoved the case and defend- ant awls fined lOs inclniing costs. _</p>
        <p>A FR ATIDULE‘T TRA.VELLER.-111rasmns Barnum, of Croft, was charqed with trayellino, on the Meshire Lines Railway from Mannhastrr to Pidgate nn the 22nd kugust wlthlut hay- ing paid his fare —Detectlya-Sancrintendent Simpson prose- cuted oa behalf of the Cana pany and said that _defendant on</p>
        <p>. _ the date in question arrived at Padote by the 5.3 train from Manchester. and while passing the porter gave up a tick-t in a very dilapidated nonditinn. The porter was not satisfied and asked for his name. Ilerendant gave the name of Brown. Ile then went hut the stationmaster sent the porter after him. Ths porter again asked derendant for his name and he then gave that of Barnum which the porter found to be true. A &amp;tective was informed, and he ques- tioned def‘ndant who stated that he get the ticket. lii the Previous June. They would prove, however, that the ticket was issued in April Ml.—Peter Monks, porter at Pad- gate. con boraterl Mr. gimpson's staternimt.—P.O. • ohnson stated that he travelled by the same train and saw defendant enter it at Manchester and leave it at Pa Igate --john Bowyer tic'&lt;et clerk near Manchester, produced the ticket book and show .d that the ticket in question was issued on the 15th ril, 11t14. It bad never been cellected and bad been in someone's possess on on all the time.De!endant stated that about June this year h was working in Manchester and was coning home one niet as it rained We bolght a ticket and then round that the rain had ceased so he stopped in Manchester. On the 22nd Au'rust ha %aged the ticket as he , hsd paid fn- it Ile denied th,t the ticaet was issued in 1894 —The rnaq,istrates considered that it was a clear nase of travelling with af.ilse ticket and fined defendant 20s. and costs.</p>
        <p>APR' VOlOll ‘SSEITLT —William Mather. Warrington. was cha-ged with having on the 4th October assaulted J Aostin in Vfnolston.—Defendant admitted strikingcomplaio- ant under greet or,vocation.—Complainant stated that on Sunday night he was goiog home from the "Dog and Part.. idc" when defendant. ran at him and knocked him sense- less, for what. r.asnn he did not know. He had n black eye in consequence —Defendant stated that on the night in qeestion w^s in the" Dog and P rtridge " and defendant ant a man named Di son were also there. Dixon insulted him repeated'y. Comolainant and Aixen went out tater on and about 10 minutes after he (defendant) went out honing to avoid them. They caw him and Dixon again made some Insulting remark. Defendint struck him and complainant then kicked defendant on the shin. He (defend nt) then struck complainant..—The magi.trttes said defendant had taken the law into his own hand. more than he ought to have done and fined him 10a including costs USING TAKEtTe —Thomas Blears,was charged with threat- ism to kill Ann Blears on the let Octobsr.—Complainant stated that defendant. was looking for herearrned with a sledee hammer with which he intended to smash her. She was ! afraid of him :—Camplainant's daught-r corroborated. and ! defendant wa bound aver to keep the peace and find two ' sureties of £5 each. ' 4 CARTER VIIVED. —Mark Wood. carter, was fined 10s, for being 4') rird• front his horse and cart in Rixton. on the 17th Sentember, and thus tpving no control over it. limeys' PENALTY POR CRUELTY TO • HonsE.--Richard. Mason was chine&amp; with having illtreated a horse by working it in an unfit state in Sankey on the 10th September John Tholes Was charged with causing it to be worked.—lnspec- tor Bilson. HR P 0.4 prosecute4.—Mr. F. Yerfoot stated Chas he saw the defendant near the Wire Works in Sankey en the dite named. He WAR in charge of a ho-se and a limy of flower pats. He noticed the htrse was distressed ant examined it. It was'.auffering from two wounds on each should-r and in rreat nuin. Defendant told him it was bad all over.—James Randall. his driver, Warrington, stated that he also saw the horse at the "Black Fnrae " where defendant told him it was very hui, It had bad wounds on the shoulders and witness made a pad. - insne,tor Bilson also &amp;scribed the horse. /idling that it was 'ante in both fore'egs from ringbene. Jigsaw Davies who said he had had a lot of trouble with the animal. He was sending it however from Runcorn to Rale. about 30 miles.—Davies was fined £2 and costs and Mason ss. Including costs.</p>
