      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>y, OCTOBER 10, 1896.</head>
        <p>y, OCTOBER 10, 1896.</p>
        <p>FOOT B ALL.</p>
        <p>RUGdY. WARRINGTON V. NIORECAMBE.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday the I.lorecamlee team paid their first visit to Warrington, and the occasion was signalised by the opening of the new grand stand by the Mayor. His Warship then entered the field and opened the game by kicking off for the visitors, who de- fended the pavilioe goal.. •There was no wind so that the teams were on an equal footing in both halves of the game. They w.‘re composed as follows :—Warrington : Heesom, fall hick ; Lewless, Burton, Cross, and Barber, three-quarters; Foden and Ilnughton,half-hacks ;Nevins, Dakin. Barrett, Schnitz., Burrows, Higham, Swift, and Tnorntoie forwards. Morecambe: Elkin, full back ; R. Holmes, ll'ae,ley.Gariiner, and Hadwen,threequarters; Blacow and Campbell, half-backs ; Batty, Benson, Watts Wilson, Cornthwaite..l. Elotrnes, G H Owen, and Hay- ton forwards. When Burton stepped out of the dress- ing-room he was received with hearty cheers which car- ried the pleasure felt by some 5,003 spectators at his re- turn to football. Bete was absent owing to an injured finger, his place tieing taken by Foden. After the kick off Houghton returned well. Burton eves immediately prominent with tackling, followed by one of his useful kicks. A scr mmaee took place and the ball came out at the Warrington end,Foden securing and passing to Bu.ton, who after a good run transferred to Cross, the latter being tackled. Another scrimmage fol- lowed, and Houghton threw to Burton, who missed the pass. This brought Mnrecamhe dawn with a rush, but Boron raced ahead and assisted Heesom in the defence. The home custodian planted the leather high in touch. The line out caused another scrimmage from which Blacow affected a smart kick. Heesom, however,secured and again sent the ball beyond the flags. Offside plaig by the visitors gave Wirrineten a free kick. Burton took the place, and though he failed to put the bell•through the posts his kick was particularly useful as the homes- ters followed it to the corner. Here Barber and Burton exchanged, the latter crossing the field but slip- ping into touch. From the ensuing scrimmage Cross had a grand opnertunity which he failed to make use of. Some PXchmtee kicking was then wit- nessed, Burton securing the advantage. A bout of pass- ing between Foden. Houghton and Cross was then witnessed, end resulted in Cross increasing the hold or the homeraters by a line kick. Up to this time Warrington had been pressing the visitors on their line continually but Maces., now got another chance and he transferred to Hadwin, whe crossed the halt-way line before Burton shoved him into touch. A weak pass from Houghton called upon Barber to save, which he did by a kick. Blacow then attempted to gain ground by kick- ing. but Cross took the ball and carried it well up the field before going in touch. Here Foden punted and the homesters following up smartly. Elkin only just managed to kick in touch. The hall as smartly hounded into olay,and Nevins snapped it un and got over at the cern?, very cleverly. Thornton failed at the kick. Barbee made his mark twice on the re-start and the emend time put his team again on the aggressive. , While Ira front of the Morecambe goal Warrington re- ceived another free- kick.- and Barber's attempt was a near shave. Immediately after flcughton found touch but the visitors retaliated by smart passing between Blacow, Blaeley,and Had win. the latter of whom gained ' some valuable emend. Both Cross and Efsesom man- aged to get a good kick in and then the home enthus- iasm was aroused by brilliant passing all along the bac k division to Lawless, but he had the misfortune to Rtum ble and the ball went into touch once more. The visitor - then gave a creditable display of passing but nothin came of it. Burton than won for himself a cheer by ree ceiving a pass and being hemmed in,delivered one of hie inalvelloue screw kieke which reached touch right in the corner. From the line out Cross punted over the line and following up rapidly he caused Elkin to kick dead. Play still waged in the visitors' quarters but the defence was good, Blacow kicking very effectively on occasions. Several inadvertent kneeke on be the Warrington backs spoiled some promisinq runs and half-time arrived with the score reading, Warrington 1 try (3 points), More- cambe. nil. Nevins cemmeneed• the second half and Blag.,,ley made a good return. More passing along the Warrington three-quarter line was .vitnessed only to he spelled by Lawless mulling. Campbell next secured from the pack and was making off when Foden ponnced down upon him and held him fast. • Again the visiters secured and a grand punt reached Holmes who snapped up the ball and made for the line with the field at his heels. He crossed the coveted line and was in the act of touching down when Nevins interposed his foot and Rent the ball to the rails. This exciting bit of play livened both teams up and some good passim; and tackling was seen. Morecambe again looked dangerous and Campbell had hard lines in knocking on at a critical moment. The leather then travelled to Holmes again but before he could get going Bur- ton laid him out. Blacow put in another kick which found. touch and' moment later Burton was called upon to stop a quick rush. Another of his kicks altered the complexion of thino once more. Foden suo- plemented the eff art with similar tactics and Cross pre- vented a return. Werrineton were now Close to the line and the ball was kicked into the hands of Scholtze who had a clear course. HP was not quite quick enough be- ing dragged down on the corner flag. Barber, who had gone centre, tackled splendidly several times and Foden made a short dodgy run before being pulled dnwn. Hee- sem put in another effective kick and the visitors were forced to concede another minor. Soon after. Cross got the ball and made the run of the afternoon. He had passed all but E'kin and seemed to have a clear course when he took it into his head to pass to Foden. The latter mulled and a splendid chance was lost. Holmes • put his side on the offensive by kicking hut Barber gained ground once more ny a run to midfield. Great determination was shown by the sea-siders however and Hadwen had just got nicely going when Lawless threw him into touch. Scholtze charged a kick down and the ball was being followed up when Elkin made an excel- lent and useful mark. Once again Burton was seen to [ advantage. He tork a nags from Houghton and skilfully eluded several opponents before eventually being grassed. ; Elkin:intercepted a Warrington pass butißarber"downed" I him before he could utilise it. This brought the game to ' a close leaving Warrington victors for the first time this ; season by 1 try to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>WIDNES v. RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>WIDNES v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Played at the Lower-house enclosure on Satnrday afternoon.before about 7.000 spectators. In the Widnes ranks appeared a new player in the person of Baty, who had been brought from Tvnedale. W. Faulkner, Run- corn's captain, won the toss, and Barber set the ball in motion. Smart following up by Forster prevented any return, and the first scrimmage was formed in the Run- corn half. Some passing, however, by the visitors relieved matters somewhat, hut Runcorn were nonalizod and Drummond sent to touch at the visitors' 25 flag. A grand run by Butterworth transferred play to the home territory, but his final pass went forward. The Cheshire men had so far the best of the scrimmages, their half- backs being in possession very frequently and ,iving the three-quarters some good chances. Warder was applauded for a fine piece of work, which caused the Widnes territory to be the scone of hostilities. The Runcornians, however, were again penalized, and by this means Widnes got back to half-way. A rush by the visiting forwards almost ended disastrously for Widnes, Baty slipping down in fielding the ball, but Martin got up in the nick of time and kicked dead. From the drop out the centre of the field was the scene of hrm• tilities for a short time. The oval was sent to Hough- ton, who fielded the ball, but even after be had cleared, Barber tackled the visiting full-back in a forcible man-</p>
        <p>nPr, and a struggle on the ground between the two players ensued in the course of which blows were ex- changed. The referee seeing them rolling about on the ground stooped the game. went to them, and the result was that both of them were ordered from the field.</p>
        <p>Even after thia Barber maa seen to acina.re up to lionqh-</p>
        <p>ton. Thr incident was of a roost refirettabfe character. Butterworth took up the position of custodian. The home team were penalized for a foul throw in, and Faulkner had an attempt at goal hut his kick was</p>
        <p>charged down and the ball sent to half-way. The game hereabouts was livened up by a passing movement by the Runcorn hacks, wbich transferred the game to the home twenty-five, where several scrimmages took place near the posts. Warder made an ineffectual attempt at goal from a fairly easy position, the ball going under the bar, and A moment later Faulkner had a similar attempt from a penalty. The Widnes lines had another very narrow eseane, the bail only just missing by inches. The Runcornians at this time were having the major portion of the game, and it was only the fine defensive tactics of the home hicks which prevented a score being recorded. Flampson got away for Widnes, and kicked down the field. hnt, Butterworth returned to touch, and St moment later Richardson was almost over after a fine run by Robinson. A penalty to Runcorn in iron.: of the home posts was, luckily for Widnes. charred down, and be means of hard work the Widnesians re- moved play to the half-way. Pendleburry and Warder were to the fore, however, with a neat nagging run, which caused the hiamestera some anviety, Nevitt even- tually kicking to the quarter line. Robinson got away with a good run, and passing to Richardson at the right moment the latter scored near the corner, Faulkner mak- ing a good but unsuccessful attempt at goal. Half time arrived with the score Runcorn, one try; Widnes nil. Faulkner restarted for Runnorn, Baty returning to touch at half-way. From the ensuing scrimmage Drummond was conspicuous with neat kick to touch, which gained a lot of eround for his side. The visitors were penalised for off-side in the scrimmage. and Baty made TRIED AND PROVED!</p>
        <p>Sufferers from Gr vel, Lumbago, Piles, Pains in the Baez Dropsy. Wind and Water Complaint, Diseases Kidneys Bladder Ctone, Sciatica. Rheumatism and Gout will find a pogitive Cure In Holiroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a small box and If not satisfied your money will be returned. Prices is. 10., Of all Chemists, or post free for 12 stamps. from HOLDROYD'S Medical Hall Checkhe,ateu., 'Yorks. Sold at 35, Bridge-st., Warringtca. Don't be put off, if you cannot get them write tothe Proprietor and :a box will be sent next post Adyt</p>
        <p>a good attempt at goal from near half way, Butterworth touching down. From the drop out Forster made .a mark, and kicked to touch at he Runcorn quarter flag. The visitors' backs attempted a passing movement, which was spoiled by a throw forward,and then Farmer gained some ground for his side for a kick to touch. Faulkner kicked down the field to Rispan, who marked and gained some ground with a long kick to touch,Nevitt supplementing his partner's effort with some neat kick- ing to the Runcorn quarters. As the result ef a fine run and kick by Warder,the home defence was severely taxed, aud Shepperd twice made bold attempts to score. The tensiOn was somewhat relieved by a free kick being awarded to Widnes, which wag taken toy Drummond who found touch near the centre. Kicking between Elaty and Warder ended in the latter finding touch in the horw. quarters. After a 'number of scrimmages on the home lines Drummond brought relief, and play by the home team further improved matters. Rispan gaining possession, ran some distanne with the ball, and supple- mented with a kick, which went over the bar, a feat which was succeded by loud and continued cheering. The hostilities were continued with a vast amount of eagerness, and repeated visits were made into each other's territory, but the defence on each side was im- penetrable. The game ended—Widnes,a dropped goal; Runcorn, one try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>SECOND DIVISION.</head>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN V. Wrreens " A" TEAMS.—At the Irwell-lane enclosure,Runcern,on Saturday afternoon before a sparse attendance. The following were the teams :—Runcorn: Jolley, back; Myers' Gregory, Wilkinson, and West, three-quarter backs;Nickson And Wood svard,half-backs ; Walker, Johnson' Woods, Richardson, T. Reece, Shaw, Antrobus, and Shaw, forwards. Widnes : Grayson, back : Peters, Hickep. Percival and Wheat, three-quar- ters backs; Murphy, and Douglas, half-backs; Aepey, Travis, Hughes, Davies, Ford, Acton. Legshon, and McCabe, forwards. Antrobus started, and Widnes at once assumed the agressive. Murphy and Douglas feed- ing their three-quarter line, but Nickson saved very cleverly, Walker giving relief with a. judicious kick, and Gregory following up tackled Peers before he could re- ply. A moment later, following some kicking between the respective backs, Jolley was at fault, and Widnes had an easy opening but mulled the chance. Still press- ing, the visitors had another opportunity,Gregory being forced to kick over his:own line. From the ensuing pack Nickson gained a let of ground, and Woodward initiated a round of passing, but Gregory failed to get through. A lot of give- and-take pity ensued at centre, • and it was evident that the forwards would monopolize the game. Widnes again got into the home quarters, but good foot Work by the home forwards took play to centre where Myers got possession, and seemed like getting through, but slipped.. Toe game progressed very quickly, Grayson. Jolley, West and Antrobus being very prominent for their respective sides. Following some loose play the ball was kicked to Jolley, who stood wall- ing to receive it, when he was foully charged by Douglas but happily the incident passed off by a caution from the referee. On the interval arriving nothing had been scored, the game being very even. Yercival restarted, Woodward returning, but the visitors got the best of the initial kicking. Gradually, the Widnessans cleared them out, and during some loose play Douglas got bold, and passing when close to the line to one of his colle,agues the latter had no difficulty in getting over. Runcorn played up strongly after this reverse, Nicklori and Antrobus especially being prominent, but the Widnes defence was strong. Just before time was called the ball was kicked over the home line, and West in trying to kick the ball dead had the misfortune to kick it more towards the field of play, with the result that a lucky try was scored, a goal a,,ertsiosf from it, the foal result being Widnes, two goals ; Runcorn, nil.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RECRTIO v. BROUGHTON Rise REATION MANCRESTRR) . —The Recreation travelled to Broughton, ' on Satutday. Whitney won the toss for the visitors,and the home captain kicked off. The Runcorn meta com- menced in splendid style and Sutton and Burnmerfield at half-back got the ball away to the three-quarters re- peatedly. Ihe home backs had to be reckoned with, and offered a stout resistance to the Runcorn onslaught. The game was very well contested and up to half-time neither aide had any advantage. The second portion of the gains was of a ftuctating character, the play at times being brilliant, while at another time it went very poor. The Runcorn men were the agressors at the start, but were reoulsed, and the hone side put in a lot of tall kicking and gave the visiting backs plenty of work. Ailey was equal to the emergency, and returned the bat to touch time after time, when the least slip on his part would have been a certain score for his opponents. Thel Runcorn men again catne to the Broughton end, but try as they could they were unable to break through the home team's defence,Melia on one occasion almost doing the needful, and Wright also had hard lines with a drop at goal, the ball going a few inches under the bar. The home men at length relieved and play settled in mid- field, and ween the referee called time a very enjoyable and even gatne resulted in a draw, neither aide having , scored.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>WA.RRINGT.)N ST. MA.IIIB v. ST. RELPINS RECS. " A."— Theif• teams met for the first time on St. Mary's ground, Warrington. St. Helens winning the toss Caldwell started, a good return being made. The Saints were awarded a free kick and soon after they almost scored, EUgginhottoni kicking t o hard,the ball going dead. St. Helens were afterwxrds given d free kick for a knock on ;.but soon aster Rutter got possessiou,and some good passing by the Saints' backs was witnessed, the ball be- ing carried from end to end of the field. Half time was called with no sire. From the restart the Rempressed but Higginhottom r,alieved with a good kick, and play Was again in the visitors' quarter, but gradually the Rees. got to the Saints' line, pressing the home team. The rest of the 2atrie was at the centre, and from a free kick Sidclell almost landed a goal from a long range. After a good game th, match was drawn with no score.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>MEETING OF THE NORTHERN UNION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>MEETING OF THE NORTHERN UNION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>2. meeting of the committee of The Northern Rugby Union was held on Tuesday evening at the Spread Eagle Hotel, Manchester. Mr. J. E. Warren' of Warrington, presided. On the motion if Mr. Rothwell, a resolution we); passed making the rule under which a fine is in- fii :ted for late atarts in leaguelcompetitions applicable to all matches played under the auspices of the Union.--A sub-committee was appointed to report to the general committee on the question of the transfer of players from one club to another with a view to the matter being brought eventually before a special general meeting of the Union. The gentlemen appointed to serve on the sub..emnfoittee were Mersrs. Hutchinson, Fattorini, and Waller (Yorkshire). and Messrs. Warren. Houghton, and Platt (Lancashire). It was also rf solved that in inter- county matches for two successive seasons the gate- money should be equally divided between the competing coun rie W. H. Bairstow was appointed referee for the Lancashire v. Cheshire match, and W. Dillon for the Yorkshire v. Cheshire match.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>TRE lANCASHIRE SECTION.</head>
        <p>TRE lANCASHIRE SECTION.</p>
        <p>A mp.ting of the committee of the Lancashire section of the Northern Union was afterwards held, and con- sider,d the applications of various clubs for the tranfer of players. Mr. J. H. smith (Widnes) presided. In foer instances the committee did not feel justified in grant- ing transfers, and thr players concerned were the two Salforl new half backs, B. Griffiths, of Peny-graig, and Ivor Grey, of Morriston ; R. F. Batey, of Tynedale, who has gone to Widnes ; and J. A. Mulligan, of Bective Rangers (Dublin),te Swinton. When the committee are satisfied that under their rules these players are in situa- tions, they will be granted their transfers accordingly. Permits were granted to J. H. Turner, J. Goodall, W. Johnson, J. Fitchett, R. R. Goodall. and J. Hibbert to play with Stockport; J. Hindley and H. Hindley with Leigh ; T. Lee and J. Barton with St. Helens ; B. Evans, H. Webster, and H.Sinclair.with Wigan ; and D.Rnight, P. Wilkinson. and C. H. Walkden, with Swinton; and W. J. Radford, with Oldham. A number of late starts were reported, and penalties imposed as follows :—Run- Corn A, 10 minutes late against Tyldesley A, 10s. ; Broughton Rangers v. Rochdale Hornets, 10 minutes late, 10i. ; St. Helens V. Rochdale Hornets, 15 minutes late, 15s. ; St. Helens Av. Salford A, 35 nainutes late. 355. ; iVarrington A v.Stockport A,28 minutes late.2Bs.; and Wilnes A against Rochdale Hornets A, 20 minutes late, 20s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. LI;R—RPOOL CASUALS. —The Warrington A.F.C. opened their seas 'n against the Liverpool Casuals Club, who have formerly beaten the local team three times,but on Saturday at Warringtoe, in dull weather, the locals notched their first victory. Connor having won the toss the Casuals kicked off towards the station goal.and after some good passing by the front rank opened tne scoring account, Crompton putting a " beauty" out of Webster's reach. Tlsis roused the home team to their work, and after some really tricky work by E. C )nnor and Selby, Carroll was afforded an easy chance,bnt shot over the bar. Warrington continued to press,but could not break through the visitors' defence, Woodruffe at back and Hughes in goal saving splendidly and the interval arrived with the score—Casuals, one goal ; Warringtonenil. On restarting Warrington con- tinued to play scientifically, the way in which the half backs fed their forwards being a treat to witness, these efforts being rewarded after about ten minutes' play by Williamson evalising after a beautiful dribble by Selby. The game continued fast,the ball travelling from goal to goal at a tremendous pace, and had it not been for the grand play of Webiter in goal and Broughton and Lina- ker at back,the visitors would have ganed the upper hand. Just before the close Selby scored the winning goal for Warrington, who retired winners by 2 to 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>CYCLE-CKA.</head>
        <p>CYCLE-CKA.</p>
        <p>The finsst embrocation in the world. Promptly restores tone to the tired or strained muscles. Made by the Elomocea Company, Limited, and put up in collapsible tubes, price 6d. ant is. 6d. each Sold by —Mr. J. R. YOUNG, Pharmaceutical chemist, Bankey-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>THE NEW GRAND STAND ON THE</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL FIELD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>OPENING BY TIIE MAYOR.</head>
        <p>OPENING BY TIIE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>The Mayor (Alderman Fairclough) at the invitation of tha Warrington Football Club, opened the new grand stand which has been erected on the Wild-rspool enclo- sure on Saturday afternoon. He was accompanied by Mr. Thomas Morris (president of the club), Aldermen Hutchinson and Greenall ; Councillors Roberts, Rogers, Shavi, Bennett, Woods,lcock, Lee, Evans,Tinnion, Monks, Wilkinson, Plumpton, Smethurst, ; Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Mr. R. T. Fairclough, Mr. R. I. Wynne-Jones, Mr. Luke Talbot (chief constable),lnspec- tor Spinks, and others. A procession was formed and proceeded to the new stand, which does credit alike to the builder and the club. To signalise the event the Mayor was asked to kick off. This he suocessfu ly did,and afterwards,made haste for the new stand agaiu, accompanied by Mr. Morris.</p>
        <p>A short description of the new stand which with other improvements has cost the club over £5OO, may not be out of place here. The new structure, situated on that side of the field nearest to the Brewery, is 100 feet in length, and exclusive of the paddock, will comfortably accommodate 700 people. The paddock itself will easily accommodate 600 people, and the whole being covered with corrugated iron, the habitues of Wilderspool have the satisfaction of knowing when the weather is of an inclement nature the sport can be wit- nessed under agreeable conditions. The centre portion of the stan.t is reserved for honorary members, officials of the club, and members of the press. The roof is 22 feet in height,sloping to 15 feet at the rear,supported by seven iron pillars. The whole is surrouuded by an orna- mental border, whilst the sides are formed of glass. Another feature is to be seen at the approach to the ground. Here about sixty feet have been sliced off the lower ground to all iw all the turnstiles being brought togethersmaking one common entrance to the ground, so that the approach will certainly have an inviting ap- pearance. The whole of the alterations have been made from drawings submitted by Mr. Percy Si!cock, A.R.1.5.A., of Egypt-street, Warrington and Mr. Dave- nport, of Stockton Heath, has been the builder. After the match the Mayor, accompanied by several members of the Town Council and officials at the Foot- ball Club,assemfil d at the Norton Arms Hotel,the head- quarters of the club. Mr. Morris proposed the toast of "I he Mayor and Corporation." They were, he said, much gr • tified by the action of the Mayor and Corpora- tion in coming there that day.—The Mayor,in reply,said he could only say how pleased he .was to have opened the new stand that day. He was highly gratified at the improvements that had been made in the fooball ground generally, and especially at the action ot the cninmittee in setting hack the fence of the field. He wished all suc- cess to the Warrington Football Club. They payed fair and square,ane it they were beaten he hoped they would taks it all in good part. (Applause) The Mayor then proposed the health of Mr. Thomas Morris, thepresident, who,as they all knew, as thp first to interest himself in any improvement that was for the good of the working- men.—Mr. Morris having responded in suitable terms, Councillnr Evans,one of the officials of the clab, at Mr. Morris's recpest made a statement as to the amount of money wl,ich had been snent on the work. The amount of the contract was £367. The. contract for the setting back si the fence was £125 10s., and altogether there had been spent between £520 and £530. The commit- tee had given to the club £2O towards the setting tack of the fence.</p>
        <p>MRS. MAYBRICK.</p>
        <p>THE LATE SUPERINTENDENT BRYNING AND THE CASE.</p>
        <p>• Regarding the statement recently circulated to the effect that Mrs. Maybrick's friends are making another effort to obtain her release en the basis of something alleged to have bc-en divulged by Superintendent Bryning before his death, Mrs. Bryning, his widow, has been seem as to the truth or otherwise of this statement. Her answer is an unqualified denial that her husband nad seen any friends ot Mrs. May brick, or that ho had any- thing to communicate beyond what came out at the trial. She added—what is well known to everyone with whom he came into professional or social contact—that his integrity w-is such that he cou:d not, in fairness to the prisoner,have withheld any information which could have operated in her favour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>NARROW ESCAPE OF A GENERAL' DAUGHTER.</head>
        <p>NARROW ESCAPE OF A GENERAL' DAUGHTER.</p>
        <p>LIEUT.-GENERAL SHAW, the well-known 'lndian veteran, whose daughter had so narrow an escape of death, has been interviewed by the Hampshire Independent. General Shaw was living at a vicarage in the Isla of Wight. The home is the model of a haven of rest. Enclosed in its own grounds, it rests beneath the shadow of the village church. In the trees were nests of sweet songsters, who are never disturbed. The General would be recognised as a soldier at once. His manly, upright beating, his smile, his pleasant voicr—all told you that you stood in the presence of one of Nature's gentlemen. " I explained to the General "(the reporter writes) " that I desired, with his clnielt, to obtain some ex- planation a 3 to the narrow esc .pe of his daughter. "At that he brightened visibly. 'You must know,' he said, 'l'm jat a bit of an enthusiast On this point; but the tale is very short. Nearly two years ago my daughter—the one you referred to—came home from India, and when I joined her in London I found her ill in bed. She had rheumatic and neuralgic pains; she , was perfeely bloolless, listless, and in a generally weak and prostrate condition. We called in a doctor, and I I am bound to say that he succeeded in getting her out of bed; but she was still absolutely colourless, was in great wretchedness, and suffering from what I should call anemia or bloodlessness. She had a kind of fuer nervous heaflache, and other pains. Well, we went to the Isle of Wight, and there I first heard of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. My daughter used the first box, and they had a marvellous effect upon her. She , regained her colour; she lost her pains, and became al- together different; and after she had been taking them some time she came in one day after her walk aud said to me" Why, father, I feel quite a glow througn me.' She went on taking the Pills, and I am glad to tell you that she recovered completely. I recommended Dr. Williams' P;rik Pills to all with whom I came in 'contact, and all who took them derived great benefit therefrom. I myself, when I have heard of people being ill, have taken them or sent them some of these Pills</p>
        <p>44 , With what result ' I a,kad. " 4 Well,' was the reply, my daughter's recovery was due to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People with- out a doubt. Their effects were marvellous, and I did not speak without first ascertaining the facts. I have had a great deal to do with medical men in this country and in India, and they laugh at the idea that patent medicines cure any disease. But I have given you this case, and that I know; I won't deal with any beyond my own parconal experience and knowledge, further than to say that the results have been satisfactory.' "'Then I may say, General, that your daughter's recovery was due to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills?' " ,Decidedly,' replied the General. 'land, as I said. I have heard of other cases of a similar character, but in- to them I will not. go, as I prefer only to tell you what I know to be:true from my own personal observation."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>EIGHTEEN MONTHS AFTER.</head>
        <p>EIGHTEEN MONTHS AFTER.</p>
        <p>It may be interesting, and is certainly important, to record that this remarkable cure proved permanent. The events described in the above interview occured a year and a half ago. Miss Shaw has been personally visited at her father's house in Sr. Leonardo, within. the last few days, and remains in robuot and perfect health.</p>
