      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>CHEMISTRY OF FOOD.</head>
        <p>THE CHEMISTRY OF FOOD.</p>
        <p>The second lecture on "The Chemistry of Foods "was delivered last week, by Mr. F. G. Ruddock, F.J.C., lecturer on Chemistry in the School of Science. Tee Lecturer began by describ- ing the growth of vegetation and the dependence of animal and vegetable growth on each other. After ex- hibiting the properties of carbonic acid, on which plants depend tor their supply of carbon, tables were shown giving the composition of the ash of wheat grains and the straw and chaff, from which it appeared that the phosphoric acid, lime and potash which are necessary for buildi ig up the animal body are concentrated in the grain. This led to a short explanation of the rotation of crops and the chief sources of supply of these substances. The importance of a supply of nitrogen was hen dealt with and the action of certain bacilli in tilled soil in bringing about a combination of nitrogen with oxygen producing nitrates and nitrites in which form the nitrogen is assimilated by the roots. Passing on to the food of man and alter describing the action of pepsin secreted by the coat of the stomach in rendering food soluble, which is then carried by the blond to different parts of the body, the lecturer proceeded to classify the essential constituents of food under three beads—albuminoids,fate and carbohydrates. The albumineids contain nitrogen and are used to carry on the vital functions of the body,tbe most concentrated form being the flesh of animils, albumen or white of ' egg,casein the albuminoid in milk and the chief consti- tuent of cheese,and fibrin ons of the albuminoid bodies present in blood were described. Albutninoids were not confined to the animal kingdom but were present in fl .ur,erassoke,. The chief sources of supply and prepar- ation for food of fats including butter and marearine, and the carbohydrates. star( h, glucose, and cane sugar were described arid a table exhibited giving the compo- sitien of whole wheat,fiae fl oar, oat meal and potatoes. From this it appeared that oa, meal containing album- minoids 16 per cent.. starch 63 Lae. cent. and fats 10 per cent. wo e the most nourishing, and attention was drawn to the composition of whole wheat and fine flour which differed very little from each other, the lecturer giving the preference to fine flour. The preparation of starch from potatoes, the conversion of starch into glucose, which is largely used as a substitute for honey, by heating with dilute acid, and the cain sugar industry were then dealt with. In the latter connection it apreared that the percentage of sugar in the beet had risen by cultivation from 2to 15 per cent., and that whereas formerly it required 18los of beet-root to make lib of sugar now it only requires 71lbs. From a series of interesting tables the lecturer then went on to show the amount of nutriment and the effects of different kinds of cooking upon food stuffs. The dietetic value of meat was very little altered by cook- ing, and attention was duly laid on the importance of putting meat into hot 'water when required for eating and into cold water when imp is to be prepared from it.ln comparing the compos- ition of boiled beef with cooked beef steak the propor- tion of albuminoids was seen to be about the same, but in the latter the fats and extractive matters were higher, giving a better flavour to the meat. In conclusion,the minimum average daily amounts of albumen, fats and carbohydrates were given and the quantities of ordinary food stuffs that this corresponded to. To obtain the necessary amount of albumen,cheese beaded the list with O'elb. followed by peas and then meat, beer being at the bottom with 3/ gallons. To obtain the necessary quan- tity of carbon bacon came first with lib., followed by flour, rice and peas, &amp;c. A heap of charcoal was shown which was stated to be the daily minimum amount of carbon required to keep the heat of the reedy up. The lecture was illustrated with diagrams and experi- ments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>WARRINGTON ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ASSESSMENT COM- MITTEE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this committee was held en Friday un- der the presidency isf Mr. A. J. Jolley. There were also present: Messrs. Wm. Houghton, John Houghton, A. Jolley, Thos. Halton, and J. L. Tunstall (assistant over- sear), J. C. Sutton (clerk) and D. S. Stone (assistant valuer). The report of the valuer (Mr. Owen) was read which stated that since the last meeting he had made the following valuations in accordance with their instructions." Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons, Limited, stabling and stable court, Stamford-street: Grose £2BB, rateable £241; one cottage, £l2, rateable £10; one cottage, £l2, rateable £lO. Some additions were being made to the Dallam Brewery, but they were not suffi- ciently advanced for assessment.—Mr. James Horton, Sankey-street—At the last meeting Mr. Horton appealed , agaihst the following assessments: House and shop corner of Sankey-street and .Dixon-street, gross £6O, rateable £5l; bakery and stable, Dixon-street, grog' £56, rateable £47 10s. The appeal was referred to him (Mr. Owen) to report upon. He found that on the house and shop he had ground rent which exceeded that actually paid by £4 103., and on the stable a ground rent which exceeded that paid by £3. These amounts should be deducted, but Mr. Horton pressed for a lower assessment inasmuch as he was using his bakery at not more than perhaps one third of its capacity and the shops adjoining him were let on a sliding scale, those immediately adjoining him being at present at £42. If the assessments were watched by the overseers he saw no reason why the committee should not let Mr. Horton have a temporary reduction, making the shop and house, gross £53, rate- able £45; and the bakery, gruss £4l, rateable £35. Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Limited: There were so many alterations proceeding in these works that it would be useless to attempt to complete his plan for re-valuation at present.—Kr. James Ashley, of Latch- ford, appealed against his assessment for land in Wash. lane, gross £5, rateable £4 10s., and house, buildings and land in Wash-lane, gross £27 10s., rateable £25. Mr. Ashley had also an assessment in Latchford Without on land of gross £32, rateable £3O 10s., making a total of gross £64 10s., rateable £6O. Mr. Ashley was not aware of the acreage he occupied, but from the ordnance map, after taking the number of fields, he found that be occupied in the township of Latchford 241 acres,tcgether with house and buildings, whilst in the township of Latchford Without, he occupied only seven acres. He paid for the whole to the Ship Canal Company £57 per annum. There was evidently a discrepancy in the assessment. Taking the rent as gross annual value, he thought the assessment should stand as follows :—ln the township of Latchford-Within,house, gross £l9 ,rateable £l6; land and buildings 24i acres, gross £3O, rateable £27. in the township of Latchford-Without,land 7 acres, gross £B;ra.teable £7 103. This would show.a reduction in the whole of the assessments of gross £7 103., but it would raise the assessment in the borough from gross £32 103. to gross £49, and would reduce the assessment out of the borough from gross £32 ts gross £8 .—The report was adopted.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>During last week, which was the 39th, of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered :</p>
        <p>Births. Deaths. Warrington 40 18 Latchford</p>
        <p>Total 45</p>
        <p>The excess of deaths over births was 27. The death rats from all causes was equal to 16.1 per 1,000, against 13.1 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 42.8 the corresponding week of last year. The death rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 1.6 per :,000, against 3.0 the average rate of the four previous weeks,and against 245 the correspnnding week of 1895. The ages of mortality were— _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>0- 1 1-5- 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 5 1 2 1 9 - 18</p>
        <p>The deaths included-1 from diarrhcel,l from whoop- ing cough, 1 from puerperal septoecmia, 2 from cancer, 2 from phthisis, 1 from old age, 1 from lung disease. 2 from brain disease, 1 from liver disease, 44 km heart- disease. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The fonwing cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Erysipelas, 1 ease, scarlet fever, 1 case puerperal fever, 1 case. Total number of patients in hospital on Sept. 26th.-8</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>NOTHING NEW!</head>
        <p>NOTHING NEW!</p>
        <p>Professor Lombroso, in the" Contemporary Review," says : "It is curious to examine the inventions which we deem novelties, but which are in reality very old. The ancients knew of the lightning conductor, or at all events, the method of attracting the lightning. The Celtic soldieis in a storm used to lie down on the ground first lightning a torch and planting the naked swords in the ground by their side with the points upward. The lightning often struck the point of the sword was passed away into the water without injuring the warrior. "The Romans, also, seem to have known the lightning-rod, though they let their knowledge slip again into oblivion. On the top of the highest tower of the Castle of Duino, on the Adriatic, there was set, from time im- memorial, a long rod of iron. It the stormy weather of summer it served to predict the ap- proach of the tempest. A soldier was always stationed by it when the sea showed any threatening of a storm. From time to time he put the point of his long javelin close to the rod. Whenever a spark passed between the two pieces of iron he rang a bell to warn the fishermen. Gerbert (Hugh Capet), in the tenth century, invented a plan for diverting lightening from the fields by planting in it long sticks tipped with very sharp lance heads." Another case cited in the" Contemporary Review " is that: "In 1662 France was already in possession of omnibuses. Tne Romaus sank artesian wells even in the Sahara. The plains of Lebanon and of Palmyra were artificially irrigated ; traces of the wells and canals are still to be found. In 1685 Papin published in the Journal des Savants an account of an experiment made by one of his friends named Wilde, who caused flowers to grow instantaneously. The secret lay in thf, preparation of the ground, but it was not revealed. " Massage is a very ancient practice, and was known to the Romans. Paracelsus,in his' Opera Medica,' speaks of homceopathy,and says that like is cured by like, and not con, rary by contrary. ' Nature herself,' he sass, shows this,and like things seek and desire each other.' Polybius also speaks of healing by similarity, and Avi- cenna of the use of infinitesimal doses of poison, of arsenic. Mireppuss also used arsenic in infinitesimal doses as a remedy for intermittent fever. In China Cannubis Indica 'was used as a sedative 220 years be- fore our era. The Arabs used aloes and camphor as we do. The speculum,the probe, the forceps,were known in the year 500; indeed, specimens of them have been found in the ruins of Pompeii, and are preserved in the National Museum at Naples. Galande, in 1665, gives a theory of ps) chic centres, pointing out the anterior por- tion of the brain as the seat at imagination; the centre of reason, and the back of memory. Aristotle noticed that the sea-water could be Ina4le drinkable by boding it and collecting the steam. " The Greeks had a pileina,a woollen or linen cuirass,so wovan as to be impenetrable by the sharpest of darts. We have not found out the secret of it. The linmans had better mills than ours for pounding olives. The Chinese had invented iron houses as early as 1200. Glass houses wore found among the Picts in Scotland and the Celts in Gaul, and many centuries earlier in Siam. The systems of irrigation which made Lombardy and Eng- land so fertile wore in existence in the time of Virgil. Glass cloth was used many centuries ago by the Chinese. All this is explained by the fact that man naturally detests what is new, and tries his best to escape it, yielding only to absolute necessity and overpowering proof, or to an acquired usage."</p>
      </div>
