      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Letter t t etlitor,</head>
        <p>Letter t t etlitor,</p>
        <p>THE RECENT ELECTION OF ALDERMAN. I Sir,—l am informed that certain members of the Council, anxious, no doubt, to find an excuse for their dishonourable conduct on Tuesday last, have circulated a statement to the effect that the resignation of Alder- man Holmes was brought about by collusion on the part of the Town Clerk and myself; and further, these same individuals, whom, if a fitting occasion offers I will publicly expose, have made further assertions so utterly base that they could only have been conceived by men totally unaccustomed to any code of honour. I wish to brand the atatemPnt in question a s a deliberate false- hood, and to say further that although I have been approached on more than one occasion with a view to induce Mr. Holmes to resign, I have absolutely refused to do so or to be a party to procuring his retirement at any time or on any terms whatever. Further, when I attended the meeting held in the Council Chamber on Wednesday evening, 30th ult. (a meeting summoned by the Town Clerk, acting on the instructions of the General Purposes Committee which wet on the previous Monday),for the purpose of "select- ing the Mayor for the ensuing year and other business," I was in as complete ignorance of Mr. Holmes' resigna- tion as any other member of the Council. I know how difficult it is to overtake a lie when it is once started,but I think I may safely leave my fellow townsmen to chersse as to whether they will take my word on this matter or that of the men who made themselves so famous on Tuesday last. Finally, I do most sincerely regret that our Town Clerk to whom we owe so much for the zeal and ability with which he has unceasingly devoted himself to the interests of the town, should through the recent discreditable event have been caused so much undeserved pain and annoyance.—Yoars truly,</p>
        <p>HENRY ROBERTS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>WARRINGTON WOMEN'S LIBERAL</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WOMEN'S LIBERAL</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>INAUGURIL PUBLIC MEETING.</p>
        <p>On Monday the Warrington Women's Liberal Associa- tion opened its winter session by a public tea and meet- ing at the Liberal Club. Tea was served in the Com- mittee room at seven p.m. and no less than 81 ladies sat down at the tables. Soon after eight o'clock a public meeting was held in the reading-room. when Mrs. Z. Armitage presided. There was an excellent attendance all available sitting and standing space being occupied. A number of gentlemen were also present. Mrs. ARMITAGE. in opening the meeting. said she was sorry Mrs. Croefield was unable to be present and take the office of president. This was the inaugural meeting for the winter and it was a great encouragement to see such a large number present. (Hear. hear.) It was an encouragement to the workers, for if they were to make the association a power in the town they would need all to rally round them. (Applause.) There were many points upon which they all needed to be educated and Mrs. Sprunt had come to -peak to them on one of tjeose points. (Applause.) She had come from Birken- head and they were thankful to her for coming. (Applause.) Mrs. SPEUNT said she would " talk " to them on "Women's d ties at Municipal Elections." She would speak on two heads : "To know "and " To do;" know- ledge and action. She often wed to think in her younger days, when life was openine out, if one could only know what to do always it wonld be so easy to do it. She was not quite so sure about that now. Going back to the subject she said fwe ought to know. If they had asked a great many people 20 years ago what women should de about the Mentions they would have said, "Stay at heme and look after the dinner and tea" ; many people would say so yet. They should know something about politics. The word politics meant the well-being of a city. If we all lived in little huts by our- selves on desert islands there would be no politics. But wherever there was a community politics began to be , discussed. Mrs. Sprunt pointed out as an instance that ' if they were careful to see that no nuisance existed on their own premises and yet knew that there was one over the wall, it was their dute to see that it was abol- ished for the general benefit. Politics was the well-being of the city or the whole country and had women nothing to do with it? Why should they not know something about it? They were half the population of the coun- try. Before they could take any interest in municipal affairs or in the affairs of the country they must know about things. They must know the condition of their town and what was wanted ; what things were going on and what questions were going forward. (Applause.) It wee, very easy so be a Tory. They had nothing to do but say they were one and that was sufficient. If they asked a person why he or she was a Tory, they would say because my father was and all the family before me. (Laughter.) It was a good thine though that there were two parties. It kept the people from going on too fast and things were " hastening'slowly." (Applause.) The Tories were rather fond of adopting Liberal measures rtlele it suited them, and now they were in power • 4 hoe quite et pected thcm t.:) give thern'Women's Suffrage. Mrs. Sprunt then referred to the beer inter* and stig- matised it as the great blot in Liverpool. Two of the finest buildings there were given oat of money got out of the beer interest. They were cemented with the blood of wives and children whose lives had been ruined by drink. (Applause ) The speaker urged her hearers not only to know but to be enthusiastic. Their duties at municipal elections often begun and ended on elec- tion day. That was far too late. (Hear, hear.) Months ago that rushing about should have been done. She was sorry that canvassing should be necessary, and it seemed an anachronism when we had the ballot. (Hear, hear.) It seemed difficult to do without it and she had found canvassing very useful. To go and talk they had to know what they were talking about and give a reason for the faith thee was in them. (Applause.) They should be ready to answer on different points and they would find people ready to listen to them. Mrs. Sprunt further observed that they should all be true Liberal,' and be always educating those about them. Casual things they might say to their neighbours were useful. She also advised thoroughness in eanyessing. Bad canvass- ing was worse than no canvassing at all. One person could take ene street and see all the voters in it. It they were out call again but be sure to see them. A good deal of useful work could be done hr bringing up voters on election day. Women should make good canvassers because they were a little hit mere cheeky and could talk a bit more than men. (Laughter and anplause.) In Liverpool they were going to make a desperate effort to recover their position and she would look with interest at the results of the Viearrington elections. She thought meetines were a fallacy to a great extent. The people did plenty of shouting hut no work. Still meetings were good for rousing enthusiasm but often the people who shouted most worked least. (Applause.) She hoped that in Warrington the women would give a long pull and a strong pull and show splendil results. (Applause.) In the coming elections they would feel that it was woman's work and business to help the men, for she had as much at stake in the country as they had. (Applause.) Mrs. W. PIERPOPST proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Sprunt. and expressed her own pleasure at seeing so many friends with them that nie.ht. She heed the words spoken by Mrs. Sprunt would be considered, and that many of those present would become useful to the Association. They wanted someone to help and take an interest in them. They would he delighted if some of those present. not already members of the Association, would give in their names to any of the ladies who had been making tea that night. They did not want to in- terfere with the men but wanted to help them in any work becoming to them. They could help in the can- vassing. She had great pleasure in proposing a hearty vote of thanks to Mrs. Sprumt.—Mrs.Ceer seconded,and the vote was carried unanimously. Mrs. Sprunt cerdially reenonded. During the evening several 'tone; were given which added largely to the uniform success of the gathering. Miss Gornall rendered " Whisper and I shall hear " in a pleasing manner. An Irish song," Maureen," was very, well suns, by Miss Pieronint. who in response to an en- core gave" Sleep Derling Sleep." Mr. F. Fieldhouse also contributed a song and Mr. M. Butterfield recited "The Lifeboat" with considerable expression and skill. Mrs.H. Roberts proposed A vote of thanks to the ladies and gentlemen who had entertained them, and after be- ing seconded by Mrs. Redgere. the vote was heartily ac- corded. A ynte of thanks to Mrs. Armitage for presiding con- eluded the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCITF,S-WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCITF,S-WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>BEWSEY-ROAD WESLEYAN CFIURCH.</p>
        <p>On Sunday,the annual harvest festival in connection with the above place of worship was held. The chapel had been beautifully decorated for the occasion by several members of the congregation, including Mrs. Winstanlv, Mrs. Warburton, Mrs. Turner, and Mrs. Jubb. The special anthems sung were in the MOT/ling, "I will lift tin mine eyes," and in the evening " The eyes of all wait upon Thee." The pulpit was occupied at both services by the Rev. %% .G. who preached two good sermons. Collections were made in aid of the circuit funds. CHURCH OF THE SACRED HEART. 'The anniversary services in connection with the open- ing of the above church were held on Suuday when two sermons were preached by the Very Rev. Father Don- nelly. of St. Francis Xavier's Liverpool, to large con- gregations. Knight's Mass in C was efficiently rendered by the choir. The soloists were Miss l. Coggins, soprano, Miss A. Richardson, contralc9 ; Mr. George Newton, tenor ; Mr. George Coggin, bass. Novello's " Sancta Maria" was rendered by Mr. G. Newton as an offertory i piece. The Rev. Father Ryan was the celebrant n the mass. At the evening service there was a rosary ser- mon. Murphy's "Ave Maria" (vas sang by the choir in an able manner, and Miss E CotTgins was heard to advantage in the treble solo. Mr. John Mullins con- ducted the choir on both oceasioias. Miss M. Bellian pre- sided at the organ. ~;;;;:=id</p>
        <p>EA.RLESTOWN (IND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>TEA PARTY AND DANCE AT BIIRTONWOOD. On Thursday evening, the annual Michaelmas teaparty and dance in connection with St. Michael's Church, Burtonwood, was held in the Parochial Schools. The weather being propitious there was a large attendance, and the gathering was perfectly successful.</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S CHURCH, EARLESTOWN. The harvest thanksgiving services in ,:onnection with the above church, were brought to a close on Sunday, when the Rev. Dr. Reinsford. of Crewe, preached at both services to large congregations. In the afternoon there was a short service, and a selection of vocal pieces by the choir. The total collections which were in aid of the Curates' stipend Lund amounted to £27 12e. HARRIERS' CLUB AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>A harriers' club has been formed for Newton and district under the title of the "Makerfieid Harriers," and already 26 members have been enrolled. The first run took place on Saturday afternoon. Start- ing from the White Lion, Newton, the members ran on past Golborne to Ashton Heath returning by Ashton Churilh to Newton. The weather being fine the run was much enjoyed.</p>
        <p>SOCIAL EVENING. On Wednesday evening, the officers of St. John's Church together with the members of the choir were entertained to tea by the Rev. T. R. Matthews, in the Mission Hall,Earlestown.There were about 40 present, and the excellent tea provided was heartily enjoyed. After tea,the Vicar addressed a few words to those present, thanking the choir for their very punctual attendance during the harvest festival services. The remainder of the evening was passed in games and dancing. FATALITY ON THE RAILWAY NEAEL NEWTON. An inquest was held at Lowton on Monday by Mr. S. Brikhouse on the body of William Clayton, 28 years of age, a platelayers ganger on the Park- side section,and who resided at Preston Brook,who was killed on the railway on Friday afternoon. The deceased,it appears, was in a stooping position,lift- ing a sleeper. when the three o'clock express from Manchester to Liverpoo: came up and struck him on the head, killing him instantly. His injuries were of a shocking nature. A verdict of "Acciden- tal death" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLLRB.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLLRB.</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the Hope of Earlestown lodge ot Good Templars was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall on Monday evening. Bro. W. Wilson,C.T., presided, and there was a good attendance. Two candidates were admitted to membership. Arrange- ments were made for fraternal visit to Ashton Ex- celsior lodge. on Thursday. Bro. G Clarke reported on the arrangements made for celebrating the 24th anniversary on Saturday week. Sister Billinge of Bolton " Claremont " lodge was present as a visitor, and gave a few interesting remarks. It was resolved to send fra•,ernal greetings to Bolton " Claremont" lodge per Sister Billinge. The pro- gramme for the evening was " Musical evening." Bro. G. Clarke being responsible. A short pro- gramme of songs, &amp;c., was given by several of the members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN CHURCH..</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN CHURCH..</p>
        <p>In connection with the above, and in aid of the Contingent fund, special harvest thanksgiving ser- vices were held in the church on Sunday. The in- terior was very tastefully decorated with an abund- ance of fruit, flowers and vegetables, the decora- tions having been carried out by Miss Greenall,Mrs. Warburton, Miss Alderson,Mrs.Alderson,Mrs.R.Jones, Miss Clare,Miss Mee,Nlrs. Pearson,Nlrs.P. Parr, Miss Newman and Mrs. Rowlands. The pulpit at the morning and evening services was occupied by the Rev. J. Bartley, and at the morning service the choir sang Jackson's "Te Deum," a duet" Oh lovely peace," being given by Miss R. Alderson and 3liss F. Holliday. At the evening service the choir sang the anthem S' Oh God who is like unto thee." In the afternoon a sacred cantata " A daughter of Moab" was very effectively rendered by the choir under the conductorship of Mr. Thomas Clare. The principals were Miss May Clare, Ruth;" Mr. J. R. Barr," Boaz;" Mrs. G, W. Kidd, "Naomi;" the other soloists being Misses F. Holliday, Higgins, Berry, Mr. E. Clare, and Mr. W. Clare. Miss Greenall accompanied on the organ. The collections through- out the day amounted to £l2. SAD DEATH OF A BOY AT EARLESTOWN.—A FATAL RESULT OF LARKING. Much commotion was caused at Earlestown on Thursday morning by the death of a lad named George Linaker, 16 years of age, a turner's appren- tice, employed at Messrs T. and T. Vicars' works. Enquiry elicited the fact that the lad who was the son of Mr. John Linaker of Farnworth-street,Earles- town, also employed at Vicars' Works, was, during the temporary absence of the foreman, larking with another apprentice of about the same age, named Thomas Lord. They were throwing at each other a piece of wood about lkin. square,and unfortunately when Linaker was stooping to avoid being hit with the wood, it strack him on the temple, causing him to fall unconscious against his machine. Assistance was soon at hand, but as they were unable to re- store him to consciousness, Dr. Valentine was sent for, and he was removed home.—The lad however, died in about a couple of minutes after reaching home. Lord was detained at the Police Station pending enquiries, but was allowed to go home on an undertaking being given that he would be pro- duced for the inquest. SONS OF TEMPERANCE. On Wednesday evening, October Bth, the usual meeting of the Samuel Morley Section of Cadets of Temperance was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, Sister Edith Judson presided. After opening,the election of officers for the quarter was proceeded with. Worthy Archon. Sister Amy Cronin ; Vice Archon, Bro. Alfred Porter ; Guide. Nellie Rushton ; Watchman, Joseph Green ; Secre- tary, Emily Hughes ; Past Worthy Archon, Sister Edith Judson. After which a spelling Bee for girls took place. Seven competed and after a very exciting contest, Jane Pickett was awarded the winner. Next meeting a contest for boys will be held at 8 o'clock. The Britain's Need Division met in the same room. Bro. S.H. Eaton presided. The Finan- cial Scribe Bro. Pearson gave the quarterly report. The finances were in a very good condition. The sickness of the members was much lower than any previous quarter. The auditors gave their report, and stated the books were satisfactorily kept and everything was in a sound and flourishing state. A conversation ensued in which several members took part on various things connected with the society and a very enjoyable meeting closed at 9.40.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EA.RLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EA.RLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT A few weeks ago,in this columni,l mentioned that some disagreement in churlh matters was in course of progress at flaydock, and from inquiries I have since made it does not appear that any amicable settlement has been arrived at. As I then stated, the matter in dispute was one of ritual, one section declaiming for" high "and the other for " low." It now seems that they have decided to draw up peti- tions,one in favour of a high church and another for a low church ritual, and I understand they are being fairly well eigned, by both factions. Tlow it , will end I cannot say,but I think it a pity to dis- turb the existing form of service which has been in operation at St. James' Church for years.</p>
        <p>The damaging of street lamps in this district has reached such a pitch that the District Council have decided, if possible, to at once put a stop to it by discovering the offenders,and punishing them. The damage is cause ' by lads, and though it is a well- known fact that a lad to keep himself in good health and spirits must be up to some mischief or other, still when his little diversion takes the form of wilfully damaging other people's property, he must be shown the error of his ways. Of course it is. rough on the parents; and although they can- not legally he held responsible,still no parent. likes to see his lad get into trouble if a little sacrifice on his part can prevent it. One or two of the delin- quents have had to put, in an topearance before the Council, and perhaps this may have a beneficial effect.</p>
        <p>The lighting of portions of the town with the in- candescent lamps was inaugurated on Friday night. and they were plainly a 'great improvement. Of course all the lamps are not yet fitted, but perhaps this will come later on. I must again call the atten- tion of the Council to the llaydock crossing, which is without doubt a moat dangerous place. I think the Council could not do better than extend their operation to this place, and turn one of the dark- est spots into a place or light. Mrs. Hughes, wife of a journeyman butcher, cut the throat of her child on Wednesday morning, at Westend Green, Wegt Hampstead. and then :nearly decapitated herself ,death ensuing in both cases before tbe arrival of medical aid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>TEE EXAAIIINTER SAT L RDA Y. OCTOBER 10, 1396</head>
        <p>TEE EXAAIIINTER SAT L RDA Y. OCTOBER 10, 1396</p>
        <p>gallons.—The Gas Committee resolved that the price of coke at the Gas Works be reduced as fellows :-Ist quality to 53. per ten ; 2nd quality to 43. per ton, and that the Clerk invite, by letter, quotations from any coke merchants or agents who would be likely to make an offer for the stock of coke on hand, or any part of it.</p>
        <p>D&amp;MAGE TO STREET LAMPS.-WARNING FRO3I THE</p>
        <p>CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>The question of the fixing of street lamps in Wargrave ward was again brought before the Gas Committee, and it was resolved that the lamps be fixed as originally re- ported upon in March, 1895,with the addition of a lamp near to the foot bridge in Wargrave Old Road, and that the work be proceeded with immediately.—ln moving the confirmation of this committee's minutes, the Chair- man said he was to point out that they had had a meet- ing of that committee prior to the meeting of the Coun- cil with respect to the great damage the Council had recently sustained by the number of lamps which had been destroyed in various places in the district, more especially in Park-road. The lamps which had been damaged there were new ones,and it was very annoying to the Council, and a great less to the district in having iproperty destroyed in the manner it had been. Two !parents of boys who had been reported for doing damage had been before the committee that afternoon, and as it was the first of the kind brought before them, they had looked over it this time, but the committee wished him (the Chairman) to state clearly and distinctly that if the damage continued to be done they would use the ut- most rigour of the law to put a stop to it.—Mr. Ormond seconded the adoption of the minutes, which were ap- proved of.</p>
        <p>NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>A serious accident happened to a petroleum motor-car a day or two ago in the environs of Boulogne. The automobile was (says the " Ties" correspondent) travelling down a steep hill at great speed when the driver, to check the pace,put on the brake too abruptly, with the result that the carriage was nearly upset and the two occupants were thrown into the ditch. They were picked up immediately after the accident in an unconscious state, and were found to be severely hurt.</p>
        <p>When foreign architects visit Japan and see the cath- edral of Baddhism the first time, they are generally antonhhed at the magnificent structure. It is executed in pure Oriental style, and is richly ornamented with 1 carvings. IL loto, a famous builder, of Nagoya City, designed it. The structure was commerced in 1878,and was completed last year. The cost has been estimated at £3,500,000. It would have greatly exceeded this amount had not numbers of Buddhists worked without any recompense. As the structure neared completion, the committee having the work in charge was much per- plexed as to fire insurance. They found that no com- pany would assume the risk on such a valuable wooden structure, the danger of destruction by fire being very great, and thus the premium would amount to an enor- mous sum of money. At last the committee decided on a design devised by Dr. Tanabe. Numbers of powerful fountains were constructed, both exterior and interior, which can be made to play on all parts of the structure at the same time. Usually only one great ornamental fountain is playing,rising to the great height of 157 feet. This is probably the largest artificial fountain in exist- ence, emitting 82,080 gallons per hour.</p>
        <p>Another inventor has,it is said, patented an air-moter bicycle. The necessary compressed air to run the machine is to be secured by air pumps instead of pedals. The entire frame of the wheel serves as the air chamber, and the seat is placed on a piston, which is supported by the air, and thus becomes pneumatic. The inventor claims that greater speed can be obtained with less ex- ertion than on another machine,a claim that will not be accepted withent the proof of actual experiments.</p>
        <p>Dr. Michael has been looking up the subject, and has reported that Lord Bacon died of pneumonia, aged 65. Ben Johnson, anoplexy. Benjamin Franklin, abscess of the lung,aged 84. Washington ,acute laryngitis,aged 87. Edward Gibbon,hydrocele,aged 57. Napoleon,cancer of the stontach. Thomas Gray, gout, aged 54. Burns, rheumatic, aged 37. Byron, heart disease, aged 36. Martin Luther,gastritis. Cromwell, intermittent fever. Sir Walter Scott, apoplexy. Shelley, drowned. Keats, consumption. John Milton, gout, aged 85. Sir Isaac Newton ,stone in the bladder. Dr. Michael says Voltaire died of" strangury," probably due to enlarged prostate. Very much has been said,says Dr. Michael, by ecclesias- tics, about the agony of his last days,as though it was a judgement for his outspoken agnosticism. In the days of 1778, when the condition received no treatment worthy of the name, what physicians would doubt that the days of Voltaire.who died when he was 84 years old, must of necessity have been agonising?</p>
        <p>The death of a lady at Lewisham, apparently in great poverty, has been followed by a remarkable discovery of money in a four-post bedstead. The lady, whose name is said to have been Bonsor or Bonfeld, was an elderly spinster, who lived in a quiet by-road off Lewis- ham High-st., and occupied a small house by herself. She was missed by her neighbours about a month ago, and eventually a policeman entered the house, and found the old lady lying on the floor dead. Further search re- vealed the fact that there was no food of any kind in the house, and but little furniture. Inquiry of a next door neighbour, however, elicited the information that she had led the life of a recluse, and was generally thought to be in needy circumstances. Further, it was stated that the old lady was somewhat eccentric, and, amongst other things, kept a number of blue Persian , cats, which she trained not to touch a number of wild rats which she attracted to the house by means of food. The relatives are said to be very well-to-do people. The unfortunate lady was buried in Lewisham Cemetery, and there the -natter seemed likely to end. Last week, however, her property was sold by auction, by order of a nephew, who became entitled to it under a will found during the search. Amongst the property were an old piano, a beautifully carved music stool, and an old- fashioned " four-post " bedstead, on which the old lady usually slept. The piano was sold to a London dealer, the music-stool, for a comparatively small sum, became the property of a local doctor, and the bed was bought for twenty-five shillings by a carpenter and joiner. On getting the stool home the doctor found it required re- pairing, and accordingly sent to the carpenter with instructions to have it re-covered with leather. In removing the old covering the carpenter found ten sovereiens embedded in the horsehair stuffing, and, tak- ing them to the doctor, was given one for his honesty. His discovery led the man to pull his " four-poster " to nieces, in the hopes that he might make a similar find. This he proceeded to do, and in the course of removing the legs or posts he found secreted in the hollow cavi- tives of three of them no less than £9OO in gold and bank notes. The man went to a public-house on Friday evening and there told his good fortune. The news quickly reached the ears of the dead lady's relatives, and it is said that they will bring an action against him te recover the property.</p>
        <p>One of the lat fads of fashionable Paris is called the "phosphorescent five o'clock." Candles or lamps are not used for illumination. The walls and ceiling as well as the floor of the room and the furniture are all covered with a phosphorescent mixture which absorbs light dur- ing the day and becomes luminous at dusk. Even the flowers with which the room is decorated, and the pic- tures which hang on the wall, and the cups and saucers and plates used at these entertainments, are subjected to a phosphorescent treatment, so that everything is bathed in a mystic glow. Not to be out of harmony with their surroundings, the ladies bidden to these func- tions wear 'phosphorescent gowns," which gleam with a strange unearthly glow. In short, the only things that remain unillumined appear to be the minds of the wearers.</p>
        <p>The mineral statistics show that there is no abate- ment in the pace at which we are rooting out our mine- ral wealth. The Blue Book just issued only carries us up to the end of 1895. As compared with the preceding year, there was an increase in the quantity of coal and iron extracted. The coal raised to the surface in 1894 amounted to 188,277,525 tons. In 1895 it stood at 1139,661,362 tons. Iron ore jumped up by 250,000 tons, and slates and oil shale showed an improvement. The quantity of lead fell away by a couple of thousand tone, whilst tin ore was represented by a paltry 10,000 tons. Silver and gold are not extracted in sufficient quantities to affect the battle of the standards. The smelted silver did not amount to eight tons, whilst the gold came to between three and four Ivandredweights. Salt showed a falling off of 82,000 tons.</p>
        <p>The Northampton, seaftoing training ship fRr boys on tile home station, has returned to Devonport after a I cruise around the English, Scotch, and Irish coasts, and will remain there until the 14th inst., when she will leave for Qllfierl9toWlll. During the cruise which has now terminated she has entered a large number of lads for the navy, althou i g,h n the north recruiting was not very successful. While she was at Portstmuth,however, much more satisfactory results were obtained, largt numbers of boys going on board. !almost daily for medi- cal examination with a view to joining the navy. Since the Northampton was commissioned at Chatham for this °articular service on the 14th of June, 1894, nearly 3,000 boys have presented .themselves, and of this num- ber 1,800 have actually pined the ship, and 1,400 of these have been drafted to the .ierets and to various ships. The cruiser Curacla, which was commissioned some months since as tender to the Northampton for similar service,also shows an excellent record. She has recruited 150 boys, and is now full up, so that there seems no lack of material for the navy in years to come.</p>
        <p>In the summer of 1894 the Empress of Russia as Prin- cess Mix spent a month at Harrogate, lodging with Mrs. Allen, of Cathcart House. Soon after her arrival, Mrs. Allen presented her husband with twins, to whom the Princess graciously stood godmother. Ever since she has taken a great interest in the family, and sent Presents occasionally to them. On Monday. the Empress's lady-in-waiting sent a letter, stating that the Empress had desired her to send her godchildren two enamelled mugs, and expressed a hope that the children were doing well. The presents duly arrived, and were found to he a pair of costly drinking vessels, richly enamelled on gold in torquoise blue and scarlet, to match in every respect a dinner service presented some time since by the Empress. Each mug was enclosed in an oak box, lined with crimson velvet.</p>
        <p>M. Desprez, to whom the Tsar and his fortunes were entrusted on Tuesday during his voyage from Ports- mouth to Cherbourg, has been knocking about in the Channel for some forty years, until by courage, know- ledge, and steadiness he has risen to the position of " pilot-major" of the great military port. This is by no means the first occasion en which a like responsibility has fallen on his broad shoulders, for it has been his proud duty to conduct our own Queen more than once across the silver streak, and the pin in his cravat with the letters V. H. set in brilliants testifies to her Majesty's grateful recognition of his services. Re has other deco- rations that iecord more valiant deeds—the gold and silver medals, for example, won by the rescue in 1877 of the crew of the British sloop Adela ; the Montyon prize for bringing seventeen men off the big Dutch vessel Marie Antoinette in 1886; and a third memento of a plunge into the docks to save the life of a child. Desprez has the stalwart figure, the broad vigorous face, the blue eyes, and the close-cropped fiaxed hair and whiskers of the Norman sailor.</p>
        <p>The movetnent to raise some tangible memorial of the invention and practical establishment of submarine tele- graphy took definite shape on Tuesday, when an influ- ential meeting was held in London. under the chair- manship of the Earl of Selborne. The death recently of the three great pioneers of the submarine telegraghic system has made the present an appropriate moment for</p>
        <p>the erection of some permanent memorial. The Marquis of Tweeddale, who has taken a leading part in initiating the movement,has secured the adhesion of the Ministers and engineers at the head of the telezraph systems of nearly every country, for nothing less than an inter- national memorial would suffice to celebrate the birth of ' that which is primarily an international system. On Wednesday a large Executive Committee was appointed. A number ot subscriptions had already been promised, but the definite decision as to the form of the memorial was left for the committee to discuss. It was agreed to ' identify it with the names of Mr. Cyrus Field, who con- ceived the idea of great submarine cables ; of Sir John Fender, who successfully laid them down ; and of Sir James Anderson, who, as captain of the Great Eastern, proved the possibility of performing the equally neces- sary feat of picking them up again for repairs—a feat which was long believed to be as impossible as it was obviously essential for any practical development of the system. Lord Kelvin and other well-known electricians are co-operating, and it is hoped to make the memorial a feature of the celebrations in honour of the Queen's long reign next May.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held on Monday after- noon in the Town Hall,Earlestown. Mr. C. B. F. Borron J.P., C.C., occupied the chair, and there were also pre- sent :—Dr. Patterson, Rev. E. W. W. Kaye, Messrs. W. Collingwood,J. W.Emmett,P. McMahon,W. Gilmour, It. Stono, J.P.. J. Simpson, W. Thompson, J. Slee, E. 'T. Evans,T. Ormand, W. H. Worsley (law clerk), Mr. R. Brierley (surveyor), and Mr. W. Shirley (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>SCHOOL ATTENDANCEL-A. CENSUS TO BE TAE.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCEL-A. CENSUS TO BE TAE.</p>
        <p>The report of the sub-committee appointed to con- sider the advisability of a census being taken of children of school age within the district was read, viz. :—" Sep- tember 21st, 1898 :—The subcommittee are unanimous in recommending that a census should be taken. The necessary arrangements have been made which will be proceeded with immediately, and completed on October 17th, 1896,if approvei by the General Committee. The sub-committee also recommend that the person taking the census should be renumerated at the rate of I±d. per house. (Signed) F. J. Wood, chairman sub-commit- tee."—The report was adopted on the motion of Mr. Collingwood,seconded by Mr. Gilmour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>TRCHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>TRCHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes of this committee stated that a letter, dated August 21st., 1896, was read from the direetor of Technical Instruction for the County Palatine of Lancas- ter, stating that subject to the conditions contained in the " Particulars respecting grants in aid of technical instruction to urban and rural districts," the County Technical Instruction Committee were prepared to recommend a Grant of a sum not exceeding £153 to this district, in aid of Technical Instruction for the year 1896 and 1897. The committee resolved that the letter be re- ferred to the Viaduct Institute Technical Instruction Committee, and that the carrying out of technical in- stroction, within the district for the session 1896 and 1897, be delegated to a committee consisting of four members of the council, viz., Mr. Borron, J.P., Mr. Wood, Mr. Thompson, and Rey. E. W. W.Whittenbury- Kaye, and five members ef the Viaduct Institute, viz., Mr. Emmett, J.P. Mr. Connor, Mr. Crawford, Mr. Davies, and Mr. d. W. Emmett; and that a copy of this resolution be forwarded to the Director of Techaical In- struction for the County Palatine of Lancaster.—The minutes were approved on the motion of the Rev. E. W. W. Kaye, seconded by Mr. Simpson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>SANITARY INSPECTOR'S MONTHLY REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE SANITARY INSPECTOR'S MONTHLY REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brierley reported :—" We have had rather a large number of scarlet fever cases during the last few weeks but lam glad to say it is of a very mild form. Most of them have been taken to the hospital, and those rema; n- ing at home are properly isolated. The toll for street hawking during the five weeks is £4 17s. 3d. Market tells and stallage £32 9a. 101.—Total £3775. Id. Against £26 14s. 7d. last year."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>TOLLS ON NEW TON COMMON.</head>
        <p>TOLLS ON NEW TON COMMON.</p>
        <p>The Clerk, a; instructed at the last meeting of the Highways, Public Works, and Sanitary Committee, re- ported with reference to collection of tolls on Newton Common. Lie stated that he had been given to under- stand that about June, 1895, the Law Clerk had taken Counsel's opinion upon the matter.—The Clerk was directed to obtain the opinion referred to, and submit it to the next meeting of the Committee. STREET HAWKING PERMITS,</p>
        <p>Correspondence from other local authorities as to the issuing of licenses or permits, payable in advance, in lieu of taking tolls for street hawking was submitted. After consideration the Highways, &amp;c., Committee re- solved—that, where possible, the toll collector be autho- rised to grant permits in lieu of taking tolls weekly, or otherwise, fot street hawking, such permits to be pay- able in advance. THE TOLL comAicTon AND THE EGG MERCHANT.</p>
        <p>A -letter, dated Ausrust 17th, 1896 was read to the Highways, Sco., Committee from the toll collector, stat- idg that T. Hyland, in the employ of Mr. James Rogers, egg merchant, High-street, Golborne, had refused to pay toll for street hawking when demanded on August 17ch,and that he had also obstructed him when attempt- ing to seize goods to the value of the toll demanded, whereupon it was resolved—that the Law Clerk be in- structed to take legal proceedings against James Rogers, of High-street, G3lborne, for recovery of the amount of toll demanded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>DAMAGED WALL.</head>
        <p>A DAMAGED WALL.</p>
        <p>Letters were read to the Highways. &amp;., Committee from Messrs. Walton and Co., Glass Works, Newton-le- Willows, complaining of damage to a wall,at the rear of 150, Lec,h-terrace, Crow-lane, Newton-le-Willows, which they stated, had been caused through the negligence of the Council's workmen on the m3rning of the 21st August, and asking the Council to repair the wall.—The Clerk stated that he had replied to Messrs. Walton that " from information supplied to me, I submit that the Council are not liable, as the damage was caused through no nPgligenee on the part our mien." The Clerk also stated that he understood the wall in question had since been repaired by Messrs. Walton. THE MEDICAL OFFICER'S APPOINTMENT.</p>
        <p>A letter was read to the Highways, Pubtic Works, and Sanitary Works Committee from the Local Government Board, acknowledging receipt of the Clerk's letter of the 13th August, notifying them of the re-appointment of Dr. J. W, Watkins as medical officer of health for the district of Ilowton-in-Makerfield for one year, from the 29th day of September, Me, s.tating tlioit it apnenrs to the Board that in futgre where a medical ocer AT heaith discharges his duty satisfactorily, it may be a question for the consideration of the Council whether it may not be desirable, en the occasion of the renewal of his appointment, to appoint him for a term of years, if not permanently, THE REPAIR Or A ro.)6TISATi. A. letter w-s react to the Highways Committee from Messrs. R Evans and Co ,Limited,in roply to the Clerk's letter of the Bth of September, calling their attention to the unsatisfactory state of the footpath in Railway-st., Earlestown (West Side), stating, that the footpath in lquestion is repairable by the Council.—The matter was referred to the Sub-Committoe on Private Streets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>NEWTON FAIR.</head>
        <p>NEWTON FAIR.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor sislomit—tCl-;'.pla—n--o-i proposed extensions of ropes and posts during fair time in High-st., Newton. After consideration it was resolved by the Highways Committee—that they be extended from the S.E. side of the Blue Bell Hotel, High-street, and carried across the rod leading to Newton Mere, leaving an opening of sufficient width for persons on foot to pass through.</p>
        <p>NEW BUILDINGS.</p>
        <p>Building plans were submitted, and the Highways Committee resolved—That the following plans be ap- proved and passed, viz. :—Recraation room at the rear of 50, Flaydock-streed, for Mr. G.W.Slinn, subject to its remmval after six months' notice ; addition to workshop for Messrs. W. Lawson and Co., Warrington-road ; new cycle works for Mr. Francis Holt, proposed to be erected near Railway Bride, War,raye-road ; new stable and coachouse for Dr. Valentine, Flavdock-street, and four houses in proposed new street off Wargrave New-road, for Mr. William Beatty. 1 A BUILDER'S MISTAKE.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Harrison, of Haydock, wrote to the Highway, Committee, stating that he had discovered that the height of the bedroom windows in the seven houses and one shop, which he had just built, in St. Heleng-road, were through an oversight only 7 feet, instead of 7 feet 6 inches as specified in the Council's Bye-laws, and offer- ing to fix a self-acting ventilator in each of thebedrnoms, if the Council would grant him the necessary certificate for habitation. The committee resolved that a certific- ate for habitation be granted to Mr. Harrison on the condition that ha pay a fine of £l, and fix in bedrooms a self-acting ventilator as suggested. Tile plans to be adjusted accordingly. THE PAV IN G OF STREETS.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor reported that Mr. Smith of Grapoenhall,</p>
        <p>owner of eight cottages in 'faun worth-street, Eartestown, had requested him tofla and korb in front of his pro- _</p>
        <p>_ - perty, and to charge him with the e.-.st on completion ; whereupon it was resolved by the Highway Committee that the Surveyor be authorised to proneed with the work on the conditions named.—The Surveyor also reported that Mr. S. Hutton. contractor, Bowdon, to whom the contract for the making of four private streets within the district had been given at the last meeting of the committee, subject to satisfactory refereneee,was, in his opinion, after due enquiries, a suitable person to undertake the work ; but that he (Mr. Hutton) had called upon him and had r-qeosted to be allowed ti, withdraw his tender of £1,686 103. 101. as he had dis- covered as error in his calculation of £l6O 164. 11d.— After consideration br the committee it was resolved that Mr. Hatton he allewed to amend his tender of £1,636 104. 10d. to £1,847 7s. 91. for the making of Mercer, Walmaler. Birley and Stanley-streets. as per resolution of the 7th September. 1896.—The Surveyor further reported to the Committee that Mr. J. J. Lee, of Heath-street, Mancheater, had withdrawn his quotation for the supply of flaps to the Council, owing to advanced prices ; whereupon it was resolved that the next lowest offer be accented, if sat-n{3'J) supplied is suitable.—The minutes of the committee were confirmed on the motion of Mr. CollinE.twood. seconded by Mr. Thompson. GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE. The question of the chairmanship of this committee was considered. It was, however, the unanimous wish of the committee that Mr. Barron should continue to act as chairman until the end of the current financial year. After this expression of opinion, Mr. Marron ex- pressed his willingness to act as chairm an as suggested. GAS MANAGER'S REPORT. Mr. Joseph Swann, gas manager, in his report to the chairman of the Gas and Water Committee stated that there was an extra increase in the make of gas over this time last year, as in the three week. ending September 21st last year, the make of vaa was 1.724,930 feet, this Tear in the same time it was 2,320,300 feet an increase of 595,400 feet—nearly 26 per cent. The only Causes he could give for this extra increase were the additional street lamps, the dark weather and the automatie meters. The make of coke increased of course according to the make of gas. They had a large stoek to start with, and he thought that during the next five months they would produce an average of 200 tons of coke per month,whieh, after deducting for their own use, left them about 130 tons for sale Per month. This must be sold as they could not possihly stock it, nearly all available space beiug occupied with coke left over from last winter. The ccn- qh‘rntien of water for last mnnth, was :—Newton Bridpe, 3,160,000 ~allon4 ; Viadilet, 1,930,000 gallons ; Park Side, 10,000 gallons:total. 5.150,000 gallons ; corresntind. month last year, 5,167,000 gallons ; decrease, 17,000</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>FINANCE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The matter of Mr. Brierley's agreement, as engineer, surveyor,and inspector of nuisances to the Council,for a term of five yeamas from April Ist, 1896, was brought forward by the Chairman of the above mentioned com- mittee, who stated that Mr. Brierley took exception to certain clauses,whereupon it was unanimovsly agreed to call him into the room to give him an opportunity of stating to the committee the reasons of his objection. After further consideration the committee resolved that they did not see their way to modify the agreement al- ready drawn up, that Mr. Brierley failing to sign the agreement on or before the 28th instant,the chairman of the Finance Committee be authorise I to give notice to labroaate the whole of the resolutions bearing upon Mr. IBrierley's appointment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>TRH INSPECTOR OF NUISANCP.S.</head>
        <p>TRH INSPECTOR OF NUISANCP.S.</p>
        <p>A letter was read to the Finance Committee from the Lncal Government Board, acknowledging receipt of the Clerk's letter of the 7th July, with reference to an ap- pointment of an Inspector of Nuisances for this district, requesting the Council to be good enough to reconsider their proposal with a view tn the appointment of an In- spector of Nuisances who would devote the whole of his time to the duties of the office.—Mr. Ormond movecl,Mr. Stone seconded,and it was agreed to,that the minutes be confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE,</head>
        <p>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE,</p>
        <p>This Committee's minutes stated that a circular letter from the Local &amp;were nent Board was submitted, stat- ing, that if the Council intended to apply for a Pro- visional Order under the Public Health Act, 1875, such application should be made not later than the 30th day of November next.—To affix the common seal of the Council to the following agreements viz. :—Between the Council and Messrs. Hardman and Holden, of Miles Platting,. Manchester, for the delivery of the surplus tar produced at the Gas Works during the year ending August 31+t, 1897 —Approximate quantity 280 tons. Consideration £1 12s. 3d. per ton; between Mr. S. Hut- ton, of Bowdon, and the Council f -)r the making of Mercer, Walmsley. Birley, and Stanley Streets, for the sum of £1,847 7s. 91. ; between the Council and Mr. R. Brierley, C.E., as to his engagement as engineer, sur- veyor, and inspector of nuisances, for a term of five years from April Ist, 1896, at a salary of £4OO per annum.—The minutes were confirmed on the motion of the Chairman.</p>
        <p>A GAME LICENSE. A letter was read from Mr. John Miller applying for a license to buy and sell game at his shop in Bridge-st., Earlestown.—A license was granted on the motion of Mr. Gilmour, seconded by Mr. Ormand. This concluded the husinpss.</p>
        <p>CO-OPERATIVE CONFERENCE AT EARLESTOWN. The Bolten District Association of the Co-operative Union held their quarterly conference at the Co-opera- tive Hall, Earle ;town, on Saturday afternoon. The ells- trict embraces 33 societies.and 70 representatives includ- ing six ladies, were present. Amengst those present were Messrs. Crooks (Executive Secretary), Horrocks (13e1t0n) chairman. John Smith (Leigh), Swanbrick (Chorley), Barlow (Farnwerth), Martin (Walkden), Rat- cliffe (Pilkington Society), Connor (educational repro- eentative), and J. G. Mullins (Eerlestown). Mr. D. Forrester, president of the Earlestown Society, occupied the chair. Whilst the delegates were assembling Mr T. F. Roberts played a selection of music on the piano. The Chairman,in opening the proseedings,said that he had great pleasure on behalf of the members of the Edrlestown Society.in giving them all a hearty welcome to the Earlestown Society. It was jest three and a half years since the Conference met there before, and during the interval ho was not aware of anything having been !rer(lo in connection with the movement in that local- ity of a striking or reenarkable character, nor had the progress of that Society been As great ae some of them could wish. They might perhaps be disposed to blame the leaders of Co-operation there for neglecting to arouse enthusiasm, and so conduct their business as to rnerit the respect and patronage of a larger section of the sur- rounding inhabitants, and he was afraid he must admit there was some truth in that. At the same time, although Earlestown was not a large plAce, there were few towns where the sharp ele- ments of competition in trading were more keen. This rendered it most essential that they should exercise their intelli ence the more, in order to make satisfactory co- ops.ative progress. In inviting the conference to Earles- town, they were conscions of being at considerable dis- advantage compared with some towns within the Belton district, by way of local attractions and objects of inter- est. Not that this part of Lancashire was barren, his- torically, or devoid of natural beauty, as both subjects might be met with more or less prominently within an hour's walk. The season however was too far advanced to take them far afield. The committee had therefore made arrangements for them to visit the place of greatest resort is the town, viz , the Railway Company's Inati- tute and grounds. the Institute officials having kindly granted permission for all delegated; who chose, to walk through the grounds and building free of charge. (Hear, heir.) Whatever might be absent in their immediate midst, in the way of romantic scenery, or elegant works of skill and art, there was nothing in the world to pre- vent them from fulfilling the main object of the confer- ence, which was to further the Co-operative cause both socially and intellectually, by friendly and good debate, feeling confident it would prove to be of mutual advan- tege to them all. (Applause.) Mr. J. G. Mulling then read an interesting paper on "Stores MAnaeement." The Chief interest in the paper centred round the part which had reference to the hold- ing of the dual position of secretary and manager by one person At the conclusion of the paper, The Chairman said that altheneh the paper was not an orieinal one, the subject happened to be of peculiar interest to the Earlestown Society, at the present time owing to its being in a state of tranaitioe with regard to matters of meneeement,and he hoped that the delegates would express their opinions upon the suhjeet, and ren- der the society all the assistance they could towards nlae.ine it on a sound basis so far as the management was concerned. A delegate asked what was meant hy being in a state of transition, was the society in an difcultv ? The Chairman said that far a number of years past, the posts of manager and secretary had been held by One man, and they had decided to divide the pesiti ins. The debate was opened berlfr. Bland, of Hudrierefield, who dwelt chiefly upon the wisdom of keening the divi- dend withirt reasonable limits, not to be striving to pay large dividends. hut rather to use some of the profits for the education of the members so that they might know what co-operation really was. A number of &amp;tante,* took part in the debate but all agreed in condemning the system of management in which one man held the dual position which gave him too much power, and removed the democratic form of government from a cooperative society. The delegates were afterwards entertained to tea, and the Conference closed with the customary votes of thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>SHOCKING DE aft CrF A REFoItIIA.TOR.Y</head>
        <p>SHOCKING DE aft CrF A REFoItIIA.TOR.Y</p>
        <p>_ *BOY AT NEWTON. On Therslay morninv,a lad named William John Bailey. 15 years or age, an inmate of the Liverpool Farm School Reformatory, Newton. met with a shocking death at the printing works of Messrs. Meorquodale and Co., where he was employed as an assistant, at one of the machines, a No. 10 per- fecter machine in charge of James Williamson, of Golhorne-road, Newton. The deceased in company with William Wright, another reformatory boy,who was also an assistant on the same machine, was standing hv when Williamson started it to ink the rollers. He stopped it again,and deceased,who was about to go under it into what is known as the pit, which is level with the ground floor to grease the rack or carriage, was told by Williamson not to do so as it was oiled the night previous. Light, the other hov, then went to the other side of the mac- hine, and. Williamson leaving deceased still stand- ing there went to start the machine again,shouting "clear" before doing so. He then heard a thud, and thinking the grease-can which is under the machine had got into the wheels, stopped the mac- hine at once and went to see what was wrong, and found deceased lying in the pit quite dead,with the top of his head crushed in. He got assistance and removed the body. Nobody saw the deceased go under the machine, and it cannot be said whether he went under it whilst it was in motion or not. PC. Williamson had the body removed to the Reformatory, and the Coroner has been notified.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE couNry ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The season of the Lancashire and Cheshire County Association was brought to a termination on Saturday by a match between the officiala and members of the Council, for which valuable awards were offered by the vice-presidents, Messrs. Coombs (Withington), Arthur Crosfield (Warrington), S.Kelsall (Stretford), and Joseph Isherwood (Leigh). Although the afternoon was not nearly so favourable as could be desired, the attendance throughout the competition was unusually large. There were 32 entrants, the winners in the first round being Messrs. J. T. Meakin (Kersal), E. Fairelough and J. Singleton (Newton-le-Willows), G. Walton (Timperley), Shackleton (Broughton), A. Rogers (Pendleton), J. Reeves (Harpurhey). A. Thompson (Urmston), T. Marshall (Hindsford), T. Locker (South Warring- ton), J. Turnbull (Radcliffe), H. Richardson (Salford Regent), F. Butterfield (Kersal Moor), R. Rothwell (Croft's Bank). J. K. Shaw (Caledonians). and J. War- burton (Tylhesley). These were reduced to eight in the third round, the results being :—H. Richardson (15) v. G. Walton (8), J. Turnbull (15) v. T. Locker (6),J. War- burton (15) v. A. Thompson (8), E. Fairclough (15) v. J. Singleton (9). The semi-finals and finals resulted in the awards being taken in the following order: 1, E. Fairclough ;2, J. Warburton ;3, H. -Rizhards-m ; 4, J. Turnbull. An extra prize was won by J. Singleton. The following is a list of the prizes taken, a portion of which only were distributed, the remainder being left over un- til the half-yearly meeting : Club championship, count‘ cup presented by President, Lower Broughton, having distanced Old Trafford, Chorlton Green, Newton-le-Wit- ' lows, South Warrington, and Withington. 880 points and two walks over; runners-up, senior, vice-president's prize, Withington having distanced Salford, Hinds- ford, llrmston, and Leigh, 1,129 points and w.o. Senior Association award, South Warring- ton,having defeated Penileton,Radeliffe,N:,rth Warring- ton twica,and Leigh, 1,328 points and a w.o. ; runners- op award, Leigh, having distanced Levenshulme, Douglas, Hazel Grove, and Wigan,9B7 points and 2 w.o. Junior Association award, Harpurhey having defeated Prestwich, Whalley and Croft's Bank, 904 points and a w.o. ; runners-up prize, Prestwich having distanced Cheetham Woodlands and Heaton Mersey,62o points and 2 w.o. Gold medallist, Robert Williamson, heaviest ag- gregate majority. Lancashire v. Yorkshire. Amateur championship prizes: 1, J. W. G. Coombs,Midland ;2, Walter Wells,Heaton Mersey ; 3; D. Bolton, North War- rington ; 4, C. Towers,Harpurhey ; 5,F. Shaw,Tyldesley; 6, W. Shaw, South Warrington ; 7, W. Wilson, Caledon- ians ; B,W. Fielding, Lower Broughton. Council prizes, half team tournament : 1, Radcliffe; 2, Old Trafford ; 3, Hazel Grove.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>AN INTERESTING MEMENTO.</head>
        <p>AN INTERESTING MEMENTO.</p>
        <p>Attached to the Jackson-Harmsworth Artie Ex- pedition was a young chemist—Mr. Child—who went out as mineralogist and photographer to the party. He has had enough of Arctic snows for the present and has just lately returned to London, where, as a matter of course, he has had to relate some of his experiences to the übiquitous inter- viewer. His professional services were not in very great request,but he evidently had a very good time, notwithstanding,in the con fortable quarters of the expedition established at Cape Flora. The most ex- citing day, the recollection of which will be for ever " photographically lined on the tablets of his mind" was June 17th when Dr. Nansen was dis- covered by the party. They were all expecting the Windward and about 9 p.m.on that day,one of their number went out after dinner,with his glass to spy Shortly afterwards he rushed in greatly excited Are we all here' said he. They counted noses, Yes they were all there, " Well then, there's a man on the ice." A general stampede for lowed; everybody wanted to be first to greet the stranger, a shipwrecked walrus hunter they supposed. Mr. Jackson was the first to reach him and when it was revealed to all that he was none other than the famous Dr. Nansen three I hearty British cheers rent the sky. His corn panoin, Lieutenant Jollonnsen. who had been some way behind, was not long in joining him. But what a spectacle they presented. Dr. Nansen in his normal state has bright flaxen hair and ruddy cheeks,but these two hardy Norsemen were as black as" naygurs," and dressed in garments fearful and wonderful. They had lived on seal, bear and whale for a year and the smoke of their blubber fuel had formed a thick black layer all over them, for they hadn't washed for 14 months; they couldn't, they had no soap, and fuel was too precious to waste for melting the anew. They had hot baths that night, we are not told how many, but after the first Mr. Child was fortunate enough to secure a lock of Dr. Nansen's hair.. A part of that identical lock is in the possession of Mr. 11. M. Ashton, chemist, War- rington, and may be seen in his window, mounted as a slile. It shows distinctly what little effect one bath can have on a man's hair if he can't wash it for 14 months; especially if he has been exposed to smoke of the kind described.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>CONCERT.—A tea party and concert was held in con- nection with the Young Men's Institute in the Church Huse, Parlgate, on Friday evening last week. Tea was partaken of by about fifty persons, after which an excel- lent concert was given, the Rev. J. J. Langham, presi- dent of the Institute, presiding. The programme which was of a varied character included songs by Messra. W. Price, H. Mines, E. Lovering, J. Unwin and J. Corn- f,rth ; violin solos by Mr. J. Roberts ; and comic songs and sketches which were well appreciated were given by Mr. Barlow. The concert proved a success and was greatly enjoyed by everyone present. THE RAILWAY FATALITY.—INQUEST. ' An inquest was held at the Stocks Inn, Padgate, on Saturday morning by Mr. S. Brighouse, on the body of Richard Stanway, who was knocked down and killed by an express train on the Cheshire Lines Railway at Pad- gate, on Thursday morning, the first instant.—Mrs. Stanway deceased's wife identified the body as that of her husband. She said that he left home on Saturday morning about half-past eight to go to work ; he was then in good health and witness had not seen him alive since.—Mr. F. Simpson, representing the Railway Com- pany. said deceased had been in their employ since he was fourteen years of age.—Stephen Ellis, ri, a telegraph line.man in the employ of the Cheshire Lines Committee, stated that (xi the morning of the first instant he and deceased went to Longharn Bridge, Padvate, to repair the telegraph wires. The work being dome they started to walk towards Warrington along the dawn main line, and would have their hacks to an approaching train. Shortly afterwards Ellison heard a thud and saw the express train dash past and turning round found Stan- way lying dead near the rails with is, quantity of blood flawing from the side of his head. Witness was walking four or five yards from the rails,and he thought deceased must have been walking on the sleeper ends. Witness could not hear the express train approaching as there was a ballast train passing on the up line at the time of the accident. He went to Padgato Station and brought the stationmaster and P.C. Heath to where the accident had happened.—P.C. Heath said lie and the station- master went with the last witness and had the body taken to the Stocks Inn, Pacigate.—William Lewis, emolnyed by the M.S.L. Railway Co., as engine driver, said the train would be travelling at the rate of about forty-five miles per hour at the time of the accident. HQ could not see the two men on account of the rain, and the smoke from the missing ballast train. A verdict of " Accidental death " was returned.</p>
        <p>The Czar and Czarina left Portsmouth on Monday morning and arrived at Cherbourg in the afternoon. Immense crowds have flocked into Paris 'to welcome the Imperial visitors, and many thousands were unable to find accommodation for the night. WHAT CAUSES PIMPLES? Clogging of the pores. The little plug in the centre of the pimple is called a blackhead, grub, or comedone. The only real preven- tive is CIITICURA SOAP. the most effective skisl purifying and beautifying soap in the world, as well as the purest and sweetest for toilet, bath, and nursery. It is the only preventive of pimples, blackheads, red, rough, and oily skin. red, rough hands, with shapeless nails, dry, thin, and falling hair, and simple bibv blemishes, because the only preventive of inflammation and clogging of the pores, the cause of minor affections 0r the skin, scali , and hair. Salo greater than the combned sales of all other skin and complexion soaps.</p>
        <p>WARRING EON.</p>
        <p>STRETTON MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A general meeting in connection with --th-e---a-bove society took place in the schoolroom on Monday evening, the chair being occupied by Mr. C. T. Whitley. The Secretary read the minutes of the last meetings, and also the accounts for the ensu- ing session, which showed a balance in hand of over £4. After the election of officers for the coming session and votes of thanks having been passed tG the retiring ones, the meeting terminated. LATCHFORD GOOD TEMPLARS. The weekly session of the B inehill Branch Lodge of the 1.0.G.T. was held on Monday evening in the Temperance Hall, Bro. Councillor Ellison, CT., pre- siding. There was a fair attendance. The reading of the minutes and other business was transacted. The programme consisted of a Biblical address by Bro. J. J. IL Corbett. His text was taken from Joshua vii. 12. "Therefore the children of Israel could not stand before their enemies." An interest- ing discourse was given, and it was appreciated by all:present. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT PERFECT THRIFT BUILDING SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The 23rd ballot of the Warrington and District Perfect Thrift Building Society was held at the, registered office, 71,Buttermarket-street on Monday' night. Mr. A. Horrocks was voted the chairman,. and Messrs. Sambrook and Wilde were elected scru- tineers, and Master Charles Woods, a non-member,. was chosen to draw the balls. 409 was the num- ber drawn, and the holder John Unsworth, of Man-- ley-street, was declared entitled to the free use of £lOO for 16 2-3 years. A vo'.e of thanks to the chairman and scrutineers terminated the meeting.</p>
        <p>Mrs PONSONBY'S_HAIR RKeTcnotzt._ a great discovery, .a</p>
        <p>- Most Refreshing Wash, Marvellous Results, Innumelable4 Testimonials, Patronised by Royalty. In great demand by Professionals, makes the Hair Grow, prev.nts -Bald- ness, Invigorating, restores hair to its naturaleolour. gives healthy and youthful appearance. Nlestastonish- ing results ; 2s 6d Bottle, post free.—il dc:L !ssMts. Fos- BONDY, 25, Lime-Bt.. Liverpool—Advt. 1.0.G.T. TEMPERANCE MISSION LODGE.</p>
        <p>The first monthly entertainment in connection with this lodge was held in the Mission Room,. Academy-street, on Wednesday, when the following members and friends took part :—Mrs. Sheath,song; Miss Tomlin, recitation ; Miss Mason, song ; Ser- geant Price, recitation ; Miss Nurse, song ; Mr. Jones, reading; Mr. Sheath, recitation ; Mrs. Nurse, song ; Master Sheath, autoharp solo ; Mr. O'Brien, song; Miss Sheath, recitation ; Mrs. Sheath, song; Master Nurse, recitation ; Miss Tomlin, recitation ; Quarter-Master Sergeant Roberts, address ; Mr. Sheath, recitation. The meeting was closed by a few remarks by the chairman, Bro. O'Brien. A WEDDING DELAYED.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon,P.C. Berry was on duty itt Manchester-road when he saw a couple of horses attached to a carriage coming towards him at e furicus rate. He put up his arms,but they took no notice of him, and then he rushed at the rein of the near anitual,to which he clung, and was dragged s distance of about, 40 yards. The animals were that brought to a standstill. The driver, almost breath- less,then came upon the scene and he explained that the horses had started from 52,Napier-street. He had jumped off the box before taking a party to the Parish Church for a wedding when the animalg took fright at a passing baker's cart and bolted off before he could get to them. The horses were noV injured,and the carriages were not damaged,but the consequence would probably have been serious bus for the constable's presence of mind and pluck.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
